      "text": "1920 + 81 = 2001?\n\nWait, that seems a bit high? Let me check again. Maybe I made an addition error.\n\nWait, let's do the addition again step by step:\n\nFirst term: 1200\n\nAdding 180: 1200 + 180 = 1380. That's correct.\n\nAdding 540 to 1380: 1380 + 540. Let's break that down: 1380 + 500 = 1880, then +40 gives 1920. That still checks out.\n\nThen adding 81: 1920 + 81. 1920 + 80 is 2000, plus 1 more is 2001. So that's 2001?\n\nHmm, but let me verify this another way to make sure I didn't make a mistake in the initial multiplication.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method. Let's try the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\nMultiply 29 by 69.\n\nFirst, write 29\n\ntimes 69\n\n________\n\nMultiply 29 by 9 (the units digit of 69):\n\n29 * 9: 20*9=180, 9*9=81, so 180+81=261. So that's 261.\n\nThen multiply 29 by 60 (the tens digit part, so shift one position to the left):\n\n29 * 60. Well, 29*6=174, so 29*60=1740.\n\nNow add those two results: 261 + 1740. Let's see:\n\n261 + 1740. 1740 + 200 is 1940, then +61 is 2001. So that's the same answer as before. Okay, so that's consistent.\n\nWait, but I feel like 29*70 would be 2030, right? Because 29*70 is 29*7*10 = 203*10 = 2030. Then subtract 29 to get 29*69, so 2030 -29 = 2001. Hmm that's another way to do it and also gives 2001. Okay so that's three different methods all leading to 2001. So maybe that is correct.\n\nWait, but let me confirm with another approach. Maybe using (30 -1)*(70 -1). Let's see:\n\n(30 -1)(70 -1) = 30*70 -30*1 -1*70 +1*1 = 2100 -30 -70 +1 = 2100 -100 +1? Wait, 30 +70 is 100, so 2100 -100 is 2000, then +1 is 2001. Yep, same answer. So that's four methods all leading to 2001. I think that's correct then. Therefore the product of 29 and 69 is 2001. Wait, but wait, is there a chance I made a mistake in any step?\n\nLet me check the first method again:\n\n20*60 = 1200, correct.\n\n20*9 = 180, correct.\n\n9*60 = 540, correct.\n\n9*9 =81, correct.\n\nAdding them: 1200+180=1380; 1380+540=1920; 1920+81=2001. Correct.\n\nAnother way: 29*69. Let me do straight multiplication:\n\n29\n\nx69\n\nMultiply 9*29: 261\n\nMultiply 60*29: 1740\n\nAdd them: 261+1740 = 2001. Correct.\n\nAlternatively, 29*69:\n\nBreak down 69 as 70-1, so 29*(70-1)=29*70 -29*1 = 2030-29=2001. Correct.\n\nAlternatively, using calculator-like step-by-step:\n\n29*69:\n\nFirst, 9*9=81, write down 1 and carryover 8.\n\n9*2 (from 29) is 18, plus 8 is 26. So first partial product is 261.\n\nThen, moving to the tens place, 6 (from 69) times 9 is 54, write down 4 and carryover 5.\n\n6*2 is 12, plus 5 is 17. So the second partial product is 1740 (since it's shifted one position to the left). Adding 261 + 1740 gives 2001. Yep.\n\nSo I think all methods confirm it's 2001. I was worried maybe I added wrong the first time, but cross-verifying with other methods shows it's consistent. So I think that's the correct answer.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 29 and 69 is calculated as follows:\n\n**Step 1:** Use the distributive property (also known as the FOIL method for binomials):\n\\[\n(20 + 9)(60 + 9) = 20 \\times 60 + 20 \\times 9 + 9 \\times 60 + 9 \\times 9\n\\]\n\n**Step 2:** Compute each term:\n- \\(20 \\times 60 = 1200\\)\n- \\(20 \\times 9 = 180\\)\n- \\(9 \\times 60 = 540\\)\n- \\(9 \\times 9 = 81\\)\n\n**Step 3:** Sum all the terms:\n\\[\n1200 + 180 + 540 + 81 = 2001\n\\]\n\n**Verification with alternative methods:**\n- **Standard multiplication:**\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{r}\n   \\quad 29 \\\\\n  \\times 69 \\\\\n  \\hline\n   261 \\quad (\\text{29 \u00d7 9}) \\\\\n  +1740 \\quad (\\text{29 \u00d7 60, shifted left}) \\\\\n  \\hline\n   2001 \\\\\n  \\end{array}\n  \\]\n- **Difference of squares:**\n  \\[\n  (30 - 1)(70 - 1) = 2100 - 30 - 70 + 1 = 2001\n  \\]\n- **Adjustment from 29\u00d770:**\n  \\[\n  29 \\times 70 = 2030 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad 2030 - 29 = 2001\n  \\]\n\n**Final Answer:**  \nThe product of 29 and 69 is \\boxed{2001}.",
