em for practice, let's see if there's another manual way.\n\nAlternatively, 47 \u00d782 can be thought of as (50 - 3)(80 + 2). Wait, that's the same as above. Anyway, all methods are converging to 3854. So I think that's the correct answer.\n\nWait, just to be thorough, let me do the multiplication step-by-step again.\n\n47 \u00d7 82:\n\nFirst, multiplying 47 by the units digit of 82, which is 2:\n\n47 \u00d7 2 = 94. Write that down as the first partial product.\n\nThen, multiplying 47 by the tens digit (8) but since it's in the tens place, it's actually 80. So 47 \u00d7 8 = 376, then shift one position to the left (because it's the tens place), so 3760.\n\nAdding 94 and 3760:\n\n3760\n\n+\u00a0\u00a094\n\n------\n\n3854\n\nYes, that's correct. So adding those together, 3760 + 90 is 3850, plus 4 is 3854.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps adding 3760 + 94 step by step:\n\n3760 + 94:\n\nStart from 3760, add 90 to get 3850, then add the remaining 4 to get 3854. Yep.\n\nAlright, so after multiple checks, I feel confident that the product of 47 and 82 is indeed 3854. I don't see any errors in my calculations, so I think that's the right answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 47 and 82 is \\boxed{3854}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 47 and 82, we can use the distributive property by breaking down the numbers into smaller parts:\n\n\\[\n(40 + 7)(80 + 2)\n\\]\n\nExpanding this using the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n40 \\times 80 + 40 \\times 2 + 7 \\times 80 + 7 \\times 2\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term separately:\n\n1. \\(40 \\times 80 = 3200\\)\n2. \\(40 \\times 2 = 80\\)\n3. \\(7 \\times 80 = 560\\)\n4. \\(7 \\times 2 = 14\\)\n\nAdding these results together:\n\n\\[\n3200 + 80 + 560 + 14\n\\]\n\nStep-by-step addition:\n\n1. \\(3200 + 80 = 3280\\)\n2. \\(3280 + 560 = 3840\\)\n3. \\(3840 + 14 = 3854\\)\n\nTo verify, we can also use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   47 \\\\\n\\times 82 \\\\\n\\hline\n   94 \\quad (\\text{47} \\times 2) \\\\\n+3760 \\quad (\\text{47} \\times 80) \\\\\n\\hline\n  3854 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 47 and 82 is \\(\\boxed{3854}\\).",
