      "text": "192 + 1600 = 1792? Wait, let me check that again because I might have messed up the shifting.\n\nWait, maybe I should write it out more carefully.\n\nLet me write the multiplication vertically:\n\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a032.\n\u00d7\u00a056.\n------.\nFirst, multiply 32 by 6: 32 \u00d7 6 = 192. That's the first partial product.\n\nThen, multiply 32 by 50 (since the 5 is in the tens place). So 32 \u00d7 50. Let me compute 32 \u00d7 5 first, which is 160, then add a zero at the end to make it 1600. That's the second partial product.\n\nNow, add the two partial products together: 192 + 1600. Let's add them:\n\n192 + 1600. \n\n1600 + 100 is 1700, and then +92 is 1792. Okay, so total is 1792.\n\nWait, but let me check this another way to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Maybe using another method, like breaking down the numbers.\n\nAlternatively, use distributive property: 32 \u00d7 56 = 32 \u00d7 (50 + 6) = 32\u00d750 + 32\u00d76. That's exactly what I did, so that gives the same partial products. So 32\u00d750 is 1600 and 32\u00d76 is 192. Adding them gives 1792. That seems right.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use another approach, like the lattice method or some other trick. Hmm, maybe breaking down both numbers into smaller components?\n\nAlternatively, 32 \u00d7 56. Let me see if I can simplify it. 32 is 30 + 2, so:\n\n(30 + 2) \u00d7 56 = 30\u00d756 + 2\u00d756.\n\nCalculate 30\u00d756: 30\u00d750 = 1500, and 30\u00d76 = 180, so 1500 + 180 = 1680.\n\nThen 2\u00d756 is 112. Adding those together: 1680 + 112 = 1792. Same result. Okay, that's good.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use the fact that 32 \u00d7 56 = (30 \u00d7 56) + (2 \u00d7 56). Wait, that's the same as above. So that's consistent.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use some other multiplication trick. For instance, 32 \u00d7 56 can be thought of as (30 \u00d7 55) + something? Not sure. Alternatively, maybe 32 \u00d7 56 is the same as 16 \u00d7 112? Since 16\u00d72=32, so 16\u00d7112. Let me see, 16\u00d7100=1600, 16\u00d712=192, so 1600+192=1792. Same answer again.\n\nHmm, so all the different methods I try are giving me 1792. That gives me more confidence that it's correct. Let me check with a calculator, but since I don't have a calculator here, I'll just do another verification.\n\nAlternatively, divide the answer by one of the numbers to see if I get the other. Let's see, 1792 divided by 32. Let me compute that. 32 \u00d7 50 is 1600, subtract that from 1792: 1792 - 1600 = 192. Then 32 \u00d7 6 = 192. So 50 + 6 = 56. That works. So 1792 divided by 32 is indeed 56. So that checks out. Alternatively, divide by 56: 1792 \u00f7 56. Let's see, 56 \u00d7 30 = 1680. 1792 - 1680 = 112. Then 56 \u00d7 2 = 112, so total is 30 + 2 = 32. So that also checks out. \n\nTherefore, I think the correct product is 1792. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations. Let me just recap the steps once again to be thorough.\n\nFirst step: multiplying 32 by 6. 2\u00d76=12, carryover 1. 3\u00d76=18 +1=19. So 192. \n\nSecond step: multiplying 32 by 50. Since it's the tens place, we can consider it as 5\u00d732=160, then times 10 gives 1600. Adding those two gives 192+1600=1792. \n\nYes, that all adds up. Alternatively, if I had done the multiplication in a different order, like 56 \u00d7 32, would that give the same result? Let me check that quickly. \n\n56 \u00d7 32: 56\u00d730=1680 and 56\u00d72=112. 1680+112=1792. Yep, same answer. So commutative property holds here, which it should. \n\nAnother way: 56 \u00d7 32. Break 56 into 50 +6 and 32 into 30 +2. So (50+6)(30+2)=50\u00d730 +50\u00d72 +6\u00d730 +6\u00d72. \n\nCalculating each term: 50\u00d730=1500, 50\u00d72=100, 6\u00d730=180, 6\u00d72=12. Adding them up: 1500+100=1600, 1600+180=1780, 1780 +12=1792. Yep, same result. \n\nTherefore, I think that's the correct answer. I can't think of any other way to verify it, but all methods lead to 1792, so I'm pretty confident that's the right product.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 32 and 56 is calculated as follows:\n\nFirst, multiply 32 by 6 (the ones place of 56):\n\\[\n32 \\times 6 = 192\n\\]\n\nNext, multiply 32 by 50 (the tens place of 56, represented by the digit 5):\n\\[\n32 \\times 50 = 1600\n\\]\n\nAdd the two partial products together:\n\\[\n192 + 1600 = 1792\n\\]\n\n**Verification:**\n- Dividing \\(1792 \\div 32 = 56\\), which confirms the result.\n- Alternatively, \\(1792 \\div 56 = 32\\), further validating the calculation.\n\n**Final Answer:**  \nThe product of 32 and 56 is \\boxed{1792}.",
