4=28, so top of diagonal 2, bottom 8 in the top-right cell.\n\nNext, 8\u00d72=16, so top 1, bottom 6 in the bottom-left cell.\n\nLastly, 8\u00d74=32, so top 3, bottom 2 in the bottom-right cell.\n\nNow, adding diagonally from right to left:\n\nThe rightmost diagonal (the ones place) has the bottom of the top-right cell (8) and the bottom of the bottom-right cell (2). 8 + 2 = 10. Write down 0 and carry over 1.\n\nNext diagonal (tens place): top of the bottom-right cell (3), bottom of the top-left cell (4), and the carried over 1. So 3 + 4 + 1 = 8. Wait, actually, I might be mixing up the diagonals here. Let me think again.\n\nWait, maybe the diagonals are structured so that each diagonal corresponds to a place value. Let me try again step by step:\n\nThe grid with the products:\n\nTop row (7 and 8) and right column (2 and 4). The cells are:\n\nTop-left cell: 14 (split into 1/4)\n\nTop-right cell: 28 (2/8)\n\nBottom-left cell: 16 (1/6)\n\nBottom-right cell: 32 (3/2)\n\nNow, the diagonals for addition:\n\nStarting from the rightmost diagonal (the right edge of the grid), which includes the lower half of the top-right cell (8) and the lower half of the bottom-right cell (2). So 8 + 2 = 10. Write 0 in that diagonal's place and carry over 1 to the next diagonal.\n\nNext diagonal (middle): the upper half of the bottom-right cell (3), the lower half of the top-left cell (4), and the carryover 1. So 3 + 4 + 1 = 8. So write 8 there.\n\nNext diagonal (leftmost): the upper half of the top-left cell (1) and the upper half of the bottom-left cell (1). Wait, actually, the bottom-left cell's upper half is part of the next diagonal? Hmm, perhaps I need to visualize this better.\n\nWait, maybe the diagonals are:\n\nRightmost: lower right cells (8 and 2) sum to 10.\n\nMiddle diagonal: upper right (2) and lower left (6) and upper left (1)? Maybe I'm confused. Let me look up the lattice method steps again mentally. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps I should just trust the previous methods since they all gave me 1872, and the lattice might be error-prone without drawing it. Since the standard algorithm and distributive property both give the same answer, I can be confident it's 1872.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use a calculator check, but since this is a thought process, I can't. But since all my manual calculations align, I think that's solid.\n\nAnother sanity check: 78 is close to 80, and 24 is 24. 80\u00d724 is 1920. Since 78 is 2 less than 80, so subtract 2\u00d724=48 from 1920. 1920 - 48 = 1872. Oh, that's another way! Nice, that also gives the same result. That's reassuring.\n\nSo, using the approximation method: rounding 78 up to 80, multiply by 24, then subtract the difference. Since 80-78=2, so 2\u00d724=48. So 80\u00d724=1920 minus 48 is 1872. Yep, that works.\n\nTherefore, all methods converge to 1872. I think that's the correct product. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations. So the answer should be 1872.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 78 and 24 is \\boxed{1872}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 78 and 24, we can use multiple methods to ensure accuracy:\n\n1. **Breaking Down the Numbers**:\n   - Split 24 into 20 and 4.\n   - Calculate \\(78 \\times 20\\):\n     \\[\n     78 \\times 20 = 1560\n     \\]\n   - Calculate \\(78 \\times 4\\):\n     \\[\n     78 \\times 4 = 312\n     \\]\n   - Add the results:\n     \\[\n     1560 + 312 = 1872\n     \\]\n\n2. **Standard Multiplication Algorithm**:\n   - Multiply 78 by 4:\n     \\[\n     78 \\times 4 = 312\n     \\]\n   - Multiply 78 by 20 (note the position of 2 in the tens place):\n     \\[\n     78 \\times 20 = 1560\n     \\]\n   - Add the results:\n     \\[\n     312 + 1560 = 1872\n     \\]\n\n3. **Distributive Property**:\n   - Expand both numbers:\n     \\[\n     (70 + 8)(20 + 4) = 70 \\times 20 + 70 \\times 4 + 8 \\times 20 + 8 \\times 4\n     \\]\n   - Calculate each term:\n     \\[\n     70 \\times 20 = 1400, \\quad 70 \\times 4 = 280, \\quad 8 \\times 20 = 160, \\quad 8 \\times 4 = 32\n     \\]\n   - Sum the terms:\n     \\[\n     1400 + 280 + 160 + 32 = 1872\n     \\]\n\n4. **Approximation Method**:\n   - Round 78 to 80 and multiply by 24:\n     \\[\n     80 \\times 24 = 1920\n     \\]\n   - Subtract the difference (since 78 is 2 less than 80):\n     \\[\n     1920 - (2 \\times 24) = 1920 - 48 = 1872\n     \\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 78 and 24 is \\(\\boxed{1872}\\).",
