      "text": "546 times 10? Wait, no, that's not right. Wait, because 60 is 6 times 10, so if I do 91 times 6 first, then multiplying by 10 would be 546 times 10, which is 5460. Wait, so that would be the result. Let me check that another way to be sure. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do 91 times 60 directly. Let me write it out the standard way. \n\n   91\nx 60\n------\n?\n\nMultiplying 91 by 0 gives 0, and then multiplying 91 by 60. Wait, actually, when multiplying by the 6 in the tens place, it's 6 times 91, but shifted one position to the left because it's the tens place. So 6 times 91 is 546, then add a zero at the end to make it 5460. Yeah, that's the same as before. \n\nWait, maybe another way: 91 times 60. Since 60 is 6*10, so 91*6*10. Which is the same as (91*10)*6? Wait, no, that would be 910*6. Let me see if that gives the same result. 910 times 6. 900*6 is 5400, and 10*6 is 60, so 5400+60=5460. Okay, same answer. \n\nAlternatively, using the distributive property: 91*(60) = (90 +1)*(60) = 90*60 +1*60 = 5400 + 60 = 5460. \n\nAll these methods give me 5460, so I think that's correct. Let me just confirm with a calculator in my mind. 90 times 60 is 5400, so 91 times 60 is 5400 + 60 = 5460. Yep, that's right. \n\nI don't think I made any mistakes here. All the different methods of breaking it down lead to the same answer. So the product of 91 and 60 is definitely 5460. \n\nWait, just to make sure I didn't confuse addition with multiplication somewhere. Let me do 91 multiplied by 60 again step by step. \n\nFirst, 91 multiplied by 60. If I write it vertically:\n\n     91\n   x 60\n--------\n     00   (91 x 0)\n  546    (91 x 6, shifted one position to the left)\n--------\n  5460\n\nYes, that's correct. The zero in the units place from multiplying by the 6 in the tens place, then adding the shifted result. So adding those two gives 5460. \n\nAlternatively, thinking in terms of exponents, 60 is 6*10^1, so 91*6*10^1. 91*6 is 546, times 10 is 5460. \n\nAnother check: 91*60. Let's think of 100*60 = 6000. Then subtract 9*60=540, so 6000-540=5460. Wait, that's another method. Since 91 is 100-9, so (100 -9)*60 = 100*60 -9*60 = 6000 -540 = 5460. \n\nAll right, that also works. So multiple methods confirm that the answer is 5460. I think that's solid. \n\nI guess I can't think of any other way to verify, but since all the different approaches give the same result, I can be confident. Maybe just do the multiplication straight up:\n\n91 times 60. 91 multiplied by 6 is 546, then times 10 is 5460. Yep. \n\nAlright, I think that's the correct answer. No mistakes detected in the reasoning.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 91 and 60 is calculated as follows:\n\n1. **Breakdown Method**:\n   - Multiply 91 by 6:  \n     \\( 91 \\times 6 = 546 \\).\n   - Then multiply the result by 10:  \n     \\( 546 \\times 10 = 5460 \\).\n\n2. **Direct Multiplication**:\n   - Write it vertically:  \n     \\[\n     \\begin{array}{r}\n       \\quad 91 \\\\\n     \\times \\quad 60 \\\\\n     \\hline\n       \\quad 0 \\quad (91 \\times 0) \\\\\n     + 5460 \\quad (91 \\times 60 \\text{ shifted left by one digit}) \\\\\n     \\hline\n       5460 \\\\\n     \\end{array}\n     \\]\n\n3. **Distributive Property**:\n   - \\( 91 \\times 60 = (90 + 1) \\times 60 = 90 \\times 60 + 1 \\times 60 = 5400 + 60 = 5460 \\).\n\n4. **Alternative Verification**:\n   - Using \\( 91 = 100 - 9 \\):  \n     \\( (100 - 9) \\times 60 = 100 \\times 60 - 9 \\times 60 = 6000 - 540 = 5460 \\).\n\nAll methods confirm the result is **5460**.\n\n**Answer:**  \nThe product of 91 and 60 is \\boxed{5460}.",
