      "text": "1400 + 35 = 1435. \n\nAlternatively, using the standard algorithm step by step: \n\nFirst, multiply 35 by 1 (units place): 35 \u00d7 1 = 35. \n\nThen, multiply 35 by 4 (tens place), but since the 4 is in the tens place, it's actually 40. So 35 \u00d7 4 = 140, but shifted one position to the left, making it 1400. \n\nAdding the two results together: 35 + 1400 = 1435. \n\nWait, that matches the previous method. So that seems right. \n\nLet me check another way to confirm. Maybe using the difference of squares or another method? Hmm, not sure if that applies here. Alternatively, perhaps breaking down the numbers differently. \n\n35 is 5\u00d77, and 41 is a prime number. But multiplying those factors wouldn't help much. \n\nAlternatively, maybe adding 35 forty-one times? That sounds tedious but let me try a small part. 35\u00d710 is 350, so 35\u00d740 would be 350\u00d74 = 1400. Then add 35 to get 1435. Yep, same answer. \n\nAnother check: Use estimation. 35 is close to 35 and 41 is close to 40. 35\u00d740 is 1400, so adding the extra 35 for the 41st time gives 1435. That seems consistent. \n\nAlternatively, using calculator verification if I had one, but since I'm doing this manually, I think the multiple methods all leading to 1435 is a good sign. \n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the initial steps. Let me redo the standard algorithm again just to be sure. \n\nWriting 35  \n\u00d741  \n------  \nFirst line: 35 \u00d71 =35  \nSecond line: 35 \u00d740 = 1400 (because 35\u00d74=140, then add a zero)  \nAdd them: 35 +1400 =1435. \n\nYes, that's the same result. I think that's correct. \n\nAlternatively, cross-check using another distributive approach: 41 \u00d735 = (30 +5) \u00d7 (40 +1). Wait, expanding that would be more complicated, but let me see:\n\n(30 +5)(40 +1) = 30\u00d740 + 30\u00d71 +5\u00d740 +5\u00d71 = 1200 +30 +200 +5 = 1200+200 is 1400, plus 30+5 is 35. So total 1435. \n\nOh! That works too. So that's another way, expanding both numbers. So that also gives 1435. Definitely, that confirms it. \n\nTherefore, I can be confident the product is 1435.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 35 and 41 is \\boxed{1435}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 35 and 41, we can use the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n35 \\times 41 = 35 \\times (40 + 1)\n\\]\n\nFirst, calculate \\(35 \\times 40\\):\n\n\\[\n35 \\times 40 = 1400\n\\]\n\nNext, calculate \\(35 \\times 1\\):\n\n\\[\n35 \\times 1 = 35\n\\]\n\nAdd the two results together:\n\n\\[\n1400 + 35 = 1435\n\\]\n\nWe can also verify this using the standard algorithm for multiplication:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   \\quad 35 \\\\\n\\times 41 \\\\\n\\hline\n   \\quad 35 \\quad (\\text{35 \u00d7 1}) \\\\\n+ 1400 \\quad (\\text{35 \u00d7 40, shifted one position to the left}) \\\\\n\\hline\n   1435 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nAnother verification using the expansion of both numbers:\n\n\\[\n(30 + 5) \\times (40 + 1) = 30 \\times 40 + 30 \\times 1 + 5 \\times 40 + 5 \\times 1\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n\\[\n30 \\times 40 = 1200 \\\\\n30 \\times 1 = 30 \\\\\n5 \\times 40 = 200 \\\\\n5 \\times 1 = 5\n\\]\n\nAdding these together:\n\n\\[\n1200 + 30 + 200 + 5 = 1435\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of 35 and 41 is \\(\\boxed{1435}\\).",
