      "text": "564 + 5640 = 6204? Wait, let me check that again. 500 + 5600 is 6100, and 64 + 40 is 104. So 6100 + 104 is 6204. Okay, so that seems right.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method like the distributive property. Let's see: 94 times 66 can be split into (90 + 4) times (60 +6). So expanding that, it's 90*60 + 90*6 + 4*60 +4*6.\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n90*60: 9*6=54, so 90*60=5400.\n\n90*6: 9*6=54, so 540.\n\n4*60: 4*6=24, so 240.\n\n4*6: 24.\n\nNow add all those together: 5400 +540 is 5940. Then 5940 +240 is 6180. Then 6180 +24=6204. Same result. Okay, that's good.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use some other trick. Like 94 is close to 100, so maybe 94 = 100 -6. So 94*66 = (100 -6)*66 = 100*66 -6*66. 100*66 is 6600, and 6*66 is 396. So 6600 - 396 = ?\n\n6600 minus 396. Let's subtract 400 from 6600, which is 6200, then add back 4, so 6204. Yep, same answer again.\n\nHmm, so all methods give me 6204. Let me just confirm with another approach. Maybe 66 is 60 +6, so 94*66 = 94*(60+6) = 94*60 +94*6. Let's compute those:\n\n94*60: 94*6=564, so 564*10=5640.\n\n94*6: 564 as before. So 5640 +564 = 6204. Yep, same.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can think of 66 as 6*11, so 94*6*11. 94*6 is 564, then 564*11. 564*10 is 5640 plus 564 is 6204 again.\n\nOkay, seems like all methods converge to 6204. So probably that's the right answer. But just to be absolutely sure, maybe do the multiplication step by step again.\n\nMultiplying 94 by 66:\n\nFirst, 94 times 6 is 564. Then 94 times 60 is 5640. Adding them gives 564 +5640. Let's do 5640 + 500 is 6140, then add 64 to get 6204. Yep.\n\nAlternatively, maybe write down the multiplication vertically again:\n\n   94\nx 66\n-------\nFirst multiply by 6:\n\n94 *6=564\n\nThen multiply by 60 (so shift one position):\n\n94*60=5640\n\nAdd them:\n\n564\n\n+5640\n\n=6204\n\nYes, that's correct. So I think the answer is 6204.\n\nWait, just to check if I didn't make a mistake in any step. Let me verify 94*66 with another method. Let's do 66*94.\n\n66*90 is 5940, and 66*4 is 264. So 5940 +264.\n\n5940 +200 is 6140, plus 64 is 6204. Yep, same result. So I think that's solid.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps use a calculator if I have one, but since this is a problem-solving exercise, I can't. But given all the cross-verification, I'm confident it's 6204.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 94 and 66 is \\boxed{6204}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 94 and 66, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n1. Multiply 94 by 6 (the units digit of 66):\n   \\[\n   94 \\times 6 = 564\n   \\]\n2. Multiply 94 by 60 (the tens digit of 66, which is equivalent to 6 shifted one position to the left):\n   \\[\n   94 \\times 60 = 5640\n   \\]\n3. Add the two partial products together:\n   \\[\n   564 + 5640 = 6204\n   \\]\n\nWe can verify this result using the distributive property:\n- Break down the numbers: \\(94 = 90 + 4\\) and \\(66 = 60 + 6\\)\n- Expand the product:\n  \\[\n  (90 + 4)(60 + 6) = 90 \\times 60 + 90 \\times 6 + 4 \\times 60 + 4 \\times 6\n  \\]\n- Calculate each term:\n  \\[\n  90 \\times 60 = 5400, \\quad 90 \\times 6 = 540, \\quad 4 \\times 60 = 240, \\quad 4 \\times 6 = 24\n  \\]\n- Sum the terms:\n  \\[\n  5400 + 540 + 240 + 24 = 6204\n  \\]\n\nAnother verification using the difference of squares:\n- Rewrite 94 as \\(100 - 6\\):\n  \\[\n  (100 - 6) \\times 66 = 100 \\times 66 - 6 \\times 66\n  \\]\n- Calculate each term:\n  \\[\n  100 \\times 66 = 6600, \\quad 6 \\times 66 = 396\n  \\]\n- Subtract the terms:\n  \\[\n  6600 - 396 = 6204\n  \\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 94 and 66 is \\(\\boxed{6204}\\).",
