      "text": "750 minus 15 is 735. \n\nWait, let me check again to be sure. Alternatively, maybe I can do it another way to confirm. Let's try multiplying 49 by 15 the standard way. .\n\nWriting it out vertically:\n\n   49\nx 15\n------\nFirst multiply 49 by 5 (the units place of 15). 49*5 is 245. Then multiply 49 by 10 (the tens place of 15, which is 1 in the tens place so 10). 49*10 is 490. Then add those two results together: 245 + 490. \n\nAdding those: 245 + 490. Let's see, 200 + 400 is 600, and 45 + 90 is 135. So 600 + 135 is 735. Yep, same answer. That's good. \n\nAlternatively, I can think of 15 as 10 + 5. So 49*(10 +5) = 49*10 + 49*5. Which again is 490 + 245 = 735. \n\nHmm, all methods are giving me 735. I think that's correct. Let me just verify with another approach. Maybe using the fact that 15 is 3*5. So 3*5*49. Multiplying 5 and 49 first. 5*49 is 245. Then 245*3. 245*3: 200*3 is 600, 40*3 is 120, 5*3 is 15. Adding those: 600 + 120 is 720, plus 15 is 735. Yep, same result. \n\nAlright, so all different multiplication methods are leading me to 735, so I feel confident that the product of 15 and 49 is indeed 735. I think that's the right answer.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 15 and 49 can be calculated using the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n15 \\times 49 = 15 \\times (50 - 1) = (15 \\times 50) - (15 \\times 1)\n\\]\n\nBreaking it down:\n1. \\(15 \\times 50 = 750\\)\n2. \\(15 \\times 1 = 15\\)\n\nSubtracting these results:\n\\[\n750 - 15 = 735\n\\]\n\nVerification using standard multiplication:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   49 \\\\\n\\times 15 \\\\\n\\hline\n  245 \\quad (\\text{49} \\times 5) \\\\\n+490 \\quad (\\text{49} \\times 10) \\\\\n\\hline\n  735 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm the answer is \\(\\boxed{735}\\).",
