place: 5 + 2 =7\n\nHundreds place: 1 + 8 =9\n\nThousands place: 0 +1 =1\n\nSo that gives 1976. Yep, same as before.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can check with a calculator if I have one, but since this is a problem-solving scenario, suppose I can't. But since all methods are giving me the same result, I can be confident it's 1976.\n\nAlternatively, I can check by dividing the answer by one of the numbers to see if I get the other. Let's divide 1976 by 26 and see if it equals 76.\n\nLet's do 1976 \u00f7 26.\n\n26 \u00d7 70 = 1820. Subtract that from 1976: 1976 - 1820 = 156.\n\nThen 156 \u00f726 =6. So total is 70 +6=76. Perfect, that checks out.\n\nAlternatively, divide 1976 by 76 and see if it's 26.\n\n76 \u00d720 = 1520. 1976 -1520 =456.\n\n76 \u00d76=456. So 20+6=26. Correct again.\n\nSo, all verification steps confirm that the product is 1976. I think that's solid. So the answer should be 1976.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 26 and 76 is \\boxed{1976}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 26 and 76, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n1. Multiply 26 by the ones digit of 76 (which is 6):\n   \\[\n   26 \\times 6 = 156\n   \\]\n2. Multiply 26 by the tens digit of 76 (which is 7, representing 70), and shift the result one place to the left:\n   \\[\n   26 \\times 7 = 182 \\quad \\text{(then shift left to get 1820)}\n   \\]\n3. Add the two results together:\n   \\[\n   156 + 1820 = 1976\n   \\]\n\nVerification using the distributive property:\n1. Break down the multiplication:\n   \\[\n   26 \\times 76 = (20 + 6) \\times 76 = 20 \\times 76 + 6 \\times 76\n   \\]\n2. Calculate each part:\n   \\[\n   20 \\times 76 = 1520 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 6 \\times 76 = 456\n   \\]\n3. Add the results:\n   \\[\n   1520 + 456 = 1976\n   \\]\n\nVerification using division:\n1. Divide the result by 26:\n   \\[\n   1976 \\div 26 = 76\n   \\]\n2. Divide the result by 76:\n   \\[\n   1976 \\div 76 = 26\n   \\]\n\nBoth verifications confirm the result is correct. \n\nThe product of 26 and 76 is \\boxed{1976}.",
