n't have one here, let me do another manual check. \n\nLet me add 53 twenty-four times. But that seems tedious, but maybe for a few times to see. \n\nAlternatively, check if 1272 divided by 24 equals 53. Let's see. \n\n1272 \u00f7 24. Let's divide both numerator and denominator by 8: 1272 \u00f78= 159, 24 \u00f78=3. So now it's 159 \u00f73. 3 goes into 15 five times, 5*3=15, subtract 15, bring down 9. 3 goes into 9 three times. So total is 53. \n\nYes, so division confirms it. Therefore, the product is indeed 1272. \n\nI think that's thorough. I can't see any mistakes in the calculations. All methods cross-verify each other. So I'm confident the answer is correct.\n\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>The product of 53 and 24 can be calculated using the standard multiplication method:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   \\phantom{0}53 \\\\\n\\times 24 \\\\\n\\hline\n   212 \\quad (\\text{53 multiplied by 4}) \\\\\n+1060 \\quad (\\text{53 multiplied by 20, shifted one position to the left}) \\\\\n\\hline\n   1272 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of 53 and 24 is \\boxed{1272}.<\uff5c\n\nTo find the product of 53 and 24, we can use the standard multiplication method:\n\n1. **Multiply 53 by 4 (units place of 24):**\n   \\[\n   53 \\times 4 = 212\n   \\]\n\n2. **Multiply 53 by 20 (tens place of 24, which is equivalent to 24's 2 multiplied by 10):**\n   \\[\n   53 \\times 20 = 1060\n   \\]\n\n3. **Add the results of the two multiplications:**\n   \\[\n   212 + 1060 = 1272\n   \\]\n\nThus, the product of 53 and 24 is \\boxed{1272}.",
