u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014  \n\u2003\u200391  (this is 91\u00d71)  \n+3640 (this is 91\u00d740, shifted one position to the left)  \n\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014  \n\u20033731  \n\nYes, that's the same as before. So I think that's solid. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use subtraction method? For instance, 91 \u00d741. Hmm, not sure if that's helpful here. Maybe 91 is close to 90, and 41 is close to 40, but not sure if that helps. Wait, let me see:\n\nAlternatively, 91 \u00d741 = (90 +1)(40 +1) = 90\u00d740 +90\u00d71 +1\u00d740 +1\u00d71 = 3600 +90 +40 +1= 3600+130+1= 3731. Same result again.\n\nAlternatively, maybe check if 3731 divided by 91 equals 41. Let me try that division. \n\nDividing 3731 by 91. Let's see: 91 \u00d740 = 3640. 3731 - 3640 = 91. So 40 +1 =41. So yes, 91\u00d741=3731. \n\nAlternatively, 3731 divided by 41. Let me see: 41 \u00d7 90 is 3690. 3731 - 3690 =41. So that's 90 +1=91. So yes, that also checks out. \n\nTherefore, after multiple methods and cross-verifications, I'm confident that the product of 91 and 41 is 3731.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 91 and 41 is \\boxed{3731}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 91 and 41, we can use the distributive property and break down the numbers into more manageable parts:\n\n1. **Using the distributive property**:\n   \\[\n   91 \\times 41 = 91 \\times (40 + 1) = (91 \\times 40) + (91 \\times 1)\n   \\]\n   - Calculate \\(91 \\times 40\\):\n     \\[\n     91 \\times 40 = 91 \\times 4 \\times 10 = 364 \\times 10 = 3640\n     \\]\n   - Calculate \\(91 \\times 1\\):\n     \\[\n     91 \\times 1 = 91\n     \\]\n   - Add the results:\n     \\[\n     3640 + 91 = 3731\n     \\]\n\n2. **Verification using another method**:\n   - Break down both numbers further:\n     \\[\n     (90 + 1) \\times (40 + 1) = 90 \\times 40 + 90 \\times 1 + 1 \\times 40 + 1 \\times 1\n     \\]\n     - Calculate each term:\n       \\[\n       90 \\times 40 = 3600\n       \\]\n       \\[\n       90 \\times 1 = 90\n       \\]\n       \\[\n       1 \\times 40 = 40\n       \\]\n       \\[\n       1 \\times 1 = 1\n       \\]\n     - Sum all terms:\n       \\[\n       3600 + 90 + 40 + 1 = 3731\n       \\]\n\n3. **Verification through division**:\n   - Check if \\(3731 \\div 91 = 41\\):\n     \\[\n     91 \\times 40 = 3640 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 3731 - 3640 = 91 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad 40 + 1 = 41\n     \\]\n   - Check if \\(3731 \\div 41 = 91\\):\n     \\[\n     41 \\times 90 = 3690 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 3731 - 3690 = 41 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad 90 + 1 = 91\n     \\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 91 and 41 is \\(\\boxed{3731}\\).",
