      "text": "189 times 10, which is 1890. Wait, is that right? Let me check another way to be sure.\n\nAlternatively, I can use the standard multiplication algorithm. Multiplying 63 by 30. Since 30 has a zero at the end, maybe I can multiply 63 by 3 and then add a zero. Exactly what I did before. So 63 times 3 is 189, then adding a zero gives 1890. That's the same result. \n\nAnother way: Maybe using distributive property. 63 times 30 can be thought of as 60 times 30 plus 3 times 30. Let's see. 60 times 30 is 1800, and 3 times 30 is 90. Adding them together gives 1800 + 90 = 1890. Yep, same answer again. \n\nHmm, all methods lead to 1890. I think that's correct. Let me confirm with a calculator in my mind. 30 times 60 is 1800, and 30 times 3 is 90. Adding them gives 1890. Definitely, that's the right answer. So the product of 30 and 63 is 1890.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 30 and 63 is calculated as follows:\n\n\\[\n30 \\times 63 = 30 \\times (60 + 3) = (30 \\times 60) + (30 \\times 3) = 1800 + 90 = 1890\n\\]\n\n**Answer:** \\(\\boxed{1890}\\)",
