      "text": "4900 + 7? Wait, no. Wait, 4870 + 27. Let me check again. 4870 + 20 is 4890, then +7 is 4897. Wait, that doesn't make sense. Wait, 4870 plus 27. Let me do it digit by digit. 4870 plus 20 is 4890, then add 7, that's 4897. Hmm, but that seems off. Wait, maybe I made a mistake in the addition steps.\n\nWait, let me try adding all the numbers again:\n\nFirst, 4000 + 720 is 4720.\n\nThen, 4720 + 150. Let's see, 4720 + 100 is 4820, plus 50 more is 4870. That's correct.\n\nThen, 4870 + 27. So 4870 + 20 is 4890, then plus 7 is 4897. Wait, is that right? Because 4870 + 27. Let me do the math another way: 4870 + 27 = 4870 + 30 - 3 = 4900 - 3 = 4897. Yeah, that's correct. Hmm, but I was expecting maybe a round number? Maybe I did the multiplication wrong?\n\nWait, let me double-check each multiplication step. Maybe I messed up one of those. Let's go back.\n\nFirst term: 80*50. 80 times 50. 8*5 is 40, and adding the two zeros gives 4000. That seems right.\n\nSecond term: 80*9. 8*9 is 72, so 720. Correct.\n\nThird term: 3*50 = 150. Correct.\n\nFourth term: 3*9=27. Correct.\n\nAdding them up again:\n\n4000 + 720 = 4720.\n\n4720 + 150: 4720 + 100 is 4820, plus 50 is 4870. Correct.\n\n4870 +27: 4870 +20 is 4890, plus 7 is 4897. So that's 4897. Wait, but I thought maybe there's a better way to do this?\n\nAlternatively, maybe using another method. Let me try multiplying 83 by 59 the standard way, vertically.\n\nSo, writing 83\n\ntimes 59\n\n________\n\nFirst, multiply 83 by 9: 83*9. Let's see, 80*9=720, 3*9=27, so total is 720+27=747. So that's 747.\n\nThen, multiply 83 by 50. Since 59 is 50 +9, so this is the hundreds place. So 83*50. Well, 83*5 is 415, then times 10 is 4150.\n\nNow, add 747 and 4150 together. 747 + 4150. Let's see, 700 + 4150 is 4850, plus 47 is 4897. So same result. Hmm.\n\nWait, so both methods give me 4897. Then maybe that's correct. But let me check with another approach, perhaps using the difference of squares or something?\n\nAlternatively, note that 83 and 59 are close to 80 and 60? Hmm, maybe not. Alternatively, 83*59 can be thought as (80 +3)*(60 -1). Wait, no, 59 is 60-1. Let's try that.\n\nSo, (80 +3)(60 -1) = 80*60 +80*(-1) +3*60 +3*(-1). Let's compute that.\n\n80*60 is 4800. 80*(-1) is -80. 3*60 is 180. 3*(-1) is -3. So adding all together:\n\n4800 -80 is 4720. 4720 +180 is 4900. 4900 -3 is 4897. Same result again. So that seems consistent.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use the identity (a)(b) = [(a + b)/2]^2 - [(a - b)/2]^2. But that might be more complicated here.\n\nAlternatively, let me use calculator verification. Wait, since I can't use a calculator, but maybe approximate. 80*60 is 4800, so 83*59 is a bit less than that? Wait, 83 is 3 more than 80, and 59 is 1 less than 60. So the difference would be 3*60 - 1*80 + (3*(-1))? Hmm, maybe not. Alternatively, the exact value is 80*60 + 80*(-1) +3*60 +3*(-1) as above, which gives 4800 -80 +180 -3 = 4800 +100 -3 = 4900 -3=4897. So that works.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I can think of 83*59 as 83*(60 -1) = 83*60 -83. 83*60 is 83*6*10. 83*6 is 498, so *10 is 4980. Then subtract 83: 4980 -83 = 4897. Yep, that's another way. So that also gives the same answer.\n\nOkay, so multiple methods are leading me to 4897, so that must be correct. Maybe I was just second-guessing because I thought the answer might be a round number, but 4897 is the correct product. Therefore, the answer is 4897.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 83 and 59 is \\boxed{4897}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 83 and 59, we can use the distributive property by breaking down the numbers into smaller parts:\n\n\\[\n(80 + 3)(50 + 9)\n\\]\n\nApplying the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n80 \\times 50 + 80 \\times 9 + 3 \\times 50 + 3 \\times 9\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term step by step:\n\n1. \\(80 \\times 50 = 4000\\)\n2. \\(80 \\times 9 = 720\\)\n3. \\(3 \\times 50 = 150\\)\n4. \\(3 \\times 9 = 27\\)\n\nAdding these results together:\n\n\\[\n4000 + 720 + 150 + 27\n\\]\n\nFirst, add \\(4000 + 720\\):\n\n\\[\n4000 + 720 = 4720\n\\]\n\nNext, add \\(4720 + 150\\):\n\n\\[\n4720 + 150 = 4870\n\\]\n\nThen, add \\(4870 + 27\\):\n\n\\[\n4870 + 27 = 4897\n\\]\n\nTo verify, we can use another method by breaking down the multiplication:\n\n\\[\n83 \\times 59 = 83 \\times (60 - 1) = 83 \\times 60 - 83\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n1. \\(83 \\times 60 = 4980\\)\n2. Subtracting 83 from 4980:\n\n\\[\n4980 - 83 = 4897\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 83 and 59 is \\(\\boxed{4897}\\).",
