      "text": "1548? Wait, hold on, let me check that addition again because 258 + 1290 might not be right.\n\nWait, 258 + 1290. Let me stack them:\n\n\u2003258.\n+1290.\n------.\n\nAdding the ones: 8 + 0 = 8. Tens: 5 +9 =14, so write 4, carryover 1. Hundreds: 2 +2 =4, plus the carried 1 is 5. Thousands: 1. So yes, that gives 1548. Hmm, okay.\n\nBut let me verify this with another method to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Maybe using the distributive property: (40 + 3)(30 +6). Let's expand this.\n\n40\u00d730 + 40\u00d76 + 3\u00d730 + 3\u00d76.\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n40\u00d730 = 1200.\n\n40\u00d76 = 240.\n\n3\u00d730 = 90.\n\n3\u00d76 = 18.\n\nAdding those up: 1200 + 240 = 1440; 1440 +90 = 1530; 1530 +18 = 1548. Okay, same result. That's a good sign.\n\nAlternatively, maybe do 43\u00d736 by breaking it down differently. Let's see, 43\u00d736 = 43\u00d7(30 +6) = 43\u00d730 +43\u00d76. Let's compute each part.\n\n43\u00d730: 43\u00d73 is 129, so times 10 is 1290.\n\n43\u00d76: 40\u00d76=240, 3\u00d76=18, so 240 +18=258.\n\nThen add 1290 +258: 1290 +200 is 1490, plus 58 is 1548. Same answer again. So that's consistent.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use another method, like the lattice method? Let me try that quickly, though I might be a bit rusty.\n\nWait, maybe overcomplicating. Since I got the same answer through two different methods, that gives me more confidence. Alternatively, use a calculator, but since I can't, but maybe check with another approach.\n\nAlternatively, think of 43\u00d736 as (40 +3)(36) =40\u00d736 +3\u00d736. Compute 40\u00d736: 4\u00d736=144, so 144\u00d710=1440. Then 3\u00d736=108. Adding those: 1440 +108 =1548. Yep, same result.\n\nHmm, so all methods are pointing to 1548. I think that's correct. Let me just check once more with standard multiplication, maybe I made a mistake in the first step.\n\nFirst partial product: 6\u00d743. 6\u00d73 is 18, carryover 1. 6\u00d74 is 24, plus 1 is 25. So that's 258. Correct.\n\nSecond partial product: 3 (tens place) \u00d743. Since it's the tens place, it's 30\u00d743. So 3\u00d743=129, so 1290. Adding 258 +1290: 258 +1290. Let's see: 258 + 1000 is 1258; 1258 +290 is 1548. Yep, that works.\n\nI think that's solid. So the product of 43 and 36 is 1548. I don't see any errors in my calculations, and multiple methods confirm it. So I'm confident that's the right answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 43 and 36 is \\boxed{1548}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 43 and 36, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n1. Write the numbers vertically aligned by place value:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n    \\ 43 \\\\\n   \\times 36 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n2. Multiply 43 by the ones digit of 36 (which is 6):\n   \\[\n   43 \\times 6 = 258\n   \\]\n\n3. Multiply 43 by the tens digit of 36 (which is 3, representing 30). This result is shifted one position to the left:\n   \\[\n   43 \\times 30 = 1290\n   \\]\n\n4. Add the two partial products together:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n    258 \\\\\n   +1290 \\\\\n   \\hline\n   1548 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\nVerification using the distributive property:\n\\[\n(40 + 3)(30 + 6) = 40 \\times 30 + 40 \\times 6 + 3 \\times 30 + 3 \\times 6\n\\]\n\\[\n= 1200 + 240 + 90 + 18 = 1548\n\\]\n\nAnother verification by breaking down the multiplication:\n\\[\n43 \\times 36 = 43 \\times (30 + 6) = 43 \\times 30 + 43 \\times 6\n\\]\n\\[\n= 1290 + 258 = 1548\n\\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 43 and 36 is \\(\\boxed{1548}\\).",
