      "text": "1260 plus 126 equals 1386. Hmm, wait, let me check that addition again. 1260 + 100 is 1360, then plus 26 more would be 1386. Yeah, that's correct. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do the multiplication the standard way, vertically. Let me try that to double-check. \n\nMultiplying 21 by 66. Let me write 21 on top and 66 below. Wait, actually, the standard algorithm is to write the numbers vertically aligned:\n\n\u2003\u00a021  \n\u00d7\u00a066  \n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500  \n\nStarting with the units place of the bottom number (6). Multiply 21 by 6. 1*6=6, 2*6=12. So that's 126. Then, moving to the tens place (the other 6 in the tens place is actually 60). So we need to shift one position to the left and multiply 21 by 60. \n\n21*60 is 1260, as before. Then adding them together: 126 + 1260 = 1386. Yep, same result. \n\nAlternatively, maybe there's another way, like using some mental math tricks. Let's see. 21 times 66. Since 21 is 3 times 7, and 66 is 6 times 11. Not sure if that helps. Alternatively, 21 times 66 is the same as 20 times 66 plus 1 times 66. Let's try that. \n\n20 times 66: 20*60 is 1200, and 20*6 is 120, so 1200 + 120 = 1320. Then adding 1*66 is 66. So 1320 +66 is 1386. Same answer again. \n\nHmm, so all methods are leading me to 1386. I think that's correct. Let me check with a calculator method. Wait, since I can't use a calculator, but maybe verify by division. Let me see if 1386 divided by 21 gives 66. \n\nDividing 1386 \u00f721. Let's see. 21*60 is 1260. Subtract that from 1386: 1386-1260 = 126. Then 126 divided by 21 is 6. So total is 60 +6 =66. Perfect, that checks out. \n\nAlternatively, divide 1386 by 66 and see if it's 21. Let's try that. 66 times 20 is 1320. Subtract that from 1386: 1386-1320 =66. 66 divided by 66 is 1. So total is 21. Yep, that works too. \n\nSo through multiple methods: distributive property, standard algorithm, different breakdowns, and verification via division, I keep getting 1386. I think that's solid. \n\nWait, just to make sure I didn't make any arithmetic errors. Let me redo one of the multiplication steps again. Let's take the standard vertical method again. \n\n21  \n\u00d766  \n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500  \nFirst multiply 21 by 6 (units place):  \n1\u00d76=6, 2\u00d76=12. So 126.  \n\nThen multiply 21 by 6 in the tens place, so it's actually 60. So we write a 0 placeholder in the units place and then multiply. Wait, actually, in the standard algorithm, when you move to the tens digit, you shift one position to the left, so it's like multiplying by 60. So you write 21*6 (126) shifted one place, which becomes 1260. Adding 126 and 1260 gives 1386. Yep, same. \n\nAnother way: 21 multiplied by 66. Since 66 is close to 70, maybe adjust. 21*(70 -4) = 21*70 -21*4. 21*70: 21*7=147, so 147*10=1470. 21*4=84. So 1470-84=1386. That's another method giving the same result. \n\nAlright, I think that's thorough. All paths lead to 1386, so I'm confident that's the correct product.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 21 and 66 is calculated as follows:\n\nUsing the distributive property:\n\\[\n21 \\times 66 = 21 \\times (60 + 6) = (21 \\times 60) + (21 \\times 6)\n\\]\n\\[\n21 \\times 60 = 1260 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 21 \\times 6 = 126\n\\]\nAdding the results:\n\\[\n1260 + 126 = 1386\n\\]\n\nVerification via standard multiplication:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n\\quad \\ 21 \\\\\n\\times 66 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\quad 126 \\quad (\\text{21 \u00d7 6}) \\\\\n+ 1260 \\quad (\\text{21 \u00d7 60, shifted left by one digit}) \\\\\n\\hline\n1386 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nDivision check:\n\\[\n1386 \\div 21 = 66 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 1386 \\div 66 = 21\n\\]\n\n**Final Answer:** \\boxed{1386}",
