      "text": "16 + 480 = 496.\n\nWait, let me check that again. Because 16 \u00d7 30 is 480, adding the 16 gives 500 - 4? Wait, 480 + 16 is 496? Hmm, yes, because 480 + 10 is 490, then plus 6 is 496. Okay, that seems right.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method to verify. Like the distributive property. Breaking down the numbers into smaller parts. Let's see, 16 times 31. Maybe 16 times (30 + 1). So that's 16\u00d730 + 16\u00d71. Which is exactly what I did before. So that gives 480 + 16 = 496 again. Okay, that's consistent.\n\nAnother way: perhaps using the difference of squares or some other trick? Let me see. Wait, 16 is 16, and 31 is 31. Hmm, not sure if that applies here. Alternatively, maybe breaking 31 into 30 +1, which we already did. Alternatively, 16 times 31. Let me think of 16 as 10 + 6. Then, (10 +6) \u00d731 = 10\u00d731 +6\u00d731. 10\u00d731 is 310, and 6\u00d731 is 186. Adding those together: 310 + 186 = 496. That's the same result again. So, that's three different methods giving me 496. That gives me more confidence that it's correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using a calculator? But since this is a problem-solving exercise, I should do it manually. Wait, another way: the lattice method? Let me try that. Hmm, for 16 \u00d731. Create a 2x2 grid because both numbers have two digits. The digits of 16 go on top, and 31 on the right side. So, split each cell into diagonals. Multiply each digit pair and add along the diagonals.\n\nFirst digit top: 1, 6; right side: 3,1.\n\nFirst cell: 1\u00d73 = 3. Split into top-left and bottom-right. So, 3 in the top left and 0 in the bottom right? Wait, maybe I need to write the tens place and the ones place. Wait, 1\u00d73=03, so split into 0 and 3. So top left cell has 0 in top left and 3 in bottom right.\n\nSecond cell in the top row: 6\u00d73=18. So split into 1 (tens) and 8 (ones).\n\nThird cell (second row first column): 1\u00d71=01, so 0 and 1.\n\nFourth cell (second row second column): 6\u00d71=06, so 0 and 6.\n\nNow, adding along the diagonals from the right. The rightmost diagonal is just the 8 from the top right cell and the 6 from the bottom right cell? Wait, maybe I'm getting confused. Let me visualize the lattice:\n\nTop row digits: 1 (column 1), 6 (column 2)\n\nRight side digits: 3 (row 1), 1 (row 2)\n\nThe grid cells:\n\nRow 1 (3):\n\nColumn 1: 1\u00d73 = 03 \u2192 top left 0, bottom right 3.\n\nColumn 2: 6\u00d73 = 18 \u2192 top left 1, bottom right 8.\n\nRow 2 (1):\n\nColumn 1: 1\u00d71 = 01 \u2192 top left 0, bottom right 1.\n\nColumn 2: 6\u00d71 = 06 \u2192 top left 0, bottom right 6.\n\nNow, the lattice should look like this with diagonals dividing each cell:\n\nFor each cell, the top left is the tens place and bottom right is the ones place? Wait, maybe it's the other way around. Wait, perhaps each cell's top left is for the upper diagonal and bottom right is lower?\n\nActually, in the lattice method, each cell is divided by a diagonal line from top right to bottom left. So the top left part is the tens digit, and the bottom right is the ones digit of the product of the two digits. Then, when summing, you add along the diagonals from the bottom right to the top left.\n\nSo arranging the lattice:\n\nThe four cells:\n\nFirst row (3):\n\nCell 1: 1\u00d73 = 03 \u2192 top left 0, bottom right 3.\n\nCell 2: 6\u00d73 =18 \u2192 top left 1, bottom right 8.\n\nSecond row (1):\n\nCell 3: 1\u00d71 =01 \u2192 top left 0, bottom right 1.\n\nCell 4: 6\u00d71 =06 \u2192 top left 0, bottom right 6.\n\nNow, the grid would look like this with diagonals:\n\nFor the first row's first cell (1\u00d73):\n\n0\n\n3\n\nBut arranged with the diagonal split.\n\nNow, to add along the diagonals. The diagonals go from the bottom right of the grid to the top left. Let me see:\n\nThe rightmost diagonal is just the bottom right of the last cell (cell 2 and cell4). Wait, perhaps it's better to number the diagonals.\n\nThe diagonals in the lattice are vertical from top to bottom. Let me try to reconstruct.\n\nThe lattice has two rows and two columns, so the diagonals for summing would be three in total: the far right diagonal (units place), the middle (tens place), and the left (hundreds place).\n\nStarting from the bottom right corner (the 8 in cell 2's bottom right and the 6 in cell4's bottom right? Wait, perhaps I need to map each cell's bottom right and top left to the diagonals.\n\nWait, maybe a better way is to look at each diagonal path from the bottom right to the top left.\n\nThe first diagonal (units place) is the bottom right of the last cell (cell 2 and cell4). Wait, the cells are arranged as:\n\nRow 1: cells (1,2)\n\nRow 2: cells (3,4)\n\nSo the grid:\n\nRow 1: [ [0/3], [1/8] ]\n\nRow 2: [ [0/1], [0/6] ]\n\nThe diagonals to sum are as follows:\n\nFirst diagonal (units place): the bottom right of cell 2 (8) and the bottom right of cell4 (6). Wait, no, perhaps each diagonal is a diagonal line through the cells.\n\nActually, the lattice method's diagonals are such that each diagonal corresponds to a place value. Starting from the bottom right, the first diagonal is the units place, then moving left, the tens, then hundreds.\n\nLooking at the cells:\n\nThe bottom right corner is the cell in row 2, column 2 (the 0/6). The diagonal starting there is just the 6 (units place).\n\nNext diagonal to the left includes the bottom right of cell 1 (3) and the top left of cell4 (0). Wait, maybe I need to visualize it as follows:\n\nWhen you draw the lattice, each cell is split by a diagonal line. Then, you add the numbers along each diagonal, starting from the bottom right.\n\nLet me try to map the numbers:\n\nThe bottom right cell (row2, column2) has the bottom right part as 6. That's the first diagonal (units place): 6.\n\nNext diagonal (tens place) includes the top right part of cell4 (which is the top left part of the bottom right cell? Wait, maybe this is getting too confusing. Alternatively, perhaps it's better to look up the lattice method