fy, we can use the distributive property:\n- Break down 31 into 30 + 1:\n  \\[\n  16 \\times 31 = 16 \\times (30 + 1) = 16 \\times 30 + 16 \\times 1 = 480 + 16 = 496\n  \\]\n\nAnother verification using the distributive property:\n- Break down 16 into 10 + 6:\n  \\[\n  (10 + 6) \\times 31 = 10 \\times 31 + 6 \\times 31 = 310 + 186 = 496\n  \\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 16 and 31 is \\(\\boxed{496}\\).",
