      "text": "7900 + 20 = 7920? Wait, that doesn't sound right. Wait, hold on, maybe I did that subtraction wrong. Let me try again. \n\nAlternatively, 8000 minus 80. Break it down: 8000 minus 80 is like subtracting 8 tens from 8000. 8000 divided by 10 is 800, so subtracting 8 tens (which is 80) would leave 7920? Hmm, actually that seems correct. Let me check with another method to confirm. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe just do the multiplication the standard way. 80 times 99. Let's see:\n\nFirst, write 80 as 80 and 99 as 99. Multiply 80 by 99. \n\nMultiplying 80 by 99:\n\nMultiply 80 by 9 (the units digit of 99): 80 \u00d7 9 = 720. \n\nThen multiply 80 by 90 (the tens digit of 99, so 9 in the tens place is 90). 80 \u00d7 90 = 7200. \n\nWait, but actually, in standard multiplication, you have to consider the place value. Let me write it out properly:\n\n    80\n\u00d7  99\n-------\nFirst, multiply 80 by 9 (units place): 80 \u00d7 9 = 720.\n\nThen multiply 80 by 90 (since the next digit is the tens place, which is 9 in the tens place, so 9\u00d710=90). 80 \u00d7 90 = 7200.\n\nAdd those two results together: 720 + 7200 = 7920. Okay, so that matches the previous result. So that's good, both methods give me 7920. \n\nAlternatively, I can also check using another approach. Let me think, 80 times 100 is 8000, so subtract 80 gives me 7920. Yep, that's the same as before. \n\nAlternatively, maybe use a calculator? But since I don't have one here, I have to rely on manual calculations. Let me do another verification step. \n\nLet me add 80 ninety-nine times. Wait, that's tedious, but perhaps I can think of it as 80 added 99 times. But that's not practical. Alternatively, maybe break down 99 into smaller numbers. \n\nWait, 99 is 90 + 9. So 80\u00d7(90+9) = 80\u00d790 + 80\u00d79, which is exactly what I did before, resulting in 7200 + 720 = 7920. \n\nHmm, so all methods lead me to the same answer. Therefore, I think the product of 80 and 99 is 7920. \n\nJust to be thorough, maybe I can do the multiplication vertically:\n\n      80\n    \u00d7 99\n    -----\n      720   (80 \u00d7 9)\n    +7200   (80 \u00d7 90, shifted one position to the left)\n    -----\n     7920\n\nYes, adding those gives 7920. So I'm confident that's the correct answer. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations here. So I think that's it.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 80 and 99 is \\boxed{7920}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 80 and 99, we can use the distributive property by recognizing that 99 is 100 minus 1:\n\n\\[\n80 \\times 99 = 80 \\times (100 - 1)\n\\]\n\nBreaking this down, we get:\n\n\\[\n80 \\times 100 - 80 \\times 1\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term separately:\n\n\\[\n80 \\times 100 = 8000\n\\]\n\\[\n80 \\times 1 = 80\n\\]\n\nSubtracting these results:\n\n\\[\n8000 - 80 = 7920\n\\]\n\nTo verify, we can also use standard multiplication:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   80 \\\\\n\\times 99 \\\\\n\\hline\n   720 \\quad (\\text{80} \\times 9) \\\\\n+7200 \\quad (\\text{80} \\times 90) \\\\\n\\hline\n  7920 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 80 and 99 is \\(\\boxed{7920}\\).",
