      "text": "3240 plus 100 is 3340, then plus 8 is 3348. Wait, no, that can't be right. Wait, 3240 + 108. Let me check again. 3240 + 100 is 3340, and then +8 is 3348? Wait, but 3240 plus 108: 3240 + 100 is 3340, plus 8 is 3348. Hmm, okay, so that would be 3348?\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should do it the standard algorithm way. Let me try that to verify. Let me write 36 multiplied by 93.\n\nFirst, writing 36\nx 93\n------\nMultiply 36 by 3: that's 108.\nThen multiply 36 by 90 (since the 9 is in the tens place). So 36 times 9 is 324, and then add a zero to make it 3240.\nThen add the two results: 108 + 3240 = 3348. Okay, so that matches.\n\nWait, so that seems straightforward, but let me confirm once more just to be sure. Maybe another method like using the distributive property differently?\n\nAlternatively, 36 times 93 can be thought of as (30 + 6)(90 + 3). Expanding that:\n\n30*90 + 30*3 + 6*90 + 6*3.\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n30*90 = 2700,\n\n30*3 = 90,\n\n6*90 = 540,\n\n6*3 = 18.\n\nAdding them all together: 2700 + 90 is 2790, plus 540 is 3330, plus 18 is 3348. Yep, same result. Hmm, so that's consistent. So maybe that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using some other trick? Like 93 is close to 100. Wait, 93 is 100 -7. So 36*(100 -7) = 36*100 - 36*7 = 3600 - 252. 3600 - 252 is 3348. Yep, that works too. That's another way. So that gives the same answer again.\n\nOkay, so all the methods are leading to 3348. Therefore, I think that's correct. Let me just do a quick check with approximate values. 36 is 30 + 6, and 93 is about 90. So 36 times 90 is 3240, and 36 times 3 is 108. Adding those gives 3348. So, the approximate method also gives the same. Therefore, I think that's the right answer. I can't see any mistakes in my calculations here, so I think the product of 36 and 93 is 3348.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 36 and 93 is \\boxed{3348}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 36 and 93, we can use the distributive property and break it down into smaller parts:\n\n1. Calculate \\(36 \\times 90\\):\n   \\[\n   36 \\times 90 = 36 \\times (9 \\times 10) = (36 \\times 9) \\times 10 = 324 \\times 10 = 3240\n   \\]\n\n2. Calculate \\(36 \\times 3\\):\n   \\[\n   36 \\times 3 = 108\n   \\]\n\n3. Add the two results together:\n   \\[\n   3240 + 108 = 3348\n   \\]\n\nWe can verify this using another method by expanding \\((30 + 6)(90 + 3)\\):\n   \\[\n   30 \\times 90 + 30 \\times 3 + 6 \\times 90 + 6 \\times 3\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = 2700 + 90 + 540 + 18 = 2700 + 90 = 2790\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   2790 + 540 = 3330\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   3330 + 18 = 3348\n   \\]\n\nAnother verification using the approximation method:\n   \\[\n   36 \\times 100 - 36 \\times 7 = 3600 - 252 = 3348\n   \\]\n\nAll methods consistently give the result \\(3348\\).\n\nThe product of 36 and 93 is \\(\\boxed{3348}\\).",
