s, I think the product of 87 and 72 is indeed 6264. I don't see any errors in my calculations now. Probably that's the right answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 87 and 72 is \\boxed{6264}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 87 and 72, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n1. Write the numbers vertically aligned by place value:\n   ```\n    87\n  \u00d7 72\n  \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n  ```\n\n2. Multiply 87 by the rightmost digit of 72 (which is 2):\n   - \\(2 \\times 7 = 14\\) (write down 4, carry over 1)\n   - \\(2 \\times 8 = 16\\) plus the carried-over 1 gives 17\n   - Partial product: 174\n\n3. Multiply 87 by the next digit of 72 (which is 7, representing 70), shifting one place to the left:\n   - \\(7 \\times 7 = 49\\) (write down 9, carry over 4)\n   - \\(7 \\times 8 = 56\\) plus the carried-over 4 gives 60\n   - Partial product: 6090 (shifted one place left)\n\n4. Add the partial products:\n   ```\n    174\n  +6090\n  \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n   6264\n  ```\n\nVerification using the distributive property:\n- Break down the numbers: \\(87 \\times 72 = (80 + 7) \\times (70 + 2)\\)\n- Expand and calculate each term:\n  - \\(80 \\times 70 = 5600\\)\n  - \\(80 \\times 2 = 160\\)\n  - \\(7 \\times 70 = 490\\)\n  - \\(7 \\times 2 = 14\\)\n- Sum the terms: \\(5600 + 160 + 490 + 14 = 6264\\)\n\nAnother verification using the difference of squares method:\n- \\(87 \\times 72 = (90 - 3) \\times 72 = 90 \\times 72 - 3 \\times 72\\)\n- Calculate each term:\n  - \\(90 \\times 72 = 6480\\)\n  - \\(3 \\times 72 = 216\\)\n- Subtract: \\(6480 - 216 = 6264\\)\n\nAll methods confirm the product is \\(\\boxed{6264}\\).",
