 1400 +140 is indeed 1540. So the estimation checks out. \n\nAlternatively, if I approximate 35 as 30 and 44 as 40, then 30\u00d740=1200, which is a rough estimate, but the actual answer is 1540, which is higher, but since both numbers were rounded down, that makes sense. \n\nAnother way, maybe using the difference of squares? Wait, 35 and 44, not sure if that applies here. Let me see. 35 times 44. Hmm, 35 is 30 +5, 44 is 40 +4. Not sure if difference of squares would help here. Maybe not necessary.\n\nAlternatively, think of 44 as 11\u00d74. So 35\u00d711\u00d74. Let's see, 35\u00d711 is 385, since 35\u00d710 is 350 plus 35 is 385. Then 385\u00d74. 385\u00d72 is 770, so times 2 again is 1540. There we go. That's another method. So that works too.\n\nAlright, so all these different methods are leading me to the same answer, 1540, so I'm pretty confident that's correct. I don't think I made any calculation errors here. Let me just write down the steps again:\n\n35 \u00d7 44 = ?\n\nBreakdown:\n\nMethod 1:\n35 \u00d7 (40 +4) = 35\u00d740 + 35\u00d74 = 1400 +140 = 1540.\n\nMethod 2:\n(30 +5)(40 +4) = 30\u00d740 +30\u00d74 +5\u00d740 +5\u00d74 = 1200 +120 +200 +20 = 1540.\n\nMethod 3:\n35 \u00d7 44 = 35 \u00d7 (50 -6) = 35\u00d750 -35\u00d76 = 1750 -210 = 1540.\n\nMethod 4:\n35 \u00d744 = 35\u00d74\u00d711 = (35\u00d74)\u00d711 = 140\u00d711 = 1540. (Wait, actually 140\u00d711 is 1540, yes.)\n\nAlternatively, 35\u00d711=385, then 385\u00d74=1540. Yep.\n\nAll methods converge to 1540, so that must be the correct product. I think that's thorough enough. I can't think of any other way to check it, but since all methods give the same result, I'm confident.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 35 and 44 is calculated as follows:\n\n\\[\n35 \\times 44 = 35 \\times (40 + 4) = (35 \\times 40) + (35 \\times 4)\n\\]\n\nBreaking it down:\n- \\( 35 \\times 40 = 1400 \\)\n- \\( 35 \\times 4 = 140 \\)\n\nAdding the results:\n\\[\n1400 + 140 = 1540\n\\]\n\n**Final Answer:** \\boxed{1540}",
