      "text": "57 + 1710 = 1767. So 57\u00d731=1767? Let me verify that again to be sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's another way to compute it mentally. Let me try the distributive property. 57\u00d731 can be thought of as (50 +7)(30 +1). Expanding that, it would be 50\u00d730 + 50\u00d71 +7\u00d730 +7\u00d71. Let's calculate each part:\n\n50\u00d730 = 1500.\n\n50\u00d71 = 50.\n\n7\u00d730 = 210.\n\n7\u00d71 =7.\n\nAdding all these up: 1500 +50 is 1550. 1550 +210 is 1760. 1760 +7 is 1767. So that matches the previous result. So that's good.\n\nWait, but let me try another method just to be thorough. Maybe using some sort of trick or rounding. For example, 57 is close to 60, and 31 is close to 30. Let's see:\n\n57 \u00d7 31 = (60 - 3)(30 +1). Let's expand that: 60\u00d730 + 60\u00d71 -3\u00d730 -3\u00d71. Which is 1800 +60 -90 -3. That would be 1800 +60 is 1860, minus 90 is 1770, minus 3 is 1767. Yep, same answer again. So that's a third method, and still 1767.\n\nAlternatively, maybe do 57\u00d730 first, then add 57\u00d71. Since 31 is 30 +1. 57\u00d730: 57\u00d73\u00d710. 57\u00d73 is 171, so times 10 is 1710. Then add 57\u00d71=57. 1710 +57 is 1767. Yep, same result.\n\nHmm, so all methods are leading me to 1767. Let me check once with a calculator, but since I don't have a calculator here, maybe verify through division. Let's see if 1767 divided by 57 is 31.\n\nLet's do 1767 \u00f757.\n\nFirst, 57\u00d730 is 1710. 1767-1710=57. So that's 30 +1 =31. So yes, 57\u00d731 is indeed 1767. So that checks out.\n\nAlternatively, maybe see if 1767 divided by 31 is 57. Let's try that.\n\n31\u00d750=1550. 1767-1550=217. 31\u00d77=217. So 50+7=57. So that works too. Perfect.\n\nTherefore, I'm confident that the product of 57 and 31 is 1767. I think that's solid. All different methods cross-verify the same result, so I can't see any error here.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 57 and 31 is \\boxed{1767}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 57 and 31, we can use multiple methods to ensure accuracy:\n\n1. **Standard Algorithm**:\n   - Multiply 57 by 1 (the ones place of 31):\n     \\[\n     57 \\times 1 = 57\n     \\]\n   - Multiply 57 by 30 (the tens place of 31, represented by 3 in the tens place):\n     \\[\n     57 \\times 30 = 1710\n     \\]\n   - Add the results:\n     \\[\n     57 + 1710 = 1767\n     \\]\n\n2. **Distributive Property**:\n   - Expand the numbers using their place values:\n     \\[\n     (50 + 7)(30 + 1) = 50 \\times 30 + 50 \\times 1 + 7 \\times 30 + 7 \\times 1\n     \\]\n   - Calculate each term:\n     \\[\n     50 \\times 30 = 1500, \\quad 50 \\times 1 = 50, \\quad 7 \\times 30 = 210, \\quad 7 \\times 1 = 7\n     \\]\n   - Sum the terms:\n     \\[\n     1500 + 50 + 210 + 7 = 1767\n     \\]\n\n3. **Rounding and Adjusting**:\n   - Express 57 as \\(60 - 3\\) and 31 as \\(30 + 1\\):\n     \\[\n     (60 - 3)(30 + 1) = 60 \\times 30 + 60 \\times 1 - 3 \\times 30 - 3 \\times 1\n     \\]\n   - Calculate each term:\n     \\[\n     60 \\times 30 = 1800, \\quad 60 \\times 1 = 60, \\quad 3 \\times 30 = 90, \\quad 3 \\times 1 = 3\n     \\]\n   - Combine the terms:\n     \\[\n     1800 + 60 - 90 - 3 = 1767\n     \\]\n\n4. **Verification by Division**:\n   - Check if \\(1767 \\div 57 = 31\\):\n     \\[\n     57 \\times 30 = 1710 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 1767 - 1710 = 57 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad 30 + 1 = 31\n     \\]\n   - Check if \\(1767 \\div 31 = 57\\):\n     \\[\n     31 \\times 50 = 1550 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 1767 - 1550 = 217 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad 31 \\times 7 = 217 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad 50 + 7 = 57\n     \\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 57 and 31 is \\(\\boxed{1767}\\).",
