      "text": "1120 + 48 = 1168.\n\nWait a second, let me verify that. Maybe I should do the multiplication the standard way to check. Let me write it out vertically:\n\n\u2003\u00a073\n\u00d7\u00a016\n-------\nFirst multiply 73 by 6 (the units place of 16): 73 \u00d7 6. 70\u00d76 is 420, and 3\u00d76 is 18, so 420 +18 = 438.\n\nThen multiply 73 by 10 (since the 1 in 16 is in the tens place, so it's actually 10). So 73 \u00d7 10 is 730.\n\nNow add the two results: 438 + 730. Let me add them. 438 + 700 is 1138, then add 30 more to get 1168. Okay, that matches my previous answer. So that's good. \n\nAlternatively, maybe use another method like the lattice method? Hmm, not sure. But since both methods gave me 1168, I think that's correct. Let me try one more way just to be thorough. \n\nBreaking down 16 into 10 and 6. So 10\u00d773 is 730, and 6\u00d773 is 438. Then adding them: 730 + 438. 700+400 is 1100, 30+38 is 68, so total is 1168. Yep, same result. \n\nAlright, so I think 1168 is the right answer. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations. All different methods give the same result. So I'm confident that's the product of 16 and 73.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 16 and 73 is calculated as follows:\n\n1. **Breakdown using distributive property**:  \n   \\( 16 \\times 73 = 16 \\times (70 + 3) \\)  \n   \\( = (16 \\times 70) + (16 \\times 3) \\)  \n   \\( = 1120 + 48 \\)  \n   \\( = 1168 \\).\n\n2. **Verification via standard multiplication**:  \n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n     \\quad 73 \\\\\n   \\times 16 \\\\\n   \\hline\n     438 \\quad (\\text{73 \u00d7 6}) \\\\\n   +730 \\quad (\\text{73 \u00d7 10, shifted left}) \\\\\n   \\hline\n     1168 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\nBoth methods confirm the result is **1168**.\n\n**Answer:**  \n\\boxed{1168}",
