      "text": "6200 - 186 = 6014? Wait, wait, no. Let me check that again. .\n\nWait, 6200 minus 186. Let me break it down differently. 6200 minus 200 would be 5800, but since I'm only subtracting 186, which is 14 less than 200, I need to add back 14. So 5800 + 14 is 5814? Wait, that contradicts my previous result. Hmm, so I must have messed up somewhere. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should do the subtraction step by step. Let's see:\n\nStarting with 6200 - 186.\n\nTake away 100 from 6200: 6100.\n\nTake away 80 from 6100: 6100 - 80 = 6020.\n\nThen take away 6 more: 6020 - 6 = 6014. Wait, okay, so that gives me 6014. But when I did the other way, I thought of 6200 - 200 = 5800, then add back 14, which would be 5814, but that's different. So why the discrepancy?\n\nAh, I think I made a mistake in the second method. Let's see:\n\nIf I subtract 200 instead of 186, that's an over-subtraction by 14 (since 200 - 186 = 14). Therefore, to correct it, I need to add back 14. So 6200 - 200 = 6000? Wait, no, 6200 minus 200 is 6000? Wait, no, 6200 minus 200 is actually 6000? Wait, no, 6200 minus 200 would be 6000? Wait, 6200 minus 200 is 6000? Wait, that can't be. 6200 minus 200 is 6000? Wait, 6200 minus 200 is 6000? Let me check:\n\nYes, because 6200 minus 200 is 6200 - 200 = 6000. But if I wanted to subtract 186, which is 14 less than 200, so I subtracted 200 and have to add back 14. So 6000 + 14 is 6014. Okay, so that matches the first method. I must have miscalculated earlier when I thought 6200 - 200 was 5800. That was a mistake. So the correct answer here is 6014.\n\nWait, but let me confirm with another method to be sure. Maybe do the standard multiplication.\n\nMultiplying 62 by 97 the traditional way:\n\nFirst, write 62  \n          x97  \n       --------\n          434  (which is 62 x 7)  \n         5580  (which is 62 x 90, shifted one position to the left)  \n       --------\n         6014\n\nLet me verify each step:\n\n62 x 7: 60x7=420, 2x7=14, so 420+14=434. That's correct.\n\n62 x 90: Well, 62 x 9 = 558, so 62 x 90 is 5580. Then, adding the two:\n\n434 + 5580 = 6014. Okay, so that matches. So the answer is 6014.\n\nAlternatively, maybe another way to compute. Let's see, 62 x 97. Hmm, perhaps using some other properties?\n\nAlternatively, maybe (60 + 2)(100 - 3) and expanding that. Wait, that's similar to the first method I used. Let me try again:\n\n(60 + 2)(100 - 3) = 60*100 + 60*(-3) + 2*100 + 2*(-3)\n\n= 6000 - 180 + 200 - 6\n\n= 6000 - 180 is 5820,\n\n5820 + 200 is 6020,\n\n6020 -6 is 6014. Yep, same result.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using another trick. For example, 62 x 97 is the same as (60 x 97) + (2 x 97). Let me try that.\n\n60 x 97: 60 x 100 is 6000, minus 60 x 3 is 180, so 6000 - 180 = 5820.\n\n2 x 97 = 194.\n\nAdding those together: 5820 + 194. Let's see, 5820 + 100 is 5920, plus 94 is 6014. Yep, same answer.\n\nHmm, so all methods lead to 6014. So I think that's correct. But maybe I should check with a calculator, but since I don't have one, let me do another verification.\n\nLet me reverse the multiplication: 97 x 62. Let's compute that.\n\n97 x 60 = 5820, 97 x 2 = 194, so 5820 + 194 = 6014. Same result.\n\nAlternatively, adding 62 ninety-seven times, but that would be tedious, but maybe adding in chunks.\n\nAlternatively, let me check if 6014 divided by 62 is 97.\n\nLet me do 6014 \u00f7 62.\n\nFirst, 62 x 90 = 5580.\n\n6014 - 5580 = 434.\n\nThen 434 \u00f7 62 is 7, because 62 x7 = 434.\n\nSo total is 90 +7 = 97. Correct. So that checks out.\n\nAlternatively, 6014 divided by 97 should be 62.\n\nLet me try: 97 x 60 = 5820. Subtract that from 6014: 6014 - 5820 = 194. Then 194 \u00f797 is 2. So total is 60 +2 =62. Correct.\n\nTherefore, the answer is 6014. I think that's solid. I used multiple methods to verify. So I'm confident that's the correct product of 62 and 97.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 62 and 97 is \\boxed{6014}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 62 and 97, we can use the distributive property by expressing 97 as \\(100 - 3\\):\n\n\\[\n62 \\times 97 = 62 \\times (100 - 3)\n\\]\n\nApplying the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n62 \\times 100 - 62 \\times 3\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term separately:\n\n\\[\n62 \\times 100 = 6200\n\\]\n\\[\n62 \\times 3 = 186\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the second result from the first:\n\n\\[\n6200 - 186 = 6014\n\\]\n\nTo verify, we can use the standard multiplication method:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   \\quad 62 \\\\\n\\times 97 \\\\\n\\hline\n   434 \\quad (\\text{62} \\times 7) \\\\\n+5580 \\quad (\\text{62} \\times 90) \\\\\n\\hline\n   6014 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product is indeed 6014. Additionally, verifying by division:\n\n- \\(6014 \\div 62 = 97\\)\n- \\(6014 \\div 97 = 62\\)\n\nThus, the product of 62 and 97 is \\(\\boxed{6014}\\).",
