      "text": "5520 + 24. Wait, hold on, did I add that correctly? Let me check again.\n\nWait, 4800 + 480 is indeed 5280. Then adding 240 gives 5280 + 240 = 5520. Then adding the last 24 would make it 5544. Oh, I almost messed that up. So the total is 5544. Hmm, let me confirm that with another method to make sure I didn't make an arithmetic error.\n\nAnother way to approach this could be using the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's write it out vertically:\n\n\u2003\u200384  \n\u00d7\u202f66  \n________  \nFirst, multiply 84 by 6 (the units digit of 66). 84 \u00d7 6 = 504. Then, multiply 84 by 60 (the tens digit), so 84 \u00d7 60 = 5040. Adding those two results together: 504 + 5040 = 5544. Okay, that matches the first method. That's reassuring.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the difference of squares or another algebraic identity. Let's see, 84 and 66. Hmm, 84 is 70 + 14, and 66 is 70 - 4. Not sure if that helps. Alternatively, maybe express them as (75 + 9) and (75 - 9), but wait, 75 squared minus 81 would be 5625 - 81 = 5544. Wait, that works!\n\nWait, let me check that again. If I can write 84 and 66 as numbers around 75. Let me see: 84 is 75 + 9, and 66 is 75 - 9? Wait, no, 75 - 9 is 66, but 75 + 9 is 84. Exactly! So (a + b)(a - b) = a\u00b2 - b\u00b2. So here, a is 75 and b is 9. Therefore, 84 \u00d7 66 = (75 + 9)(75 - 9) = 75\u00b2 - 9\u00b2. \n\nCalculating that: 75 squared is 5625, and 9 squared is 81. Subtracting them gives 5625 - 81 = 5544. Perfect! So that's another way and it gives the same result. So that confirms it again. \n\nAlternatively, maybe using some other method like the Russian peasant multiplication? Although that might take longer, but let me try. \n\nRussian peasant multiplication involves doubling and halving. Let's see, take 84 and 66. \n\nStart with two columns:\n\n84 | 66\n\nWe need to halve the left number and double the right number until the left is 1.\n\nHalve 84: 42, double 66: 132\n\n42 | 132\n\nHalve 42: 21, double 132: 264\n\n21 | 264\n\nHalve 21: 10.5, but we can only use integers, so maybe we stop when it's odd? Wait, this might get complicated. Alternatively, continuing:\n\nHalve 21: 10.5, but since it's a fraction, maybe discard the fraction and keep track of the remainders? Hmm, perhaps this is getting too involved. Let me try again properly.\n\nThe method is: repeatedly halve the left number, ignoring remainders, and double the right number until the left is 1. Then add up the numbers in the right column which correspond to even steps? Wait, maybe I need to recall the exact steps.\n\nAlternatively, maybe this is taking too much time. Since we already have two methods giving the same answer, maybe that's enough. But just to be thorough, let me try another approach.\n\nAnother way is to note that 84 \u00d7 66 = 84 \u00d7 (70 - 4) = 84\u00d770 - 84\u00d74. Calculating each part:\n\n84 \u00d770: 84 \u00d77 = 588, so times 10 is 5880. \n\n84 \u00d74 = 336. \n\nSubtracting gives 5880 - 336 = 5544. \n\nAgain the same answer. \n\nAlternatively, breaking down into prime factors?\n\n84 factors: 2\u00d72\u00d73\u00d77 (since 84 = 12\u00d77 = 2\u00d72\u00d73\u00d77)\n\n66 factors: 2\u00d73\u00d711 (since 66 = 6\u00d711 = 2\u00d73\u00d711)\n\nSo multiplying together: 2\u00d72\u00d73\u00d77 \u00d7 2\u00d73\u00d711. Combine like terms: 2^3 \u00d7 3^2 \u00d77 \u00d711.\n\nCalculating step by step:\n\n2^3 = 8, 3^2 =9. So 8\u00d79 =72. Then 72\u00d77 = 504. Then 504 \u00d711. 504\u00d710=5040 + 504 = 5544. \n\nYep, that works too. \n\nSo all methods lead to 5544. Therefore, I can be confident that the product of 84 and 66 is 5544.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 84 and 66 is calculated as follows:\n\n**Method 1: Distributive Property**  \nBreak down the numbers:  \n\\[\n(80 + 4) \\times (60 + 6) = 80 \\times 60 + 80 \\times 6 + 4 \\times 60 + 4 \\times 6\n\\]  \nCalculate each term:  \n\\[\n4800 + 480 + 240 + 24 = 5544\n\\]\n\n**Method 2: Standard Multiplication**  \n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   84 \\\\\n\\times 66 \\\\\n\\hline\n   504 \\quad (\\text{84 \u00d7 6}) \\\\\n+5040 \\quad (\\text{84 \u00d7 60}) \\\\\n\\hline\n  5544 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\n**Method 3: Difference of Squares**  \nExpress as \\((75 + 9)(75 - 9)\\):  \n\\[\n75^2 - 9^2 = 5625 - 81 = 5544\n\\]\n\n**Method 4: Prime Factorization**  \nFactorize and multiply:  \n\\[\n84 = 2^2 \\times 3 \\times 7, \\quad 66 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 11 \\\\\n\\text{Product} = 2^3 \\times 3^2 \\times 7 \\times 11 = 8 \\times 9 \\times 7 \\times 11 = 5544\n\\]\n\n**Final Answer:**  \n\\boxed{5544}",
