  55\nx 63\n-------\nFirst, 3*55: 3*5=15, write down 5, carryover 1. 3*5=15 +1=16. So 165.\n\nThen, 6 (tens place) so when we do 6*55, it's actually 60*55. \n\n6*5=30, write down 0, carryover 3. \n\n6*5=30 +3=33. So that gives 330. But since it's the tens place, shift left, so 3300. \n\nAdding 165 +3300 = 3465. \n\nYep, same as before. \n\nAlternatively, maybe cross-multiplying in a different order. Let me see:\n\n55 *63 = (50 +5)(60 +3) as before, which we did. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps breaking down 63 as 70-7? Let me try that. \n\n55*(70-7) = 55*70 -55*7. \n\n55*70: 55*7=385, so times 10 is 3850,\n\n55*7: 385,\n\nSo 3850 - 385 = 3465. \n\nAgain the same answer. \n\nAlright, so after trying multiple methods and cross-verifying, I can be confident that the product of 55 and 63 is 3465. \n\nWait, but just to ensure I didn't make a calculation mistake in any step. Let me verify one of the methods again. Let's take the first one, the standard algorithm:\n\n55 x 63.\n\nFirst partial product: 3*55 = 165. \n\nSecond partial product: 60*55. 55*60. Well, 55*6 is 330, so 55*60 is 3300. Adding those gives 165+3300= 3465. Yep.\n\nAnother way, 55*63. Let me do it in my head step by step. 50*60 is 3000, 50*3 is 150, 5*60 is 300, 5*3 is 15. Adding those: 3000+150=3150, +300 is 3450, +15 is 3465. \n\nYes, that works. \n\nAlternatively, thinking of 63 as 6*10 + 3, so 55*6*10 +55*3. 55*6 is 330, times 10 is 3300, plus 165 is 3465. \n\nAll paths lead to the same result, so I think that's solid. \n\nTherefore, the answer should be 3465.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 55 and 63 is calculated as follows:\n\nFirst, multiply 55 by 3:\n\\[\n55 \\times 3 = 165\n\\]\n\nNext, multiply 55 by 60 (since 63 = 60 + 3):\n\\[\n55 \\times 60 = 3,\\!300\n\\]\n\nAdd the two results together:\n\\[\n165 + 3,\\!300 = 3,\\!465\n\\]\n\n**Answer:** \\boxed{3465}",
