6\u00d728, but shifted one position to the left. So 6\u00d728=168, shifted left one digit becomes 1680. So that's the same as multiplying by 60. \n\nI think all these methods are consistent. So I feel confident that 60 multiplied by 28 is 1680. I can't think of any other way to verify it, but since all methods give the same result, it must be correct.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 60 and 28 is calculated as follows:\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n1. **Break down the multiplication** using the distributive property:\n   \\[\n   60 \\times 28 = 60 \\times (20 + 8)\n   \\]\n2. **Multiply separately**:\n   \\[\n   60 \\times 20 = 1200 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 60 \\times 8 = 480\n   \\]\n3. **Add the results**:\n   \\[\n   1200 + 480 = 1680\n   \\]\n\n**Final Answer:**  \nThe product of 60 and 28 is **1680**.",
