hat's another way: 40 times 44 is 1760, plus 1 times 44 is 44, so total 1760+44=1804. \n\nYes, that works too. So breaking down 41 as 40 +1, then multiplying each by 44. \n\nTherefore, all methods converge to 1804, so I'm confident that's the correct product of 41 and 44.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 41 and 44 is \\boxed{1804}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 41 and 44, we can use the distributive property and break down the multiplication into manageable parts:\n\n1. Multiply 41 by 4 (the ones place of 44):\n   \\[\n   41 \\times 4 = 164\n   \\]\n\n2. Multiply 41 by 40 (the tens place of 44, which is equivalent to 40):\n   \\[\n   41 \\times 40 = 1640\n   \\]\n\n3. Add the results of the two multiplications:\n   \\[\n   164 + 1640 = 1804\n   \\]\n\nWe can verify this by expanding the product using the distributive property:\n\\[\n(40 + 1)(40 + 4) = 40 \\times 40 + 40 \\times 4 + 1 \\times 40 + 1 \\times 4\n\\]\n\\[\n= 1600 + 160 + 40 + 4 = 1804\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 41 and 44 is \\(\\boxed{1804}\\).",
