or the tens place gives 4320. Then adding 216 + 4320 gives 4536. That seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can check using a calculator, but since I can't use one, I'll just verify with another method. Let's see, 72*63 is equal to (70 + 2)*(60 +3). Expanding that:\n\n70*60 + 70*3 + 2*60 + 2*3\n\nWhich is 4200 + 210 + 120 + 6.\n\nAdding those together:\n\n4200 + 210 = 4410\n\n4410 + 120 = 4530\n\n4530 +6 = 4536. Yep, same answer again. So that's another way to break it down and still get 4536.\n\nTherefore, I'm pretty confident the product of 72 and 63 is 4536.\n\nWait, just another sanity check: 70 times 60 is 4200, which is less than the actual product. Since 72 is 2 more than 70 and 63 is 3 more than 60, so the actual product should be a bit higher. 4200 + (extra from 70*3 + 2*60 + 2*3). Which as above adds up to 4536. Seems reasonable.\n\nAlternatively, if I approximate 70*60 = 4200, 70*63 would be 4410, and then adding 2*63=126, so 4410 +126= 4536. Exactly. So that works too.\n\nAlright, so after multiple methods and checks, I believe the answer is 4536. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations, so I think that's correct.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 72 and 63 is \\boxed{4536}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 72 and 63, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n1. Multiply 72 by 3 (the ones place of 63):\n   \\[\n   72 \\times 3 = 216\n   \\]\n\n2. Multiply 72 by 60 (the tens place of 63, which is equivalent to 60):\n   \\[\n   72 \\times 60 = 4320\n   \\]\n\n3. Add the results of the two partial products:\n   \\[\n   216 + 4320 = 4536\n   \\]\n\nWe can verify this using the distributive property:\n- Break down 63 into 60 and 3:\n  \\[\n  72 \\times 63 = 72 \\times (60 + 3) = 72 \\times 60 + 72 \\times 3\n  \\]\n- Calculate each part:\n  \\[\n  72 \\times 60 = 4320 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 72 \\times 3 = 216\n  \\]\n- Sum the results:\n  \\[\n  4320 + 216 = 4536\n  \\]\n\nAnother verification using the expanded form:\n- Expand both numbers:\n  \\[\n  (70 + 2) \\times (60 + 3) = 70 \\times 60 + 70 \\times 3 + 2 \\times 60 + 2 \\times 3\n  \\]\n- Calculate each term:\n  \\[\n  70 \\times 60 = 4200, \\quad 70 \\times 3 = 210, \\quad 2 \\times 60 = 120, \\quad 2 \\times 3 = 6\n  \\]\n- Sum the terms:\n  \\[\n  4200 + 210 + 120 + 6 = 4536\n  \\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 72 and 63 is \\(\\boxed{4536}\\).",
