      "text": "720 +72 = 792. Wait, let me check that addition again. 720 plus 70 is 790, and then plus 2 more is 792. Yeah, that's right. \n\nAlternatively, I could do the standard multiplication algorithm. Let me try that way to confirm. \n\nMultiplying 36 by 22. \n\nFirst, write 36  \ntimes 22  \n________  \nMultiply 36 by 2 (the units place of 22): 36*2 =72.  \n\nThen, multiply 36 by 20 (the tens place, so we shift one position to the left): 36*20 =720.  \n\nAdd them together: 72 + 720 =792. Yep, same result.  \n\nWait another way: Maybe using the fact that 22 is 20 +2, so 36*(20+2)=36*20 +36*2 as before.  \n\nAlternatively, maybe think of 36*22 as (30+6)*(20+2). Let me expand that. \n\n30*20 +30*2 +6*20 +6*2. \n\nCalculating each term: 30*20 is 600, 30*2 is 60, 6*20 is 120, and 6*2 is 12. Adding those together: 600+60=660, 660+120=780, 780+12=792. \n\nSame answer again. So that seems consistent. \n\nHmm, maybe do it another way. Let me see, 22 is double of 11, so 36*11 is 396, so doubling that would be 792. Wait, is that right? 36*11, since 11 times a two-digit number is the number with the sum of its digits in the middle. So 36*11 is 3 (3+6)=9 6, so 396. Then doubling that gives 792. Yeah, that works too. \n\nAlternatively, maybe using exponents? Not sure, but seems like overcomplicating. \n\nAlternatively, I can use a calculator, but since I'm doing this manually, the methods above all lead to 792, so I think that's the correct answer. \n\nWait, just to be thorough, let me do the multiplication step by step again. \n\n36  \nx22  \n------  \nFirst, multiply 36 by 2 (the ones place of the multiplier):  \n6*2=12, write down 2 and carry over 1.  \n3*2=6 plus the carried 1 is 7. So that gives 72.  \n\nThen, multiply 36 by the second digit, which is the 2 in the tens place, so actually 20. So we need to shift one position to the left.  \n\n36*2 (tens place, so 20):  \n6*2=12, write 2, carry over 1.  \n3*2=6 plus 1 is 7. So that gives 72, but shifted one place to the left, so 720.  \n\nAdding 72 + 720: 72+720=792.  \n\nYep, all methods confirm that it's 792. I think that's solid. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations. So the product of 36 and 22 is 792.  \n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 36 and 22 is \\boxed{792}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 36 and 22, we can use the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n36 \\times 22 = 36 \\times (20 + 2)\n\\]\n\nBreaking it down:\n\n1. Calculate \\(36 \\times 20\\):\n   \\[\n   36 \\times 20 = 720\n   \\]\n\n2. Calculate \\(36 \\times 2\\):\n   \\[\n   36 \\times 2 = 72\n   \\]\n\n3. Add the results of the two calculations:\n   \\[\n   720 + 72 = 792\n   \\]\n\nWe can verify this using the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n1. Multiply 36 by 2 (the units place of 22):\n   \\[\n   36 \\times 2 = 72\n   \\]\n\n2. Multiply 36 by 20 (the tens place of 22, shifting one position to the left):\n   \\[\n   36 \\times 20 = 720\n   \\]\n\n3. Add the results:\n   \\[\n   72 + 720 = 792\n   \\]\n\nAnother verification using the expanded form \\((30 + 6) \\times (20 + 2)\\):\n\n1. Expand the terms:\n   \\[\n   30 \\times 20 + 30 \\times 2 + 6 \\times 20 + 6 \\times 2\n   \\]\n\n2. Calculate each term:\n   \\[\n   30 \\times 20 = 600, \\quad 30 \\times 2 = 60, \\quad 6 \\times 20 = 120, \\quad 6 \\times 2 = 12\n   \\]\n\n3. Sum the results:\n   \\[\n   600 + 60 + 120 + 12 = 792\n   \\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 36 and 22 is \\(\\boxed{792}\\).",
