, that's solid. \n\nI think I'm confident that the product is 780. I don't see any errors in my calculations, and multiple methods confirm it. So I guess that's the answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 65 and 12 is \\boxed{780}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 65 and 12, we can use several methods to ensure accuracy:\n\n1. **Breaking Down the Numbers**:\n   - Calculate \\(65 \\times 10 = 650\\)\n   - Calculate \\(65 \\times 2 = 130\\)\n   - Add the results: \\(650 + 130 = 780\\)\n\n2. **Standard Vertical Multiplication**:\n   - Multiply 65 by 2: \\(65 \\times 2 = 130\\)\n   - Multiply 65 by 10 (shifted one position to the left): \\(65 \\times 10 = 650\\)\n   - Add the results: \n     \\[\n     \\begin{array}{r}\n      130 \\\\\n     +650 \\\\\n     \\hline\n      780 \\\\\n     \\end{array}\n     \\]\n\n3. **Distributive Property**:\n   - Break down 65 into 60 and 5:\n     - \\(60 \\times 12 = 720\\)\n     - \\(5 \\times 12 = 60\\)\n   - Add the results: \\(720 + 60 = 780\\)\n\n4. **Expanding Both Numbers**:\n   - Use the expansion \\((60 + 5) \\times (10 + 2)\\):\n     - \\(60 \\times 10 = 600\\)\n     - \\(60 \\times 2 = 120\\)\n     - \\(5 \\times 10 = 50\\)\n     - \\(5 \\times 2 = 10\\)\n   - Add all terms: \\(600 + 120 + 50 + 10 = 780\\)\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 65 and 12 is \\(\\boxed{780}\\).",
