      "index": 0,
      "text": "600 + 12 = 612. That seems straightforward. .\n\nLet me check using the vertical multiplication method again to verify. .\n\nStarting over:\n\n   12  \nx 51  \n------  \nFirst multiply 12 by 1 (units place):  \n12*1 = 12.  \n\nThen multiply 12 by 5 (tens place, so actually 50).  \n12*5 = 60. But since it's the tens place, we write it shifted one position to the left:  \n\nSo,\n\n   12  \nx 51  \n------  \n   12   (this is 12*1)  \n 600   (this is 12*50, written shifted left by one)  \n------  \nAdding those two results together: 12 + 600 = 612.  \n\nOkay, that matches the distributive method. Maybe I confused myself earlier when trying to do the multiplication step by step, but breaking it down into parts helped. .\n\nAlternatively, I could also use another method, like the lattice method, but I think for now the standard way is sufficient. Let me just confirm with a calculator in my head. Wait, 12 times 50 is 600, plus another 12 is 612. Yeah, that seems right. .\n\nWait another thought: 51 times 10 is 510, and 51 times 2 is 102, so adding those together: 510 + 102 is 612. Yep, that also works. So, that's another way to verify. .\n\nAlternatively, breaking down both numbers into prime factors? Not sure if that's easier here. Let me try. 12 is 2*2*3, and 51 is 3*17. Then multiplying them together: (2^2 * 3) * (3 *17) = 2^2 * 3^2 *17. Calculating that would be 4*9*17. 4*9 is 36, then 36*17. Hmm, 36*17 is (30*17)+(6*17)= 510 + 102= 612 again. So that also works. .\n\nSo, all methods lead to 612. Therefore, I think that's the correct product. I can't think of any mistakes here. The key was breaking it down into parts and checking with different methods. Sometimes when I do multiplication vertically, I might forget to shift the second line, but since I double-checked with other methods, I can be more confident. .\n\nAnother way to check is by estimation. 12 times 50 is 600, so 12 times 51 should be just a bit more, which 612 is. So that makes sense. 12 more than 600 is exactly 612, so that fits. .\n\nAlright, I think that's thorough enough. The answer is 612.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 12 and 51 is calculated as follows:\n\nUsing the distributive property:\n\\[\n12 \\times 51 = 12 \\times (50 + 1) = (12 \\times 50) + (12 \\times 1) = 600 + 12 = 612\n\\]\n\nVerification via vertical multiplication:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   12 \\\\\n\\times 51 \\\\\n\\hline\n   12 \\quad (\\text{12 \u00d7 1}) \\\\\n+ 600 \\quad (\\text{12 \u00d7 50, shifted left}) \\\\\n\\hline\n   612 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is \\(\\boxed{612}\\).",
      "logprobs": null,
