      "text": "3300 + 66 = 3366. Wait, let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do it another way to confirm. Let's see, 51 multiplied by 66. Another way to break it down could be to think of 66 as 60 + 6. So 51 times 60 plus 51 times 6. \n\nCalculating 51 times 60 first. 50 times 60 is 3000, and 1 times 60 is 60, so that gives 3000 + 60 = 3060. Then, 51 times 6. 50 times 6 is 300 and 1 times 6 is 6, so 300 + 6 = 306. Adding those two results together: 3060 + 306. \n\n3060 plus 300 is 3360, then plus 6 more is 3366. Okay, that matches my previous result. That's a good sign. \n\nAlternatively, maybe using the standard multiplication algorithm. Let me write it down vertically:\n\n\u200351  \n\u00d766  \n------  \nFirst multiply 51 by 6: 51\u00d76. 1\u00d76 is 6, 5\u00d76 is 30, so that's 306.  \nThen multiply 51 by 60 (since the 6 in the tens place is worth 60). So 51\u00d760.  \n51\u00d76 is 306, so adding a zero gives 3060.  \nAdding 306 and 3060 together: 306 + 3060 = 3366. Yep, same answer.  \n\nI think that's correct. Let me just confirm with a calculator-like approach. 51 times 66. Let's do 50 times 66 first, which is 3300, then add 1 times 66, so 3300 + 66 = 3366. Yep, all methods give the same result. I think that's solid. \n\nWait another thought: 51 times 66. Maybe using the fact that 51 is 50 +1 and 66 is 60 +6. But that might complicate more. Alternatively, maybe recognize that 51\u00d766 is the same as (50+1)(60+6). Expanding this would give 50\u00d760 + 50\u00d76 +1\u00d760 +1\u00d76. \n\nCalculating each term:\n50\u00d760 = 3000\n50\u00d76 = 300\n1\u00d760 = 60\n1\u00d76 = 6\nAdding all those together: 3000 + 300 = 3300; 3300 +60 = 3360; 3360 +6 = 3366. \n\nSame answer again. Okay, so all different methods confirm that the product is 3366. I think that's correct. I can't see any mistakes in my calculations. Let me just do straight multiplication once again to be thorough. \n\n51  \n\u00d766  \nMultiply 6 (units place) by 51: 6\u00d71=6, 6\u00d75=30. So 306.  \nMultiply 6 (tens place, so it's actually 60) by 51: 6\u00d751=306, but shifted one position to the left, so 3060.  \nAdding 306 + 3060 gives 3366. Yep, same result. \n\nAlright, I think that's solid. The answer is 3366.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 51 and 66 can be calculated using the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n51 \\times 66 = (50 + 1) \\times 66 = 50 \\times 66 + 1 \\times 66\n\\]\n\nFirst, compute \\(50 \\times 66\\):\n\\[\n50 \\times 60 = 3000 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 50 \\times 6 = 300 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad 3000 + 300 = 3300\n\\]\n\nNext, compute \\(1 \\times 66\\):\n\\[\n1 \\times 66 = 66\n\\]\n\nAdd the results together:\n\\[\n3300 + 66 = 3366\n\\]\n\n**Verification via standard multiplication:**\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n\\quad \\ 51 \\\\\n\\times 66 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\quad 306 \\quad (\\text{51 \u00d7 6}) \\\\\n+ 3060 \\quad (\\text{51 \u00d7 60, shifted left by one digit}) \\\\\n\\hline\n3366 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm the result. \n\n**Answer:** \\(\\boxed{3366}\\)",
