nother mathematical approach. For instance, using the fact that (a)(b) = [(a + b)/2]^2 - [(a - b)/2]^2. But that might complicate more. Let's see:\n\nLet a =99, b=31. Then [(99+31)/2]^2 - [(99-31)/2]^2.\n\nCompute (130/2)^2 - (68/2)^2 = 65\u00b2 - 34\u00b2.\n\n65 squared is 4225. 34 squared is 1156. Then 4225 - 1156 = 3069. Wow, same result. That's a good check. \n\nSo that formula works here. Therefore, I'm pretty confident the answer is 3069. \n\nAlternatively, maybe divide 99 by 31 and see if the multiplication holds? Wait, not sure. Alternatively, use a calculator? But since I can't, but given that multiple methods give the same answer, I think it's safe. \n\nWait, another check: 30 times 100 is 3000, so 30\u00d799 would be 2970, which is 30 less than 3000. But that's not directly helpful. Alternatively, 31\u00d7100 is 3100, subtract 31 gives 3069, so that's consistent again. \n\nHmm, I think I've verified it sufficiently. So the product of 99 and 31 is 3069.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 99 and 31 is \\boxed{3069}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 99 and 31, we can use the distributive property by recognizing that 99 is 100 - 1. \n\nFirst, we rewrite the problem using the distributive property:\n\\[\n99 \\times 31 = (100 - 1) \\times 31\n\\]\n\nApplying the distributive property:\n\\[\n(100 - 1) \\times 31 = 100 \\times 31 - 1 \\times 31\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term separately:\n\\[\n100 \\times 31 = 3100\n\\]\n\\[\n1 \\times 31 = 31\n\\]\n\nSubtracting these results:\n\\[\n3100 - 31 = 3069\n\\]\n\nTo verify, we can also use the standard multiplication method:\n\\[\n99 \\times 31 = 99 \\times (30 + 1) = 99 \\times 30 + 99 \\times 1\n\\]\n\\[\n99 \\times 30 = 2970\n\\]\n\\[\n99 \\times 1 = 99\n\\]\n\\[\n2970 + 99 = 3069\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 99 and 31 is \\(\\boxed{3069}\\).",
