*3, which is exactly what I did earlier. \n\nHmm, seems consistent. Alternatively, maybe use a calculator? But since this is a problem-solving exercise, I should do it manually. \n\nWait, another way: 51 times 73. Let me think of 51 as 50 +1. So 50*73 +1*73. 50*73 is half of 100*73, which is 7300, so half of that is 3650. Then add 73, so 3650 +73 is 3723. Yep, same answer again. \n\nAlternatively, 73 times 50 is 3650, plus 73 more. 3650 +73: 3650 + 70 is 3720, plus 3 is 3723. \n\nHmm, so all methods lead me to 3723. Therefore, I think that is the correct answer. \n\nWait, just to be absolutely sure, maybe I can do it in another way. Let me try the cross-multiplication method. \n\n51 \u00d7 73:\n\nBreak down into (50 + 1)(70 +3). Wait, that's the same as before. Alternatively, maybe do 51 \u00d773 as (51 \u00d770) + (51 \u00d73). Which is exactly what I did earlier. \n\nAlternatively, 73 \u00d751. Let me switch the numbers. 73 times 50 is 3650, plus 73 times 1 is 73. So total 3650 +73 is 3723. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps using the standard vertical method again but writing it the other way:\n\n   73\nx 51\n-----\n?\n\nMultiply 73 by 1: 73. Multiply 73 by 50: 73\u00d750 = 3650. Then add them: 73 +3650 = 3723. Same result. \n\nHmm, so I think that's right. I don't see any discrepancies in the calculations. \n\nAlternatively, maybe check using estimation. 50 times 70 is 3500, and 50 times 73 would be 3650, and 51 is a bit more, so 51\u00d773 should be a bit more than 3650, which 3723 is. \n\nAlternatively, 50\u00d770 is 3500, 50\u00d73 is 150, 1\u00d770 is 70, 1\u00d73 is 3. All those added together: 3500+150=3650, +70=3720, +3=3723. \n\nSo, after multiple checks, I believe the product of 51 and 73 is 3723. I don't see any errors in my calculations, so I think that's the correct answer. \n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 51 and 73 is \\boxed{3723}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 51 and 73, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm and the distributive property for verification.\n\nFirst, using the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   51 \\\\\n\\times 73 \\\\\n\\hline\n  153 \\quad \\text{(51 multiplied by 3)} \\\\\n+3570 \\quad \\text{(51 multiplied by 70, shifted one position to the left)} \\\\\n\\hline\n  3723 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nNext, using the distributive property:\n\\[\n(50 + 1)(70 + 3) = 50 \\times 70 + 50 \\times 3 + 1 \\times 70 + 1 \\times 3\n\\]\nCalculating each term:\n\\[\n50 \\times 70 = 3500\n\\]\n\\[\n50 \\times 3 = 150\n\\]\n\\[\n1 \\times 70 = 70\n\\]\n\\[\n1 \\times 3 = 3\n\\]\nAdding all the terms together:\n\\[\n3500 + 150 + 70 + 3 = 3723\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 51 and 73 is \\(\\boxed{3723}\\).",
