ing 80 into 80 and 0 isn't helpful. Alternatively, split 39 into 30+9. So, 80*30 +80*9. 80*30 is 2400, and 80*9 is 720. Again, adding those gives 2400+720=3120. \n\nHmm, so all these different methods are giving me the same answer, 3120, so I think that's correct. \n\nWait, but just to make sure I didn't make a mistake in any of the steps. Let me check each calculation again. \n\nFirst method: 8*39. 8*40 is 320, minus 8 gives 312. 312*10 is 3120. That seems right. \n\nSecond method: 80*39. The standard multiplication:\n\n   80\nx 39\n--------\n  720  (which is 80*9)\n+2400  (which is 80*30, shifted one position to the left)\n--------\n 3120\n\nYes, adding 720 and 2400 gives 3120. \n\nThird method: distributive property with 80*(40-1) = 3200-80=3120. \n\nFourth method: breaking 39 into 30+9, so 80*30=2400, 80*9=720, total 3120. \n\nAll methods confirm the same result. \n\nAlternatively, maybe do the multiplication vertically again step by step. Let me write it out again:\n\n   80\nx 39\n--------\nFirst multiply 80 by 9: \n\n9 * 0 = 0, write down 0\n9 * 8 =72, write down 72 next to it, so 720.\n\nThen multiply 80 by 3 (the tens place, so it's actually 30):\n\n3 * 0 =0\n3 *8 =24, but since it's the tens place, this is 2400.\n\nAdding 720 +2400 gives 3120. \n\nAlternatively, if I line them up properly:\n\n      80\n    x39\n    -----\n      720  (80 x 9)\n    +2400  (80 x 30, which is 3 shifted one position left)\n    -----\n      3120\n\nYep, that's correct. \n\nI think that's thorough. I can't see any mistake here, so the product should be 3120. I think that's the right answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 80 and 39 is \\boxed{3120}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 80 and 39, we can use multiple methods to verify the result:\n\n1. **Breaking down the multiplication**:\n   - \\( 80 \\times 39 = 8 \\times 10 \\times 39 \\)\n   - First, calculate \\( 8 \\times 39 \\):\n     - \\( 8 \\times 40 = 320 \\)\n     - Subtract \\( 8 \\times 1 = 8 \\) from 320: \\( 320