rry over 1.\n\nNext diagonal: 5 +3 +2 + the carried 1 = 11, so write 1 and carry over 1.\n\nFinally the leftmost diagonal: 1 carried over, so 1.\n\nPutting those together gives 1 (from the left), then 1, then 2, then 5. Wait, that would be 1 1 2 5? Wait, maybe I messed up the lattice steps.\n\nWait, perhaps I need to visualize it better. Let me try again.\n\nThe lattice grid for 63\u00d795:\n\nThe top number is 6 (tens) and 3 (units). The right number is 9 (tens) and 5 (units).\n\nThe grid has four cells:\n\nTop row: 6 and 3.\n\nRight column: 9 and 5.\n\nEach cell is divided by a diagonal line.\n\nFirst cell (top-left): 6\u00d79=54, so top-left part is 5, bottom-right is 4.\n\nSecond cell (top-right): 6\u00d75=30, so top is 3, bottom 0.\n\nThird cell (bottom-left): 3\u00d79=27, top is 2, bottom 7.\n\nFourth cell (bottom-right): 3\u00d75=15, top is 1, bottom 5.\n\nThen, when adding diagonally from the right:\n\nStarting at the bottom right, the rightmost diagonal is the bottom number of the last cell (5). Then moving left, the next diagonal includes the bottom of the top-right cell (0) and the bottom of the bottom-right cell (wait no, maybe I need to think of it as diagonals going from top-right to bottom-left). Hmm, maybe I need to recall the exact steps.\n\nWait, perhaps it's better to think that each diagonal corresponds to a digit place. Let me see:\n\nThe diagonals from the right side:\n\nFirst diagonal (units place): the bottom of the bottom-right cell (5). That's 5.\n\nSecond diagonal (tens place): the bottom of the top-right cell (0) and the bottom of the bottom-left cell (7). 0 + 7 =7.\n\nThird diagonal (hundreds place): the bottom of the top-left cell (4) and the top of the bottom-left cell (2). Wait no, maybe I'm mixing up the diagonals. Alternatively, maybe each cell's bottom and top parts contribute to the diagonals. Maybe I need to add the numbers along the diagonals as follows:\n\nStarting from the lower right corner, the first diagonal is just the 5 (from 15's units place). The next diagonal is 0 (from 30's units) and 7 (from 27's units). Wait, but 30 is split into 3 and 0, so the bottom part is 0, and 27 is split into 2 and7, so the bottom part is7. Then the second diagonal would be 0 +7 =7. Then the next diagonal is 4 (from 54's units) and 1 (from 15's tens?) Wait, no. Maybe this is getting too confusing. Alternatively, perhaps I should look up the lattice steps again, but since I can't right now, maybe I should just trust my previous methods which all gave 5985. Since the lattice method is taking me too long and I might be making a mistake here, I'll stick with the prior results.\n\nAlternatively, I can use another check: 63\u00d795. If I approximate, 60\u00d795 is 5700, and 3\u00d795 is 285, so 5700+285=5985. Yep, that's the same answer. So all methods are leading me to 5985, so I think that's correct.\n\nWait, just to make sure, maybe I can divide the result by one of the numbers to see if I get the other. Let's try dividing 5985 by 63. If that gives 95, then it's correct.\n\nDividing 5985 \u00f7 63. Let's see. 63\u00d790 is 5670. 5985 - 5670 is 315. 63\u00d75 is 315. So 90+5=95. Perfect, that works. So yes, 63\u00d795 is indeed 5985.\n\nAnother check: divide 5985 by 95 and see if it's 63. Let's see, 95\u00d760 is 5700. 5985 -5700 is 285. 95\u00d73=285. So 60+3=63. Perfect again. So that confirms it.\n\nTherefore, after multiple methods and checks, I can confidently say the product is 5985.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 63 and 95 is \\boxed{5985}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 63 and 95, we can use multiple methods to ensure accuracy:\n\n1. **Distributive Property**:\n   - Break down the numbers: \\(63 = 60 + 3\\) and \\(95 = 90 + 5\\).\n   - Multiply each part:\n     - \\(60 \\times 90 = 5400\\)\n     - \\(60 \\times 5 = 300\\)\n     - \\(3 \\times 90 = 270\\)\n     - \\(3 \\times 5 = 15\\)\n   - Sum the results:\n     \\[\n     5400 + 300 + 270 + 15 = 5985\n     \\]\n\n2. **Standard Vertical Multiplication**:\n   - Multiply 63 by 5:\n     \\[\n     63 \\times 5 = 315\n     \\]\n   - Multiply 63 by 90 (note the 9 is in the tens place):\n     \\[\n     63 \\times 90 = 5670\n     \\]\n   - Add the results:\n     \\[\n     315 + 5670 = 5985\n     \\]\n\n3. **Alternative Method (Using 100 - 5)**:\n   - Rewrite 95 as \\(100 - 5\\):\n     \\[\n     63 \\times (100 - 5) = 63 \\times 100 - 63 \\times 5\n     \\]\n   - Calculate each part:\n     \\[\n     6300 - 315 = 5985\n     \\]\n\n4. **Verification by Division**:\n   - Divide the result by 63:\n     \\[\n     5985 \\div 63 = 95\n     \\]\n   - Divide the result by 95:\n     \\[\n     5985 \\div 95 = 63\n     \\]\n\nAll methods confirm the product is \\(\\boxed{5985}\\).",
