      "text": "4790 +24= 4814. Wait, is that right?\n\nWait, let me check my addition again because sometimes I might make a mistake there. Let's add the numbers again:\n\n4000 + 640 is indeed 4640. Then 4640 + 150: 4640 + 100 is 4740, plus 50 is 4790. Then 4790 +24: 4790 +20 is 4810, plus 4 is 4814. Okay, that seems correct.\n\nBut let me verify using another method to ensure I didn't make a mistake. Maybe using the standard multiplication algorithm.\n\nMultiplying 83 by 58:\n\nFirst, write 83\ntimes 58\n________\n\nMultiply 83 by 8: 83*8. 80*8=640, 3*8=24; so 640+24=664.\n\nThen multiply 83 by 50. Since it's the tens place, so 50 is 5*10. So 83*50 is 83*5*10. 83*5 is 415, so times 10 is 4150.\n\nNow add the two results: 664 + 4150. Let's see, 664 + 4150. 4150 + 600 is 4750, then +64 is 4814. So same result. Okay, so that checks out.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do this another way, like using the difference of squares or some other trick? Let me see if that's possible.\n\nWait, 83 and 58... Hmm, not sure if they can be expressed as numbers around a common midpoint. Let me think. Alternatively, maybe 83*58 can be written as (80 +3)(60 -2). Wait, 58 is 60-2? Wait, 60-2 is 58, yes. Hmm, maybe that could work? Let me try that.\n\nSo (80 + 3)(60 - 2). Using the distributive property again:\n\n80*60 + 80*(-2) + 3*60 + 3*(-2).\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n80*60 = 4800,\n\n80*(-2) = -160,\n\n3*60 = 180,\n\n3*(-2)= -6.\n\nAdding them all together: 4800 -160 + 180 -6.\n\nLet's compute step by step:\n\n4800 -160 is 4640,\n\n4640 +180 is 4820,\n\n4820 -6 is 4814.\n\nSame answer! So that works too. So that's another way to get the same result. So that confirms it again.\n\nHmm, so all methods are pointing towards 4814. I think that's the correct answer. Let me just check once more with a calculator-like approach.\n\nAlternatively, 83 * 58.\n\nBreak down 58 into 50 +8 as before, so 83*(50+8) = 83*50 +83*8. Which is the same as before, so 4150 +664 = 4814. Yep.\n\nAlternatively, maybe 83*58 can be thought of as 83*60 minus 83*2. Let's see:\n\n83*60 = (80*60) + (3*60) = 4800 + 180 = 4980,\n\nThen subtract 83*2 = 166,\n\nSo 4980 -166 = 4814. Yep, that works as well.\n\nSo all different methods are confirming that the product is 4814. Therefore, I can be confident that the answer is correct. I think that's thorough enough.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 83 and 58 is \\boxed{4814}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 83 and 58, we can use the distributive property by breaking down each number into smaller parts:\n\n1. Break down the numbers:\n   - \\(83 = 80 + 3\\)\n   - \\(58 = 50 + 8\\)\n\n2. Apply the distributive property:\n   \\[\n   (80 + 3)(50 + 8) = 80 \\times 50 + 80 \\times 8 + 3 \\times 50 + 3 \\times 8\n   \\]\n\n3. Calculate each term individually:\n   - \\(80 \\times 50 = 4000\\)\n   - \\(80 \\times 8 = 640\\)\n   - \\(3 \\times 50 = 150\\)\n   - \\(3 \\times 8 = 24\\)\n\n4. Sum the results of each term:\n   \\[\n   4000 + 640 + 150 + 24\n   \\]\n\n5. Perform the addition step-by-step:\n   - \\(4000 + 640 = 4640\\)\n   - \\(4640 + 150 = 4790\\)\n   - \\(4790 + 24 = 4814\\)\n\nVerification using another method (standard multiplication algorithm):\n- Multiply 83 by 8: \\(83 \\times 8 = 664\\)\n- Multiply 83 by 50: \\(83 \\times 50 = 4150\\)\n- Add the results: \\(664 + 4150 = 4814\\)\n\nVerification using another method (difference of squares approach):\n- Express \\(58\\) as \\(60 - 2\\)\n- Apply the distributive property:\n  \\[\n  (80 + 3)(60 - 2) = 80 \\times 60 - 80 \\times 2 + 3 \\times 60 - 3 \\times 2\n  \\]\n- Calculate each term:\n  - \\(80 \\times 60 = 4800\\)\n  - \\(80 \\times (-2) = -160\\)\n  - \\(3 \\times 60 = 180\\)\n  - \\(3 \\times (-2) = -6\\)\n- Sum the results:\n  \\[\n  4800 - 160 + 180 - 6 = 4814\n  \\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 83 and 58 is \\(\\boxed{4814}\\).",
