 which is (14\u00d73)\u00d710 = 42\u00d710 = 420. \n\nAlternatively, maybe using fractions or decimals? Not sure if that's necessary here, but let's try converting 14 into a decimal... Hmm, maybe overcomplicating. Alternatively, think of 30 as 3\u00d710, so 14*(3*10)= (14*3)*10 as before. \n\nAlternatively, use the commutative property: 30 times 14. Maybe that's easier for some people. 30 times 10 is 300, and 30 times 4 is 120, so 300+120=420. Yep, same thing. \n\nI think all these methods are leading me to the same answer, 420. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations. Let me just confirm once more with a calculator in my mind. 14 multiplied by 30. If 14*10 is 140, then 14*30 is three times that, so 140*3. 140+140=280, plus another 140 is 420. Yes, that works. \n\nAlternatively, breaking down 14 into 7*2, so 7*2*30 = 7*(2*30)=7*60=420. That also works. \n\nHmm, seems like every approach I take gets me to 420, so I'm pretty confident that's the right answer. I think I've checked it thoroughly enough. No steps seem to be missing or miscalculated. So the product of 14 and 30 is indeed 420.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 14 and 30 is calculated by multiplying the two numbers together. \n\nFirst, break down the multiplication for simplicity:\n\\[\n14 \\times 30 = 14 \\times (3 \\times 10) = (14 \\times 3) \\times 10\n\\]\nCalculate \\(14 \\times 3\\):\n\\[\n14 \\times 3 = 42\n\\]\nThen multiply the result by 10:\n\\[\n42 \\times 10 = 420\n\\]\n\n**Verification using the distributive property:**\n\\[\n14 \\times 30 = (10 + 4) \\times 30 = (10 \\times 30) + (4 \\times 30) = 300 + 120 = 420\n\\]\n\n**Direct multiplication:**\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n\\quad \\ 14 \\\\\n\\times 30 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\quad 0 \\quad \\text{(14 \u00d7 0)} \\\\\n420 \\quad \\ \\ \\text{(14 \u00d7 30 shifted one position left)} \\\\\n\\hline\n\\quad 420 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nAll methods confirm the result is **420**.\n\n**Answer:** \\boxed{420}",
