138. Seems like that's the correct answer.\n\nWait, but let me try another way just to be thorough. Maybe using the fact that 66 is 6\u00d711, so 93\u00d76\u00d711. Let's see:\n\nFirst compute 93 \u00d76, which is 558. Then multiply that by 11. 558 \u00d711. There's a trick for multiplying by 11 where you add the digits. But for three-digit numbers, it's a bit different. Let me recall: for a three-digit number, say ABC \u00d711, it's A (A+B) (B+C) C, but if any of the sums are more than 9, you carry over.\n\nSo for 558 \u00d711:\n\nStart with the first digit: 5\n\nThen 5 +5 =10, so write 0 and carry over 1.\n\nNext digit: 5 +8 =13, plus the carried over 1 is 14. Write 4 and carry over 1.\n\nLast digit: 8, plus the carried over 1 is 9.\n\nWait, but let me write it step by step:\n\n558 \u00d711:\n\nBreak it down as 558\u00d710 +558\u00d71 = 5,580 +558 = 6,138. Yep, same as before. So that's another way.\n\nAlternatively, using the digit addition method:\n\n5 (5+5)=10, so write 0 carry 1, then (5+8 +1)=14. Wait, maybe my initial thought was wrong. Let me see:\n\nActually, the method for three-digit numbers:\n\n558 \u00d711:\n\nStart from the right:\n\nThe units digit: 8 \u00d71 =8, plus nothing yet. Wait, maybe I should think of it as:\n\nMultiply 558 by 11:\n\nFirst digit: 5\n\nSecond digit: 5 +5 =10\n\nThird digit:5 +8=13\n\nFourth digit:8\n\nBut since this is a three-digit number, the result is four digits:\n\nWait, perhaps it's better to do it step by step.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I confused the method. Let me just do it the straightforward way:\n\n558 \u00d711:\n\n558 \u00d7 10 is 5,580, plus 558 is 6,138. So that works.\n\nOkay, so all methods lead to 6,138. Therefore, I can be confident that the product of 93 and 66 is 6,138.\n\nAlternatively, to ensure that I didn't make any arithmetic errors, maybe I can check using a calculator method, but since I'm doing this manually, let me try another approach.\n\nWait, what if I approximate to check if it's reasonable?