hich includes the lower right part of the bottom right cell (5) and the upper right part of the cell to its left (0 from the first row's second cell). So 5 +0=5.\n\nNext diagonal (tens place): the upper part of the second cell in the first row (1) and the lower part of the first cell in the second row (4) plus the upper part of the first cell in the first row (0). Wait, perhaps this is getting too confusing. Maybe an alternative approach.\n\nAlternatively, writing the diagonals as:\n\nThe first diagonal (units): the rightmost digit of the rightmost column. The rightmost column has cells 1/0 (first row) and 0/5 (second row). The right parts are 0 and 5, so sum is 5.\n\nNext diagonal (tens): the left part of the right column (1 from first row's second cell) and the right part of the left column (8 from first row's first cell and 4 from second row's first cell).\n\nWait, maybe I'm mixing up. Let me try to sum each diagonal properly.\n\nLet me write the grid with the numbers separated by the diagonal lines:\n\nFor the first row (first multiplier digit 2):\n\nFirst cell (top-left): 0 / 8 (so upper left is 0, lower right is 8)\n\nSecond cell (top-right): 1 / 0 (upper left 1, lower right 0)\n\nSecond row (second multiplier digit 1):\n\nFirst cell (bottom-left): 0 / 4 (upper left 0, lower right 4)\n\nSecond cell (bottom-right): 0 / 5 (upper left 0, lower right 5)\n\nNow, the diagonals are formed by the lower right parts of the cells in a diagonal line from top-right to bottom-left.\n\nStarting from the top-right corner cell (second cell first row): its lower right is 0. The next diagonal would be the lower right of the first cell first row (8) and the lower right of the second cell second row (5). Then the next diagonal is the lower right of the first cell second row (4) and the upper left parts? Wait, maybe I need to think of diagonals as the sum of the lower right and upper left of adjacent cells.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to sum the numbers along each diagonal line from the bottom right to the top left.\n\nLet me structure it as follows:\n\nThe rightmost diagonal (units place) is just the lower right of the bottom right cell: 5.\n\nNext diagonal (tens place): the lower right of the cell to the left of bottom right (which is the first cell of the second row, its lower right is 4) plus the upper right of the cell above the bottom right (the second cell of the first row's upper right is 1). Wait, maybe:\n\nWait, perhaps I need to arrange all the digits in the grid properly and then add along the diagonals.\n\nThe grid with the divided cells:\n\nFirst row (2):\n\nFirst cell: 0 | 8\n\nSecond cell:1 | 0\n\nSecond row (1):\n\nFirst cell:0 |4\n\nSecond cell:0 |5\n\nNow, to form the diagonals:\n\nStarting from the bottom right, the first diagonal (units place) is just the 5 from the bottom right cell's right part.\n\nNext diagonal (tens place) would be the 4 (from first cell of second row's right part) and the 0 (from second cell first row's right part). Wait, no, maybe it's the 4 and the 0 from the cells above and to the left?\n\nAlternatively, the diagonals are as follows:\n\n- The first diagonal (units): the rightmost digit in the rightmost column: 5 (from bottom right cell's right part).\n\n- The second diagonal (tens): the digits in the diagonal that goes from the cell to the left of the bottom right (which has 4 in its right part) and the cell above it (second cell first row's right part is 0). So 4 +0 =4. But adding that, but then also the upper left part of the first cell?\n\nHmm, maybe this is getting too convoluted. Let me try adding all the diagonals properly.\n\nStarting from the bottom right, moving up-left:\n\nFirst diagonal (units): 5 (from bottom-right cell's right side).\n\nSecond diagonal (tens): 4 (from first cell of second row's right side) and 0 (from second cell first row's right side). So total 4+0=4.\n\nThird diagonal (hundreds place): 0 (from first cell first row's right side) and 1 (from second cell first row's left side) and 0 (from first cell second row's left side). Wait, this might not be correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the left parts and the right parts are added differently. Let me recall that in lattice method, each cell's number is split into tens and units, and the diagonals are the sum of the digits along each diagonal line.\n\nPerhaps arranging all the numbers in the grid:\n\nThe grid after multiplication looks like this:\n\nFirst row (for digit 2):\n\nCell 1: 0 (tens) | 8 (units)\n\nCell 2:1 (tens)| 0 (units)\n\nSecond row (for digit 1):\n\nCell 3:0 (tens)|4 (units)\n\nCell 4:0 (tens)|5 (units)\n\nNow, the diagonals are:\n\nStarting from the bottom right (cell4's units digit 5) as the first diagonal (units place).\n\nThe next diagonal is the sum of cell3's units (4) and cell2's units (0) \u2192 4+0=4 (tens place).\n\nThe next diagonal is the sum of cell1's units (8), cell2's tens (1), and cell3's tens (0) \u2192 8 +1 +0 =9 (hundreds place).\n\nThe last diagonal is cell1's tens (0) \u2192 0 (thousands place).\n\nSo combining these diagonals from right to left: 5 (units), 4 (tens), 9 (hundreds), 0 (thousands). So the number is 945. That matches the previous results. Okay, so the lattice method also gives 945. Good.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can check using another approach, like the distributive property again but breaking down differently. For instance, 45\u00d721 is the same as (40 +5)(20 +1). Wait, I did that already, but perhaps expanding in another way. Alternatively, 45\u00d721 = 45\u00d7(20 +1) =45\u00d720 +45\u00d71 =900 +45=945. Same result.\n\nAlternatively, using mental math: 45 times 20 is 900, and then adding another 45 gives 945. That's straightforward.\n\nAlternatively, I can think of 21 as 21, so 45\u00d721. Maybe using the fact that 45\u00d72=90, so 45\u00d720=900, then adding 45 gives 945. Yeah, that's the same as before.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using some other multiplication trick. For example, 45 is 9\u00d75, and 21 is 3\u00d77. But not sure if that helps here. Alternatively, 45\u00d721 = (45\u00d77)\u00d73. Let's see: 45\u00d77 is 315, then 315\u00d73 is 945. That works too. Let me compute 45\u00d77: 40\u00d77=280, 5\u00d77=35, so 280+35=315. Then 315\u00d73: 300\u00d73=900, 15\u00d73=45, so 900+45=945. Yep, same answer.\n\nAlternatively, breaking down 21 as 21 = 20 +1, as before. All methods point to 945. I think that's solid.\n\nWait, but just to ensure I didn't make any calculation errors, let me do the standard algorithm again step by step.\n\n45\n\n\u00d721\n\n--------\n\nFirst, multiply 45 by 1 (units place):\n\n1\u00d75 =5, 1\u00d74=4 \u2192 45.\n\nThen multiply 45 by 2 (tens place, so it's actually 20). So 2\u00d75=10, write down 0 and carry over 1. Then 2\u00d74=8 plus the carried 1 is 9. So that gives 90. But since it's the tens place, we shift one position to the left, so it's 900.\n\nAdding 45 and 900: 900 +45. 900+40=940, plus5 is 945. Correct.\n\nHmm, so after all these methods and checks, I can be confident the answer is 945. I don't see any discrepancies in the calculations, so I think that's the correct product of 45 and 21.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 45 and 21 is \\boxed{945}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 45 and 21, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n1. Write the numbers vertically aligned by place value:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n    45 \\\\\n   \\times 21 \\\\\n   \\hline\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n2. Multiply 45 by the units digit of 21 (which is 1):\n   \\[\n   45 \\times 1 = 45\n   \\]\n   Write this partial product down:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n    45 \\\\\n   \\times 21 \\\\\n   \\hline\n    45 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n3. Multiply 45 by the tens digit of 21 (which is 2, representing 20). Shift the result one place to the left:\n   \\[\n   45 \\times 2 = 90 \\quad \\text{(this represents 45 \\times 20 = 900)}\n   \\]\n   Write this partial product shifted left:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n    45 \\\\\n   \\times