      "text": "1800 - 18 equals 1782. Wait, let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake. .\n\n1800 minus 10 would be 1790, and then minus 8 more would be 1782. Yeah, that's right. Alternatively, maybe I can verify by another method to be sure. .\n\nAnother way to multiply 99 by 18 is to do standard multiplication. Let's set it up:\n\n   99\n\u00d7 18\n------\nFirst multiply 99 by 8. 9 times 8 is 72, carry over the 7. 9 times 8 again (since the second digit is also 9) plus the carried 7 is 72 +7=79. Wait, no, hold on. Maybe I need to do it step by step.\n\nWait, actually, when multiplying 99 by 8, it's 99 \u00d78. 9\u00d78 is 72, write down 2 and carry 7. Then the next digit (the tens place) is also 9, so 9\u00d78=72 plus the carried 7 is 79. So that gives 792.\n\nThen multiply 99 by 10 (since the 1 in 18 is in the tens place). So 99\u00d710 is 990. Then add the two results together: 792 + 990. Let's add those. 792 + 900 is 1692, then add 90 more to get 1782. Yep, same answer. .\n\nHmm, so both methods give me 1782. That seems consistent. Maybe I can check with a calculator if I have one, but since this is a problem-solving exercise, I think my manual calculations are correct here. .\n\nAlternatively, another way to verify is by using the distributive property again but breaking down 18 instead. Let's see, 18 is 10 + 8. So 99\u00d7(10+8) = 99\u00d710 + 99\u00d78. That would be 990 + 792, which again is 1782. Yep, same result. .\n\nI think that's solid. All different methods lead to the same answer. So the product of 99 and 18 is definitely 1782. I don't see any errors in my calculations here. So I'm confident that this is the correct result.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 99 and 18 can be calculated efficiently using the distributive property by recognizing that 99 is 100 - 1:\n\n\\[\n99 \\times 18 = (100 - 1) \\times 18 = 100 \\times 18 - 1 \\times 18\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term separately:\n- \\( 100 \\times 18 = 1800 \\)\n- \\( 1 \\times 18 = 18 \\)\n\nSubtracting these results:\n\\[\n1800 - 18 = 1782\n\\]\n\n**Verification via standard multiplication:**\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n   & \\quad \\ 99 \\\\\n\\times & \\ 18 \\\\\n\\hline\n   & 792 \\quad (\\text{99 \u00d7 8}) \\\\\n+ & 990 \\quad (\\text{99 \u00d7 10, shifted one position left}) \\\\\n\\hline\n   & 1782 \\\\\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm the result. \n\n**Answer:** \\(\\boxed{1782}\\)",
