 560+56=616. \n\nAdding those together: 1760 +616. Let's compute that. 1760 +600 is 2360, then +16 is 2376. Again, same result. \n\nOkay, so all methods lead to 2376, so I think that's correct. \n\nAlternatively, maybe use a calculator? But since this is a problem-solving exercise, I should rely on my calculations. Since all different methods give me the same answer, I can be confident it's correct. \n\nTherefore, the product of 27 and 88 is 2376.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 27 and 88 is \\boxed{2376}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 27 and 88, we can use the distributive property by breaking down the numbers:\n\n1. Break down 88 into 80 and 8:\n   \\[\n   27 \\times 88 = 27 \\times (80 + 8)\n   \\]\n\n2. Calculate each part separately:\n   - First part: \\(27 \\times 80\\)\n     \\[\n     27 \\times 8 = 216 \\quad \\text{(then multiply by 10)} \\quad 216 \\times 10 = 2160\n     \\]\n   - Second part: \\(27 \\times 8\\)\n     \\[\n     27 \\times 8 = 216\n     \\]\n\n3. Add the results of the two parts:\n   \\[\n   2160 + 216 = 2376\n   \\]\n\nVerification using the standard multiplication algorithm:\n- Multiply 27 by 8 (units digit of 88):\n  \\[\n  27 \\times 8 = 216\n  \\]\n- Multiply 27 by 80 (tens digit of 88, shifted one position left):\n  \\[\n  27 \\times 80 = 2160\n  \\]\n- Add the results:\n  \\[\n  216 + 2160 = 2376\n  \\]\n\nAnother verification by breaking down 27 into 20 and 7:\n- Calculate \\(20 \\times 88\\) and \\(7 \\times 88\\):\n  \\[\n  20 \\times 88 = 1760 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 7 \\times 88 = 616\n  \\]\n- Add the results:\n  \\[\n  1760 + 616 = 2376\n  \\]\n\nAll methods confirm the result is consistent. Therefore, the product of 27 and 88 is \\(\\boxed{2376}\\).",
