0 +1)(78) = 30\u00d778 +1\u00d778. 30\u00d778 is 2340, plus 78 is 2418. Exactly the same as before. So that works. \n\nHmm, so all the methods are leading me to 2418. Therefore, I think the product of 31 and 78 is 2418. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations, and the cross-verification using different methods gives the same result, so that's probably correct. \n\nWait, but let me just do the multiplication again step by step to be thorough. \n\nFirst partial product: 31 \u00d78. \n\n31 \u00d78: \n\n1\u00d78=8, \n\n30\u00d78=240. \n\n240 +8=248. Correct. \n\nSecond partial product: 31 \u00d770. \n\n31 \u00d77 = 217, so 31 \u00d770 is 217\u00d710=2170. Correct. \n\nAdding them together:\n\n248 +2170. \n\n2170 +200 =2370,\n\n2370 +48 =2418. Yep, same as before. \n\nAlright, so I think that's solid. Therefore, the answer should be 2418. \n\nJust to ensure I didn't transpose any numbers, maybe I can write down the multiplication vertically again:\n\n```.\n    31.\n  \u00d7 78.\n  -----\n    248  (31\u00d78)\n  +2170  (31\u00d770, shifted one position to the left)\n  -----\n    2418\n```.\n\nYes, that's exactly how it should look. So, the product is indeed 2418. I don't see any errors here. \n\nAnother way: Let's try the standard algorithm again, but writing it out step by step. \n\nMultiplying 31 by 8:\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit of the top number (1) times 8: 1\u00d78=8. \n\nThen the next digit (3) times 8: 3\u00d78=24. So writing that down gives 248. \n\nThen multiplying by the 7 (which is in the tens place, so it's 70):\n\nAgain, starting with the rightmost digit of the top number (1) times 7 (but remember it's actually 70, so when written, we have to shift one position to the left). So 1\u00d77=7, written in the tens column. Then 3\u00d77=21, so writing that in the hundreds and tens place. So it would be 217 shifted one place left, which is 2170. \n\nAdding the two partial products: 248 +2170 =2418. \n\nAll methods confirm the same result. I think that's thorough. Therefore, I can confidently say the product is 2418. \n\nWait, just thinking if I might have messed up the shifting. Let me see: when multiplying by the tens digit (7), we have to shift one place to the left. So, when writing the second partial product, it should be 2170, which is indeed 217 shifted left by one digit. So the addition is correct. \n\nAlternatively, when adding, maybe writing it vertically:\n\n```.\n   248.\n+2170.\n------.\n   2418\n```.\n\nAdding the units place: 8 +0=8.\n\nTens place:4 +7=11. Write down 1 and carry over 1.\n\nHundreds place:2 +1 +1 (carry)=4.\n\nThousands place:2.\n\nWait, but 248 is:\n\n- 8 (units)\n\n- 4 (tens)\n\n- 2 (hundreds)\n\nAnd 2170 is:\n\n- 0 (units)\n\n- 7 (tens)\n\n- 1 (hundreds)\n\n- 2 (thousands)\n\nSo when adding:\n\nUnits:8+0=8\n\nTens:4+7=11 \u2192 write 1, carryover 1\n\nHundreds:2 +1 +1 (carryover) =4\n\nThousands:2 (from 2170) plus 0 (since 248 has no thousands place) \u21922.\n\nSo total is 2418. Exactly. So that's correct. \n\nOkay, that's three different ways of adding the partial products and all confirm the same answer. \n\nAnother cross-check: 31 \u00d778. Let me see if I can break it down differently. \n\nPerhaps 31 \u00d778 = 31 \u00d7 (80 - 2) = 31\u00d780 - 31\u00d72. \n\nCalculating 31\u00d780: 31\u00d78\u00d710. 31\u00d78 is 248, so times 10 is 2480.\n\n31\u00d72 is 62.\n\nSo 2480 -62 = ?\n\n2480 -60 is 2420, then subtract 2 more is 2418. \n\nSame answer again. \n\nThis method also gives 2418. So that's another confirmation. \n\nAlternatively, 78 \u00d730 +78\u00d71. Which is 2340 +78=2418. Yep, same as before. \n\nI think that's enough verification. All paths lead to 2418, so the answer must be correct. \n\nJust to recap, the problem was straightforward, but going through multiple methods helps ensure there's no arithmetic mistake. Since all methods concur, I can be sure that 2418 is the right product of 31 and 78. \n\nTherefore, the final answer is 2418.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 31 and 78 is \\boxed{2418}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 31 and 78, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n1. Multiply 31 by 8 (the units digit of 78):\n   \\[\n   31 \\times 8 = 248\n   \\]\n   This gives the first partial product: 248.\n\n2. Multiply 31 by 70 (the tens digit of 78, which is 7 in the tens place, so 70):\n   \\[\n   31 \\times 70 = 2170\n   \\]\n   This gives the second partial product: 2170.\n\n3. Add the two partial products together:\n   \\[\n   248 + 2170 = 2418\n   \\]\n\nWe can verify this result using different methods:\n- **Distributive Property**:\n  \\[\n  (30 + 1)(70 + 8) = 30 \\times 70 + 30 \\times 8 + 1 \\times 70 + 1 \\times 8 = 2100 + 240 + 70 + 8 = 2418\n  \\]\n\n- **Alternative Breakdown**:\n  \\[\n  31 \\times 78 = 31 \\times (80 - 2) = 31 \\times 80 - 31 \\times 2 = 2480 - 62 = 2418\n  \\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 31 and 78 is \\(\\boxed{2418}\\).",
