      "text": "2760 - 100 = 2660, then add back 8 to get 2668? Wait, let me check that again. If I subtract 92 from 2760, another way to think is breaking it into 2760 - 90 - 2. \n\n2760 minus 90 is 2670, and then minus 2 is 2668. Yeah, that matches the first method. So that gives me 2668. \n\nWait, but let me verify this another way to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Maybe do the multiplication the standard way, vertically. Let me set it up:\n\nMultiply 92 by 29.\n\nFirst, write 92  \n\u00d7\u00a029  \n________\n\nMultiply 92 by 9 (units place): 92 \u00d7 9. Let's see, 90 \u00d7 9 = 810, and 2 \u00d7 9 = 18, so 810 + 18 = 828.\n\nThen multiply 92 by 20 (tens place, so it's 2 in the tens place, so actually 20). So 92 \u00d7 20. That would be 92 \u00d7 2 = 184, then add a zero to get 1840.\n\nNow add the two results together: 828 + 1840. Let's add 800 + 1800 = 2600, and 28 + 40 = 68. So total is 2600 + 68 = 2668. Okay, that matches my previous answer. So that seems right.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method to confirm. Let's see, perhaps using the standard algorithm again but breaking down differently. Wait, perhaps use another distributive approach. Let's try (90 + 2) \u00d7 (20 + 9). That would be expanding the terms:\n\n(90 + 2)(20 + 9) = 90\u00d720 + 90\u00d79 + 2\u00d720 + 2\u00d79.\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n90\u00d720 = 1800,\n\n90\u00d79 = 810,\n\n2\u00d720 = 40,\n\n2\u00d79 = 18.\n\nAdding them all together: 1800 + 810 = 2610; 40 + 18 = 58; then 2610 + 58 = 2668. Yep, same answer again.\n\nHmm, so all methods are pointing to 2668. Wait a second, but just to be thorough, let me check with a calculator method. Since I don't have a calculator here, but maybe do another verification. Let's see, 29 times 90 is 2610, and 29 times 2 is 58, so adding them gives 2610 + 58 = 2668. Yep, that's the same as before.\n\nAlternatively, maybe check if 2668 divided by 29 equals 92. Let's try dividing 2668 by 29. Let's see, 29 times 90 is 2610, subtract that from 2668: 2668 - 2610 = 58. Then 58 divided by 29 is 2. So total is 90 + 2 = 92. Perfect, so that checks out.\n\nAlternatively, maybe another way: 29 is a prime number? Well, 29 is a prime, so I can't simplify that. Alternatively, use some other multiplication trick. For example, 29 is 30 -1, so 92*(30 -1) = 92*30 -92, as I did before, which gives 2760 - 92 = 2668. So all methods confirm that the product is 2668. \n\nI think that's solid. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations, and cross-verified using multiple methods: distributive property, standard vertical multiplication, breaking into components, and division to check. All lead to the same result, so I can be confident that the answer is 2668.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 92 and 29 is \\boxed{2668}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 92 and 29, we can use the distributive property by breaking down 29 into 30 - 1:\n\n\\[\n92 \\times 29 = 92 \\times (30 - 1) = (92 \\times 30) - (92 \\times 1)\n\\]\n\nCalculating each part separately:\n- \\( 92 \\times 30 = 2760 \\)\n- \\( 92 \\times 1 = 92 \\)\n\nSubtracting these results:\n\\[\n2760 - 92 = 2668\n\\]\n\nTo verify, we can use the standard vertical multiplication method:\n- Multiply 92 by 9 (units place): \\( 92 \\times 9 = 828 \\)\n- Multiply 92 by 20 (tens place): \\( 92 \\times 20 = 1840 \\)\n\nAdding these results:\n\\[\n828 + 1840 = 2668\n\\]\n\nAnother verification using the distributive property expanded form:\n\\[\n(90 + 2) \\times (20 + 9) = 90 \\times 20 + 90 \\times 9 + 2 \\times 20 + 2 \\times 9\n\\]\n\\[\n= 1800 + 810 + 40 + 18 = 2668\n\\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 92 and 29 is \\(\\boxed{2668}\\).",
