    {
      "index": 0,
      "text": "4770 + 200 is 4970, and then plus 12 more is 4982. Wait, let me check that again. 4770 + 212. Hmm, 4770 plus 200 would be 4970, then plus 12 is indeed 4982. Okay, so that's the total.\n\nBut just to make sure I didn't make a mistake, maybe I can try another method to cross-verify. Another way to multiply numbers is using the standard algorithm. Let me set it up vertically:\n\n\u2003\u00a053  \n\u00d7\u00a094  \n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500  \nFirst, multiply 53 by 4 (the units digit of 94). 53 \u00d7 4 = 212. Then multiply 53 by 90 (the tens digit part, so actually 9 in the tens place means 90). 53 \u00d7 90 = 4770, just like before. Then add those two results: 212 + 4770 = 4982. Okay, same result. That seems good.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method like the lattice method? Hmm, but I might not remember the exact steps for that. Alternatively, perhaps I can check using estimation. Let's see, 53 is close to 50 and 94 is close to 90. So 50 \u00d7 90 = 4500. The actual answer should be a bit higher since both numbers are a bit higher than 50 and 90. 4982 is indeed higher than 4500, so that makes sense. Maybe another check: 53 \u00d7 100 would be 5300. Since 94 is 6 less than 100, so 53 \u00d7 6 = 318, so 5300 - 318 = 4982. Wait, that's another way to do it?\n\nWait, hold on. Let me think. If I consider 94 as 100 - 6, then 53 \u00d7 (100 - 6) = 53\u00d7100 - 53\u00d76 = 5300 - 318. Let me compute that. 5300 minus 300 is 5000, minus 18 more is 4982. Yep, that also gives the same answer. So that seems to confirm it again.\n\nHmm, so all three methods are giving me 4982. That gives me more confidence that the answer is correct. Let me just do the standard multiplication step-by-step again to be thorough.\n\nMultiplying 53 by 94:\n\nFirst step: Multiply 53 by 4 (units place):\n\n4 \u00d7 3 = 12, write down 2 and carry over 1.\n\n4 \u00d7 5 = 20, plus the carried 1 is 21. So first partial product is 212.\n\nSecond step: Multiply 53 by 9 (tens place). But since it's the tens place, we need to shift one position to the left, so effectively multiplying by 90.\n\n9 \u00d7 3 = 27, write down 7 and carry over 2.\n\n9 \u00d7 5 = 45, plus the carried 2 is 47. So the second partial product is 477, but shifted one place to the left, making it 4770.\n\nAdding 212 + 4770:\n\n212  \n+4770  \n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500  \n4982\n\nYep, that checks out. All right, so I think the product of 53 and 94 is indeed 4982. I've verified it multiple ways, so I feel confident about this answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 53 and 94 is \\boxed{4982}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 53 and 94, we can use the distributive property and the standard algorithm for multiplication.\n\nFirst, breaking down the problem using the distributive property:\n\\[\n53 \\times 94 = 53 \\times (90 + 4) = (53 \\times 90) + (53 \\times 4)\n\\]\n\nCalculating each part separately:\n1. \\( 53 \\times 90 \\):\n   \\[\n   53 \\times 90 = 53 \\times 9 \\times 10 = (50 \\times 9 + 3 \\times 9) \\times 10 = (450 + 27) \\times 10 = 477 \\times 10 = 4770\n   \\]\n\n2. \\( 53 \\times 4 \\):\n   \\[\n   53 \\times 4 = (50 \\times 4) + (3 \\times 4) = 200 + 12 = 212\n   \\]\n\nAdding the results of the two parts:\n\\[\n4770 + 212 = 4982\n\\]\n\nVerification using the standard algorithm:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   \\quad 53 \\\\\n\\times 94 \\\\\n\\hline\n   212 \\quad (\\text{53 \u00d7 4}) \\\\\n+4770 \\quad (\\text{53 \u00d7 90, shifted one position to the left}) \\\\\n\\hline\n   4982 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nAnother verification using the complement method:\n\\[\n53 \\times 94 = 53 \\times (100 - 6) = 53 \\times 100 - 53 \\times 6 = 5300 - 318 = 4982\n\\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 53 and 94 is \\(\\boxed{4982}\\).",
