      "text": "460 + 92 = 552. Wait, is that right? Let me check again to be sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should do the multiplication the standard way. Let me write it down vertically:\n\n\u2003\u200346  \n\u00d7\u00a012  \n------  \nFirst, multiply 46 by 2 (the ones place of 12).  \n46 \u00d7 2 = 92.  \nThen, multiply 46 by 10 (the tens place of 12).  \n46 \u00d7 10 = 460.  \nAdding those together gives 92 + 460 = 552. That matches what I got before. \n\nHmm, maybe another way to verify is using the distributive property. (40 + 6) \u00d7 (10 + 2). Let's expand that:\n\n40\u00d710 + 40\u00d72 + 6\u00d710 + 6\u00d72  \n= 400 + 80 + 60 + 12  \nAdding those: 400 + 80 is 480, plus 60 is 540, plus 12 is 552. Yep, same result. Okay, so that seems consistent.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use some other method. Let's see, 46 times 12. Hmm, 12 is 12, so maybe doubling 46 six times? Wait, that might complicate. Alternatively, 46 times 12 can be thought of as 46 times 6 times 2. Wait, 46 times 6 is 276, then 276 times 2 is 552. That also works. \n\nAlternatively, breaking down 46 into 40 + 6 and then multiplying by 12:\n\n40\u00d712 = 480  \n6\u00d712 = 72  \nAdding them gives 480 + 72 = 552. \n\nHmm, all methods are leading me to 552. I think that's the correct answer. Let me just do one more check using calculator steps in my head. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the fact that 46 \u00d7 12 is the same as (45 + 1) \u00d7 12. 45 times 12 is... let's see, 45\u00d710=450 and 45\u00d72=90, so 450+90=540. Then add 1\u00d712=12, so 540 +12=552. Yep, that works too. \n\nAlright, all different methods give me the same result. I think 552 is the correct product of 46 and 12. I can't see any mistakes in my calculations, so I feel confident about this answer.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 46 and 12 is calculated as follows:\n\n1. **Breakdown using place value:**\n   - Multiply 46 by 10:  \n     \\( 46 \\times 10 = 460 \\)\n   - Multiply 46 by 2:  \n     \\( 46 \\times 2 = 92 \\)\n   - Add the results:  \n     \\( 460 + 92 = 552 \\)\n\n2. **Verification with distributive property:**  \n   \\( (40 + 6) \\times (10 + 2) = 40 \\times 10 + 40 \\times 2 + 6 \\times 10 + 6 \\times 2 \\)  \n   \\( = 400 + 80 + 60 + 12 = 552 \\)\n\n3. **Alternative method:**  \n   \\( 46 \\times 12 = 46 \\times (6 \\times 2) = (46 \\times 6) \\times 2 = 276 \\times 2 = 552 \\)\n\nAll methods confirm the result is consistent. \n\n**Answer:** \\(\\boxed{552}\\)",
