 result.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use calculator verification, but since I can't use a calculator, but given that three different methods all give me 4704, I can be confident that's the right answer. \n\nWait, let me try another approach, using the difference of squares? Because 48 and 98 are both even numbers. Hmm, not sure if that applies here, but let me see.\n\nWait, the product of 48 and 98. Maybe express them as (something squared minus something else squared). Let's see.\n\nAlternatively, maybe express both numbers as multiples of some base. Hmm, not sure. Alternatively, maybe think of 48\u00d798 as (40+8)(90+8). Let's try that.\n\nExpanding (40 + 8)(90 + 8):\n\nFirst, 40\u00d790 = 3600,\n\n40\u00d78 = 320,\n\n8\u00d790 = 720,\n\n8\u00d78 = 64.\n\nAdding all those together: 3600 + 320 = 3920; 3920 + 720 = 4640; 4640 + 64 = 4704. Again the same answer. So that works too.\n\nSo all these methods consistently lead to 4704. Therefore, I can be pretty sure that the product of 48 and 98 is 4704.\n\nWait, just to make sure I didn't make a mistake in subtraction earlier. Let me check that first method again.\n\n48\u00d7100 is 4800, correct. 48\u00d72 is 96, correct. So subtracting 96 from 4800: 4800 - 100 is 4700, but since I only took away 96, which is 4 less than 100, so add 4 back: 4700 +4 = 4704. Yep, that's correct.\n\nAnother way: 4800 minus 96. Break down 96 into 90 +6. So 4800 -90 is 4710, then subtract 6 more: 4710-6=4704. Yep, same result.\n\nAlright, so all the different calculation paths lead me to 4704. Therefore, I'm confident that's the correct answer. I think that's solid. No mistakes detected in my calculations. So I'll go with 4704 as the product of 48 and 98.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 48 and 98 is \\boxed{4704}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 48 and 98, we can use the distributive property by rewriting 98 as \\(100 - 2\\):\n\n\\[\n48 \\times 98 = 48 \\times (100 - 2)\n\\]\n\nApplying the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n48 \\times 100 - 48 \\times 2\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term separately:\n\n\\[\n48 \\times 100 = 4800\n\\]\n\\[\n48 \\times 2 = 96\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the second term from the first:\n\n\\[\n4800 - 96 = 4704\n\\]\n\nTo verify, we can use the standard multiplication method:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   48 \\\\\n\\times 98 \\\\\n\\hline\n   384 \\quad (\\text{48} \\times 8) \\\\\n+4320 \\quad (\\text{48} \\times 90) \\\\\n\\hline\n  4704 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product is \\(4704\\). Another verification using the expansion of \\((50 - 2)(100 - 2)\\) also results in \\(4704\\):\n\n\\[\n(50 - 2)(100 - 2) = 50 \\times 100 - 50 \\times 2 - 2 \\times 100 + 2 \\times 2 = 5000 - 100 - 200 + 4 = 4704\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of 48 and 98 is \\(\\boxed{4704}\\).",
