      "text": "632 followed by a zero, so 6320. Wait, is that right? Let me check again.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method to verify. Let me try the standard algorithm for multiplication. Writing it out vertically:\n\n   79\nx 80\n------\n?\n\nStarting with the 0 in the ones place: 79 times 0 is 0. Then moving to the 8 in the tens place, which is actually 80. So, 79 times 80. Since the 8 is in the tens place, it's like multiplying by 8 and then shifting one place to the left. So again, 79*8=632, then shift left one digit gives 6320. That matches my first method. \n\nHmm, but just to be thorough, maybe I can use another approach, like the distributive property. Let's see:\n\n79 * 80 = (80 - 1) * 80  \n= 80*80 - 1*80  \n= 6400 - 80  \n= 6320\n\nOh, that works too. So subtracting 80 from 6400 gives 6320. That's consistent with the previous results. \n\nAlternatively, maybe adding 79 eighty times? But that would be tedious. Let me see if there's another way. \n\nWait, maybe breaking down 79 into 70 + 9 and then multiplying each by 80 and adding the results. \n\n70*80 = 5600  \n9*80 = 720  \nAdding those together: 5600 + 720 = 6320. \n\nAgain, same answer. Hmm, so all these different methods are giving me 6320. That gives me more confidence that the answer is correct. \n\nLet me also check using a calculator, but since I don't have one here, maybe approximate to see if it makes sense. \n\n80 times 80 is 6400, so 79 is one less than 80. So 79 times 80 should be 6400 minus 80, which is 6320. Yep, that matches. \n\nAlternatively, if I think of 79*80 as (80 -1)*80, that's exactly what I did before. \n\nI think all these methods are converging on 6320, so I think that's the correct product. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations, and the cross-verification using different methods gives me confidence. \n\nTherefore, I can conclude that the product of 79 and 80 is 6320.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 79 and 80 is calculated as follows:\n\n1. **Breakdown Method**:  \n   \\( 79 \\times 80 = 79 \\times (8 \\times 10) = (79 \\times 8) \\times 10 \\)  \n   \\( 79 \\times 8 = 632 \\) (since \\(70 \\times 8 = 560\\) and \\(9 \\times 8 = 72\\), so \\(560 + 72 = 632\\))  \n   Thus, \\( 632 \\times 10 = 6320 \\).\n\n2. **Distributive Property**:  \n   \\( 79 \\times 80 = (80 - 1) \\times 80 = 80 \\times 80 - 1 \\times 80 \\)  \n   \\( = 6400 - 80 = 6320 \\).\n\n3. **Standard Multiplication**:  \n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n     \\quad 79 \\\\\n   \\times 80 \\\\\n   \\hline\n     \\quad 0 \\quad \\text{(79 \u00d7 0)} \\\\\n   + 6320 \\quad \\text{(79 \u00d7 80, shifted left by one digit)} \\\\\n   \\hline\n     6320 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\nAll methods confirm the result is **6320**.\n\n**Answer:** \\boxed{6320}",
