\n\nThe first diagonal (far right) has only the lower part of the bottom-right cell: 8.\n\nSecond diagonal (next to the right) includes the lower part of the top-right cell (6) and the upper part of the bottom-right cell (2). So 6 + 2 =8.\n\nThird diagonal includes the upper part of the top-right cell (1), the lower part of the bottom-left cell (3), and the upper part of the top-left cell (3). Wait, this is getting too tangled.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps arranging all the digits in the grid:\n\nThe top parts of the diagonals in each cell:\n\nFor the first row first column cell [3/6], the top part is 3, bottom is6.\n\nFirst row second column [1/6], top 1, bottom6.\n\nSecond row first column [6/3], top6, bottom3.\n\nSecond row second column [2/8], top2, bottom8.\n\nNow, the diagonals are the sums of the top-right to bottom-left diagonals.\n\nStarting from the bottom left of the grid:\n\nThe first diagonal (starting at the bottom-left cell's top part 6):\n\nWait, perhaps it's better to list all the numbers along each diagonal:\n\nThe rightmost diagonal (units place) is the bottom-right cell's bottom part: 8.\n\nThe next diagonal (tens place) is the sum of the top part of the bottom-right cell (2) and the bottom part of the top-right cell (6). 2+6=8.\n\nThe next diagonal (hundreds place) is the sum of the top part of the top-right cell (1), the bottom part of the bottom-left cell (3), and the top part of the top-left cell (3). Wait, 1 +3 +3=7?\n\nWait, let's see:\n\nActually, the diagonals should be:\n\nFirst diagonal (units): 8.\n\nSecond diagonal (tens): 2 (from [2/8]'s top) + 6 (from [1/6]'s bottom) = 8.\n\nThird diagonal (hundreds): 6 (from [6/3]'s top) + 1 (from [1/6]'s top?) Hmm, no, maybe different.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the hundreds place is the sum of the remaining top parts and bottom parts:\n\nLooking at the grid:\n\nThe top parts (above the diagonals):\n\nFirst row first column:3\n\nFirst row second column:1\n\nSecond row first column:6\n\nSecond row second column:2\n\nThe bottom parts (below the diagonals):\n\nFirst row first column:6\n\nFirst row second column:6\n\nSecond row first column:3\n\nSecond row second column:8\n\nNow, arranging them in diagonals from bottom to top:\n\nStarting from the bottom right, the first diagonal (units) is the bottom part of the last cell (8).\n\nNext diagonal (tens place):\n\nThe bottom part of the cell before that (6 from first row second column) and the top part of the cell below it (2 from second row second column). Wait, this is confusing.\n\nAlternatively, the diagonals are constructed by adding numbers along the direction where the diagonals go from top-right to bottom-left.\n\nSo:\n\nFirst diagonal (units): The very bottom-right corner's \"bottom\" part is 8. That's units.\n\nSecond diagonal (tens): The \"bottom\" part of the cell above it (6 from first row second column) and the \"top\" part of the cell to the left (2 from second row second column?), no.\n\nWait maybe better to list each diagonal's components:\n\nFirst diagonal (units place): 8.\n\nSecond diagonal (tens place): 6 (from first row second column's bottom) and 3 (from second row first column's bottom). Wait, 6 +3=9?\n\nWait, the second diagonal would be the diagonal that includes the bottom parts of the first row second column (6) and the bottom part of the second row first column (3)? Hmm, perhaps.\n\nWait, let's see:\n\nImagine the grid with the numbers written as:\n\nCell (1,1): 3 (top), 6 (bottom)\n\nCell (1,2):1 (top),6 (bottom)\n\nCell (2,1):6 (top),3 (bottom)\n\nCell (2,2):2 (top),8 (bottom)\n\nNow, if we imagine the grid as:\n\nRow 1: [3\\6] [1\\6]\n\nRow 2: [6\\3] [2\\8]\n\nThe diagonals are the diagonals going from top-right to bottom-left.\n\nStarting from the bottom-right corner (cell 2,2):\n\n- First diagonal: only the 8 (the bottom of cell 2,2).\n\n- Second diagonal: includes the bottom of cell 2,1 (3) and the top of cell 1,2 (1). Wait, no.\n\nAlternatively, the diagonals are formed by moving from the top edge to the bottom edge:\n\nThe first diagonal (units place) is the bottom-right number:8.\n\nNext diagonal (tens place): the numbers that are in the diagonal just to the left, which would be the bottom of cell (1,2)=6 and the top of cell (2,2)=2. Adding those gives 6+2=8.\n\nNext diagonal (hundreds place): the numbers in the next diagonal: bottom of cell (1,1)=6, top of cell (2,1)=6, and top of cell (1,2)=1? Wait, no.\n\nWait, maybe:\n\nThird diagonal (hundreds place) includes the bottom of cell (1,1)=6, and top of cell (2,1)=6, and the remaining?\n\nWait, perhaps:\n\nThird diagonal (hundreds place): the numbers along the diagonal that includes cell (1,1)'s bottom (6), cell (2,1)'s top (6), and cell (1,2)'s top (1). Hmm, not sure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps:\n\nThird diagonal includes cell (1,1)'s bottom (6) and cell (2,1)'s top (6). 6+6=12, and then?\n\nHmm, this is getting too confusing. Maybe I should try adding the numbers as per the lattice method's standard process.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I should just accept that since the first two methods gave me 4418, and the lattice is giving me confusion, maybe I should proceed with the previous results.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I can check using another method: 94 *47 is equal to (90+4)*(40+7)=90*40 +90*7 +4*40 +4*7= 3600+630+160+28= 3600+630 is 4230, plus 160 is 4390, plus 28 is 4418. So that's consistent.\n\nAlternatively, let me use a calculator to verify, but since I can't actually use a calculator, perhaps I can do another multiplication.\n\nAlternatively, 94 *47: Let's think of 94*47 as 47*90 +47*4.\n\n47*90: 47*9=423, so times 10 is 4230.\n\n47*4=188.\n\nAdding those together:4230+188. 4230+100=4330, +88=4418. Yep, that's the same.\n\nOkay, so all methods are leading me to 4418. So I think that's the correct answer. I guess I was just overcomplicating with the lattice method, but since the other methods confirm it, I can be confident.\n\nWait, but just to make sure, let me try another approach: using the difference of squares or something?\n\nHmm, 94 and47. Maybe not directly, but perhaps express them as numbers around a common midpoint?\n\nAlternatively, 94*47 is the same as (90 +4)*(50-3). Wait, I did that earlier. Yeah, that was the distributive approach again.\n\nAlternatively, 47 is half of 94, but 47 is actually half of 94? Wait, 47*2=94, yes! Wait, that's interesting. Wait, so if 47 is half of 94, then 94*47 = 47*2*47 = 2*(47)^2.\n\nSo, 47 squared times 2.\n\nLet me compute 47 squared first. 47 squared is (40+7)^2 = 40^2 +2*40*7 +7^2 =1600 + 560 +49= 1600+560 is 2160, plus 49 is 2209.\n\nThen, multiply by 2: 2209*2=4418. Oh, that's another way and it gives the same answer. Nice! So that's a fourth method confirming the same result.\n\nTherefore, I can be pretty sure that the product of 94 and 47 is 4418.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 94 and 47 is \\boxed{4418}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 94 and 47, we can use multiple methods to ensure accuracy.\n\n1. **Distributive Property**:\n   - Break down the numbers: \\(94 = 90 + 4\\) and \\(47 = 40 + 7\\).\n   - Expand using the distributive property:\n     \\[\n     (90 + 4)(40 + 7) = 90 \\times 40 + 90 \\times 7 + 4 \\times 40 + 4 \\times 7\n     \\]\n   - Calculate each term:\n     \\[\n     90 \\times 40 = 3600, \\quad 90 \\times 7 = 630, \\quad 4 \\times 40 = 160, \\quad 4 \\times 7 = 28\n     \\]\n   - Sum the results:\n     \\[\n     3600 + 630 + 160 + 28 = 4418\n     \\]\n\n2. **Vertical Multiplication**:\n   - Multiply 94 by 7 and then by 40 (shifted left by one position):\n     \\[\n     94 \\times 7 = 658, \\quad 94 \\times 40 = 3760\n     \\]\n   - Add the partial products:\n     \\[\n     658 + 3760 = 4418\n     \\]\n\n3. **Alternative Distributive Approach**:\n   - Express 47 as \\(50 - 3\\):\n     \\[\n     94 \\times (50 - 3) = 94 \\times 50 - 94 \\times 3\n     \\]\n   - Calculate each term:\n     \\[\n     94 \\times 50 = 4700, \\quad 94 \\times 3 = 282\n     \\]\n   - Subtract the results:\n     \\[\n     4700 - 282 = 4418\n     \\]\n\n4. **Difference of Squares**:\n   - Recognize that \\(94 = 2 \\times 47\\):\n     \\[\n     94 \\times 47 = 2 \\times 47^2\n     \\]\n   - Calculate \\(47^2\\) and then multiply by 2:\n     \\[\n     47^2 = 2209, \\quad 2 \\times 2209 = 4418\n     \\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 94 and 47 is \\(\\boxed{4418}\\).",
