      "index": 0,
      "text": "140 times 10. That would be 1400. Wait, is that right?\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do it another way to check. Like, 28 times 50. Since 50 is half of 100, maybe I can think of it as 28 times 100 divided by 2. Let's see: 28 times 100 is 2800. Divided by 2 is 1400. Okay, same answer. That's a good sign.\n\nAnother way to verify could be the standard multiplication algorithm. Let me write it out:\n\n\u2003\u00a028\n\u00d7\u00a050\n-------\nFirst, multiply 28 by 0 (the units place of 50), which is 0. Then multiply 28 by 5 (the tens place of 50, so actually 50). \n\nBut when you write it out:\n\n28 \u00d7 50:\n\nMultiplying 28 by 5 gives 140, and since the 5 is in the tens place, it's actually 50, so we add a zero at the end, making it 1400. Yep, that matches. \n\nHmm, so all methods point to 1400. Let me see if I can think of another way. Maybe using distributive property? Like 28 \u00d7 (50) = 28 \u00d7 (5 \u00d7 10) = (28 \u00d7 5) \u00d7 10, which is the same as before. \n\nAlternatively, breaking down 28 into 20 + 8. Then 20\u00d750 + 8\u00d750. 20\u00d750 is 1000, and 8\u00d750 is 400. Adding them gives 1400. Yep, that works too.\n\nWait, just making sure I didn't make a mistake in any step. Let me check each calculation again.\n\n20 times 50 is indeed 1000 because 20\u00d75 is 100, and then \u00d710 is 1000. Then 8\u00d750 is 400, since 8\u00d75 is 40, times 10 is 400. Adding 1000 and 400 is 1400. Correct.\n\nAlternatively, if I use the standard vertical multiplication:\n\n28  \n\u00d750  \n------  \nFirst, 28 \u00d7 0 = 0, written as 0.  \nThen 28 \u00d75 (but shifted one position to the left because it's the tens place), so 28\u00d75=140, shifted left becomes 1400.  \nAdding 0 + 1400 gives 1400.  \n\nAll right, so all the different methods confirm that 28 multiplied by 50 is 1400. I think that's solid. I don't see any errors in my calculations here. Maybe one more check with a calculator? But since I can't use a calculator, I can use another met