o that's another way to see it.\n\nSo all the different methods I can think of give me 1840, so I'm pretty confident that the product of 40 and 46 is 1840. I think that's the answer. Let me just write it in the box as requested.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 40 and 46 is \\boxed{1840}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 40 and 46, we can use the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n40 \\times 46 = 40 \\times (40 + 6)\n\\]\n\nBreaking it down:\n\n\\[\n40 \\times 40 = 1600\n\\]\n\\[\n40 \\times 6 = 240\n\\]\n\nAdding the results together:\n\n\\[\n1600 + 240 = 1840\n\\]\n\nAnother method using the commutative property:\n\n\\[\n46 \\times 40 = (46 \\times 4) \\times 10\n\\]\n\nCalculating \\(46 \\times 4\\):\n\n\\[\n46 \\times 4 = 184\n\\]\n\nThen multiplying by 10:\n\n\\[\n184 \\times 10 = 1840\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 40 and 46 is \\(\\boxed{1840}\\).",
