of 22) and 5 (first digit of 58) = 10. 1 above, 0 below.\n\nFourth cell (bottom-right): 2\u00d78=16. 1 above, 6 below.\n\nNow add along the diagonals:\n\nStarting from the rightmost diagonal: just the 6 from the bottom-right cell.\n\nNext diagonal: 6 (from second cell) + 0 (from fourth cell's upper part?) Wait, maybe I need to visualize better. Maybe I messed up.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I should lay it out step by step.\n\nWait, maybe lattice multiplication is confusing me here. Since other methods are giving the same answer, I think I can be confident it's 1276.\n\nAlternatively, let me just do 22*58 as (20+2)*(50+8).\n\nExpanding that: 20*50 + 20*8 + 2*50 + 2*8.\n\n20\u00d750 = 1000,\n\n20\u00d78 = 160,\n\n2\u00d750 = 100,\n\n2\u00d78 = 16.\n\nAdding all those together: 1000 + 160 is 1160, plus 100 is 1260, plus 16 is 1276. Yep, same result.\n\nAlright, so all methods are pointing to 1276. I think that's solid. Maybe one last check using a calculator approach. Since I can't use a calculator, but I can approximate.\n\n22 times 60 would be 1320. Since 58 is 2 less than 60, so subtract 2*22=44 from 1320. 1320-44 is indeed 1276. Perfect, that's the same as the earlier method.\n\nOkay, I can't see any mistakes here. All different methods confirm the product is 1276. I think that's the correct answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 22 and 58 is \\boxed{1276}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 22 and 58, we can use multiple methods to verify the result:\n\n1. **Standard Multiplication Algorithm:**\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n     \\quad 22 \\\\\n   \\times 58 \\\\\n   \\hline\n     176 \\quad (\\text{22} \\times 8) \\\\\n   +1100 \\quad (\\text{22} \\times 50) \\\\\n   \\hline\n     1276 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n   Adding the partial products \\(176\\) and \\(1100\\) gives \\(1276\\).\n\n2. **Distributive Property:**\n   \\[\n   22 \\times 58 = 22 \\times (50 + 8) = (22 \\times 50) + (22 \\times 8)\n   \\]\n   Calculating each part:\n   \\[\n   22 \\times 50 = 1100 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 22 \\times 8 = 176\n   \\]\n   Adding these results:\n   \\[\n   1100 + 176 = 1276\n   \\]\n\n3. **Alternative Breakdown:**\n   \\[\n   22 \\times 58 = 22 \\times (60 - 2) = (22 \\times 60) - (22 \\times 2)\n   \\]\n   Calculating each part:\n   \\[\n   22 \\times 60 = 1320 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 22 \\times 2 = 44\n   \\]\n   Subtracting these results:\n   \\[\n   1320 - 44 = 1276\n   \\]\n\n4. **Expanding All Terms:**\n   \\[\n   (20 + 2) \\times (50 + 8) = 20 \\times 50 + 20 \\times 8 + 2 \\times 50 + 2 \\times 8\n   \\]\n   Calculating each term:\n   \\[\n   20 \\times 50 = 1000, \\quad 20 \\times 8 = 160, \\quad 2 \\times 50 = 100, \\quad 2 \\times 8 = 16\n   \\]\n   Adding these results:\n   \\[\n   1000 + 160 + 100 + 16 = 1276\n   \\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 22 and 58 is \\(\\boxed{1276}\\).",
