s solid. Maybe I can check prime factors? Let's see. 77 is 7\u00d711, and 36 is 6\u00d76, which is 2\u00b2\u00d73\u00b2. So multiplying them together: 7\u00d711\u00d72\u00b2\u00d73\u00b2. Then multiplying all together. Let's compute step by step. 2\u00b2 is 4, 3\u00b2 is 9. So 4\u00d79=36. Then 36\u00d77=252, then 252\u00d711. 252\u00d711 is 2772. Yep, that's the same as before. So prime factors also confirm. .\n\nAlright, I think that's thorough. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations, and all the different methods cross-check to the same result. So I'm confident the product of 77 and 36 is 2772.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 77 and 36 is calculated as follows:\n\n1. **Breakdown using the distributive property:**\n   - Multiply 77 by 6 (units place of 36):\n     \\[\n     77 \\times 6 = 462\n     \\]\n   - Multiply 77 by 30 (tens place of 36, represented as \\(3 \\times 10\\)):\n     \\[\n     77 \\times 30 = 2310\n     \\]\n   - Add the partial products:\n     \\[\n     462 + 2310 = 2772\n     \\]\n\n2. **Verification using alternative methods:**\n   - **Difference of terms:**\n     \\[\n     (80 - 3) \\times 36 = 80 \\times 36 - 3 \\times 36 = 2880 - 108 = 2772\n     \\]\n   - **Prime factorization:**\n     \\[\n     77 = 7 \\times 11,\\quad 36 = 2^2 \\times 3^2\n     \\]\n     Multiplying all factors:\n     \\[\n     7 \\times 11 \\times 2^2 \\times 3^2 = (7 \\times 3^2 \\times 2^2) \\times 11 = 252 \\times 11 = 2772\n     \\]\n\n**Final Answer:** \\(\\boxed{2772}\\)",
