      "text": "1710 plus 54 is 1764. Wait, let me check again to be sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's another way to compute it. Like 36 multiplied by 50 would be 1800, but since it's 49, which is one less than 50, subtract 36 from 1800. So 1800 - 36 is indeed 1764. Yeah, that method seems quicker. That confirms the same answer. So either way, I get 1764. I think that's correct. Let me just do the standard multiplication to verify.\n\n36  \ntimes 49  \n________  \nFirst multiply 36 by 9: 36*9. 30*9=270, 6*9=54, so 270+54=324.  \nThen multiply 36 by 40 (since the 4 is in the tens place, so 4*10). So 36*40= 36*4*10. 36*4 is 144, so 144*10=1440.  \nNow add 324 and 1440 together. 324 + 1440. Let's see, 324 + 1400 is 1724, then +40 more gives 1764. Yep, same answer. So that's definitely right. 36 multiplied by 49 is 1764. I think that's solid. No mistakes in the calculations. All methods cross-check to the same result. So I'm confident that's the correct product.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 36 and 49 is calculated as follows:\n\n1. **Using the distributive property:**\n   \\[\n   36 \\times 49 = (30 + 6)(40 + 9)\n   \\]\n   Expand the terms:\n   \\[\n   = 30 \\times 40 + 30 \\times 9 + 6 \\times 40 + 6 \\times 9\n   \\]\n   Calculate each part:\n   \\[\n   = 1200 + 270 + 240 + 54\n   \\]\n   Sum the results:\n   \\[\n   1200 + 270 = 1470 \\\\\n   1470 + 240 = 1710 \\\\\n   1710 + 54 = 1764\n   \\]\n\n2. **Alternative method (breaking into known multiples):**\n   \\[\n   36 \\times 49 = 36 \\times (50 - 1) = 36 \\times 50 - 36 \\times 1\n   \\]\n   Compute:\n   \\[\n   = 1800 - 36 = 1764\n   \\]\n\n3. **Standard multiplication:**\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n      36 \\\\\n    \\times 49 \\\\\n    \\hline\n      324 \\quad (\\text{36 \u00d7 9}) \\\\\n    +1440 \\quad (\\text{36 \u00d7 40, shifted one position left}) \\\\\n    \\hline\n      1764 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n**Final Answer:**  \n\\boxed{1764}",
