      "text": "4800 + 80 + 60 + 1.\n\nWait, let me add those up step by step. 4800 + 80 is 4880. Then 4880 + 60 is 4940. Then 4940 +1 is 4941. So is the answer 4941?.\n\nHmm, but let me check another way to verify. Maybe using the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's write it out like:\n\n     81\nx   61\n-------\nFirst, multiply 81 by 1: that's 81.\n\nThen multiply 81 by 60 (since the 6 is in the tens place). So 81 times 6 is 486, and then add a zero to make it 4860.\n\nAdd those two results together: 81 + 4860. Let's see, 4860 + 80 is 4940, plus 1 is 4941. Okay, that matches the first method. So that's good.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's another method? Like using the difference of squares or something? Let me see. Wait, 81 is 9 squared, and 61 is... not sure. Hmm. Maybe not. Let's see:\n\nAlternatively, 81 multiplied by 60 would be 81*60 = 4860, then add another 81, so 4860 +81= 4941. Yep, same result. So that's three ways and they all give 4941.\n\nWait, maybe I should just do straight multiplication to confirm again:\n\n81 times 61. Let's do 81 times 61:\n\nMultiply 1 times 81: 81.\n\nMultiply 60 times 81: as above.\n\nWait, adding 81 and 4860 gives 4941. So that's consistent.\n\nAlternatively, if I use a calculator, but since I can't here, but since all methods give the same answer, I think that's solid. So I think the product of 81 and 61 is 4941. Yeah, I think that's correct.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 81 and 61 can be calculated using the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n(80 + 1)(60 + 1) = 80 \\times 60 + 80 \\times 1 + 1 \\times 60 + 1 \\times 1\n\\]\n\nBreaking it down:\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n80 \\times 60 &= 4800, \\\\\n80 \\times 1 &= 80, \\\\\n1 \\times 60 &= 60, \\\\\n1 \\times 1 &= 1.\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nAdding these results:\n\\[\n4800 + 80 + 60 + 1 = 4880 + 60 + 1 = 4940 + 1 = \\boxed{4941}.\n\\]\n\nVerification using standard multiplication:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   \\quad 81 \\\\\n\\times 61 \\\\\n\\hline\n   \\quad 81 \\quad (\\text{81 \u00d7 1}) \\\\\n+ 4860 \\quad (\\text{81 \u00d7 60, shifted left by 1 digit}) \\\\\n\\hline\n   4941 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm the result is \\(\\boxed{4941}\\).",
      "logprobs": null,
      "finish_reason": "stop",
      "stop_reason": null,
      "prompt_logprobs": null
    }
