      "text": "225 + 900 = 1125. \n\nWait, but let me double-check to make sure I didn't make any mistakes. Maybe another way to verify is by using the distributive property. Breaking down the numbers: 45 can be 40 + 5, and 25 can stay as 25. So (40 + 5) \u00d7 25. Applying distributive property: 40\u00d725 + 5\u00d725.\n\nCalculating 40\u00d725: 40 times 20 is 800, and 40 times 5 is 200, so 800 + 200 = 1000. Then 5\u00d725 is 125. Adding those together: 1000 + 125 = 1125. Okay, that matches my previous result. Good.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using another method, like the lattice method or some other trick. Wait, 25 is a quarter of 100, so maybe 45 times 25 is the same as 45 divided by 4 times 100? Let me see. 45 divided by 4 is 11.25, and then multiplied by 100 would be 1125. Yep, that also works. So that confirms it again.\n\nAlternatively, maybe breaking down 25 into 20 +5: 45\u00d720 +45\u00d75. 45\u00d720 is 900, and 45\u00d75 is 225. Adding those gives 1125. Same answer again. So all methods are giving me 1125, so I think that's correct.\n\nI guess another way to check is using a calculator, but since I don't have one here, doing multiple methods helps ensure accuracy. Since all methods give me 1125, I can be confident that's the right answer. So yeah, the product of 45 and 25 is 1125.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 45 and 25 is calculated by multiplying the two numbers together:\n\n\\[\n45 \\times 25 = 1125\n\\]\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n1. **Standard Multiplication:**\n   - Multiply 45 by 5 (ones place of 25):\n     \\[\n     45 \\times 5 = 225\n     \\]\n   - Multiply 45 by 20 (tens place of 25, which is \\(2 \\times 10\\)):\n     \\[\n     45 \\times 20 = 900\n     \\]\n   - Add the partial products:\n     \\[\n     225 + 900 = 1125\n     \\]\n\n2. **Verification via Distributive Property:**\n   - Break down \\(45 \\times 25\\) into:\n     \\[\n     (40 + 5) \\times 25 = (40 \\times 25) + (5 \\times 25)\n     \\]\n   - Calculate each part:\n     \\[\n     40 \\times 25 = 1000 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 5 \\times 25 = 125\n     \\]\n   - Sum the results:\n     \\[\n     1000 + 125 = 1125\n     \\]\n\n**Final Answer:**  \n\\boxed{1125}",
