      "text": "69 times 10?\n\nWait, yes, because I broke 30 into 3 times 10. So then I need to multiply that 69 by 10. Multiplying by 10 is straightforward, you just add a zero at the end. So 69 times 10 would be 690. \n\nLet me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Alternatively, maybe I can do the multiplication the standard way. Let's see:\n\n   23\nx 30\n-----\n?\n\nStarting with the 0 in the ones place, multiplying 23 by 0 gives 0. Then moving to the 3 in the tens place, which is actually 30, so 23 times 30. But here, when written vertically, since the 3 is in the tens place, it's 23 times 3 times 10. Wait, that's the same as before. So 23 times 3 is 69, and then shifted one place because of the 10, so 690. Yep, that matches.\n\nAlternatively, I can think of 23 times 30 as 23 times 3 times 10, which is (23\u00d73)\u00d710. Since multiplication is associative, the grouping doesn't matter. So 23\u00d73=69, then 69\u00d710=690. That seems right.\n\nAnother way to check could be to use the distributive property. Maybe breaking down 23 into 20 + 3. So (20 + 3) \u00d7 30. That would be 20\u00d730 + 3\u00d730. Calculating each part:\n\n20 times 30 is 600, because 20\u00d730 is like 2\u00d73=6 with two zeros added (one from 20 and one from 30), so 600.\n\nThen 3 times 30 is 90. Adding those together, 600 + 90 equals 690. Yep, same result. Okay, so that confirms it again.\n\nWait, maybe another way. Let me try the standard algorithm again but more carefully. Multiplying 23 by 30. Since 30 has a zero in the ones place and a 3 in the tens, so when you multiply, first multiply 23 by 0, which is 0, then multiply 23 by 3, but shifted one position to the left. So:\n\n23\n\u00d730\n------\nFirst line: 23\u00d70 = 0\nSecond line (shifted one position left): 23\u00d73 = 69 \u2192 so 690\nAdd them together: 0 + 690 = 690. Yep, same thing.\n\nHmm, seems like all methods are giving me 690. Maybe I can do a quick estimation check. 23 is close to 20, and 30 is already a round number. 20\u00d730 is 600. The actual numbers are a bit higher (since 23 is 3 more than 20), so 3 more times 30 would be an extra 90, so 600+90=690. That matches exactly. So that estimation also works out.\n\nAlternatively, if I use a calculator, but since I don't have one here, but since all my manual calculations are giving me the same answer, I think that's solid.\n\nWait, just thinking, sometimes when multiplying numbers with zeros, people sometimes miscount the zeros, but in this case, since it's only one zero in 30, and when we broke it down into 3\u00d710, we accounted for the zero by adding it at the end, so that seems okay.\n\nAnother way: 30 is 3\u00d710, so 23\u00d73\u00d710. 23\u00d73 is 69, then 69\u00d710 is 690. Yep, same steps again.\n\nI think I've verified it multiple times through different methods: breaking into parts, distributive property, standard multiplication algorithm, and estimation. All lead to 690. I don't see any mistakes in my reasoning, so I feel confident that's the correct product of 23 and 30.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 23 and 30 is \\boxed{690}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 23 and 30, we can use the following steps:\n\n1. Recognize that multiplying by 30 is the same as multiplying by 3 and then by 10.\n2. First, calculate \\(23 \\times 3\\):\n   \\[\n   23 \\times 3 = 69\n   \\]\n3. Then, multiply the result by 10:\n   \\[\n   69 \\times 10 = 690\n   \\]\n\nWe can also verify this using the distributive property:\n1. Break down 23 into 20 and 3:\n   \\[\n   (20 + 3) \\times 30\n   \\]\n2. Distribute the multiplication:\n   \\[\n   20 \\times 30 + 3 \\times 30\n   \\]\n3. Calculate each part:\n   \\[\n   20 \\times 30 = 600 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 3 \\times 30 = 90\n   \\]\n4. Add the results:\n   \\[\n   600 + 90 = 690\n   \\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 23 and 30 is \\(\\boxed{690}\\).",
