\n\nAlternatively, 13 times 90 is the same as 90 times 13. Let me reverse the multiplication. 90 times 10 is 900, and 90 times 3 is 270. Adding them gives 900 + 270 = 1170. Same result again. \n\nOkay, I think I'm confident that the product of 13 and 90 is 1170. I can't think of any other way to verify it, and all methods cross-check to the same answer. So, yeah, the answer should be 1170.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 13 and 90 is \\boxed{1170}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 13 and 90, we can use several methods to ensure accuracy:\n\n1. **Breaking Down the Numbers**:\n   - Recognize that \\(90 = 9 \\times 10\\).\n   - Calculate \\(13 \\times 9\\) first:\n     \\[\n     13 \\times 9 = (10 \\times 9) + (3 \\times 9) = 90 + 27 = 117\n     \\]\n   - Then multiply the result by 10:\n     \\[\n     117 \\times 10 = 1170\n     \\]\n\n2. **Standard Multiplication**:\n   - Multiply 13 by 0 (units place of 90) to get 0.\n   - Multiply 13 by 9 (tens place of 90, which is 90) and shift one position left:\n     \\[\n     13 \\times 9 = 117 \\quad \\text{(shifted left gives 1170)}\n     \\]\n   - Sum the partial products:\n     \\[\n     0 + 1170 = 1170\n     \\]\n\n3. **Distributive Property**:\n   - Break 13 into 10 and 3:\n     \\[\n     (10 + 3) \\times 90 = (10 \\times 90) + (3 \\times 90) = 900 + 270 = 1170\n     \\]\n\n4. **Estimation and Adjustment**:\n   - Calculate \\(13 \\times 100 = 1300\\).\n   - Subtract \\(13 \\times 10 = 130\\):\n     \\[\n     1300 - 130 = 1170\n     \\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 13 and 90 is \\(\\boxed{1170}\\).",
