      "text": "4500 + 12 = 4512? Wait, no, let me check that again.\n\nAlternatively, 4700 minus 188. Let's subtract 200 first: 4700 - 200 = 4500. But since I subtracted 12 more than needed (because 200 - 188 = 12), I have to add back 12. So 4500 +12 = 4512. That makes sense. So the answer is 4512?\n\nWait a second, let me verify that with another method to be sure. Maybe doing the multiplication the standard way.\n\nMultiplying 47 by 96:\n\nFirst, write it as:\n\n\u2003\u00a047\n\u00d7 96\n-------\n?\n\nStarting with the 6 (units place) times 47: 6*7=42, write down 2 and carry over 4. Then 6*4=24 plus the 4 is 28. So that gives 282.\n\nThen, the 9 (tens place) times 47, but shifted one position to the left. So 9*7=63, write down 3 and carry over 6. Then 9*4=36 plus 6 is 42. So that gives 4230 (since it's shifted left by one).\n\nNow add the two results: 282 + 4230. Let's see, 282 + 4230. 282 + 4000 is 4282, then add 230 more: 4282 +230 = 4512. Okay, so same result. So that's good.\n\nAlternatively, maybe check using another method. Let me see. 47*96. Maybe using the distributive property another way. For instance, 40*96 + 7*96. Let's try that.\n\n40*96: 40*90 is 3600, and 40*6 is 240, so total 3600 +240 = 3840.\n\nThen 7*96: 7*90 is 630, and 7*6 is 42. So 630+42=672.\n\nAdding those together: 3840 + 672. 3840 + 600 is 4440, plus 72 is 4512. Yep, same answer again.\n\nHmm, so all methods are pointing to 4512. I think that's solid. But just to make sure, maybe divide the answer by one of the numbers to see if it gives the other. Let's divide 4512 by 47 and see if we get 96.\n\nDividing 4512 \u00f7 47. Let's see how many times 47 goes into 4512.\n\nFirst, 47*90 is 4230. Subtract that from 4512: 4512 - 4230 = 282. Then, 47*6 is 282. So total is 90 +6 = 96. Perfect, that works out. So division checks out.\n\nAlternatively, 4512 \u00f7 96. Let me see if that gives 47. 96*40 is 3840. Subtract that from 4512: 4512 - 3840 = 672. Then 96*7 is 672. So 40+7 =47. Yep, that works too.\n\nAlright, so all methods confirm that 47 multiplied by 96 is indeed 4512. I think that's the correct answer. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations, and the cross-verification by division worked. So I'm confident.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 47 and 96 is \\boxed{4512}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 47 and 96, we can use the distributive property by breaking down 96 into 100 - 4:\n\n\\[\n47 \\times 96 = 47 \\times (100 - 4)\n\\]\n\nCalculating each part separately:\n\n\\[\n47 \\times 100 = 4700\n\\]\n\\[\n47 \\times 4 = 188\n\\]\n\nSubtracting these results:\n\n\\[\n4700 - 188 = 4512\n\\]\n\nTo verify, we can use the standard multiplication method:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   \\quad 47 \\\\\n\\times 96 \\\\\n\\hline\n   282 \\quad (\\text{47} \\times 6) \\\\\n+4230 \\quad (\\text{47} \\times 90, \\text{shifted one position left}) \\\\\n\\hline\n   4512 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nAnother verification using distributive property in another form:\n\n\\[\n47 \\times 96 = (40 + 7) \\times 96 = 40 \\times 96 + 7 \\times 96\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n\\[\n40 \\times 96 = 3840\n\\]\n\\[\n7 \\times 96 = 672\n\\]\n\nAdding these results:\n\n\\[\n3840 + 672 = 4512\n\\]\n\nCross-verifying by division:\n\n\\[\n4512 \\div 47 = 96 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 4512 \\div 96 = 47\n\\]\n\nAll methods confirm the result is correct. The product of 47 and 96 is \\boxed{4512}.",
