.\n\nAlternatively, 96 * 57 can be thought of as 96*(50 +7) = 96*50 + 96*7.\n\nCalculating 96*50: 96*5=480, so times 10 is 4800.\n\n96*7: 90*7=630 and 6*7=42, so 630+42=672.\n\nAdding them together: 4800 + 672 = 5472. Yep, same answer.\n\nAlternatively, 57*96 is the same as 57*(100 -4) = 57*100 - 57*4 = 5700 - 228 = 5472. Exactly as the first method.\n\nHmm, so all methods are converging to 5472. I think that's solid. But just to be extra sure, perhaps do the multiplication step by step again.\n\nLet me write it out vertically again:\n\n\u2003\u200396  \n\u00d7\u00a057  \n------  \n\u2003672  (96 \u00d7 7)  \n+4800 (96 \u00d7 50)  \n------  \n\u20035472  \n\nYes, that's correct. So the product is indeed 5472. I don't see any errors in my calculations, and cross-verifying with different methods all lead to the same result. Therefore, I can be confident the answer is 5472.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 96 and 57 is \\boxed{5472}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 96 and 57, we can use the distributive property by breaking down the numbers into smaller parts.\n\nFirst, we rewrite 96 as \\(100 - 4\\):\n\\[\n(100 - 4) \\times 57\n\\]\n\nUsing the distributive property:\n\\[\n100 \\times 57 - 4 \\times 57\n\\]\n\nCalculating each part separately:\n\\[\n100 \\times 57 = 5700\n\\]\n\\[\n4 \\times 57 = 228\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the results:\n\\[\n5700 - 228 = 5472\n\\]\n\nTo verify, we can use the standard multiplication method:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   \\quad 96 \\\\\n\\times 57 \\\\\n\\hline\n   672 \\quad (\\text{from } 96 \\times 7) \\\\\n+4800 \\quad (\\text{from } 96 \\times 50) \\\\\n\\hline\n   5472 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 96 and 57 is \\(\\boxed{5472}\\).",
