is 594. That's simple too. \n\nSo, all these different methods are giving me the same result. I think that's solid. \n\nI guess the answer is 594. I don't see any errors in my calculations. Let me just recap once more. \n\nMultiplying 54 by 11:\n\nFirst, 54*10 is 540, plus 54 is 594. \n\nOr, using the 11-trick: 5 and 4 add to 9, so 5 9 4. \n\nDivision check: 594 divided by 11 is indeed 54. \n\nAll right, that seems solid. I think that's the correct product.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 11 and 54 is \\boxed{594}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 11 and 54, we can use several methods to ensure accuracy:\n\n1. **Standard Multiplication**:\n   - Multiply 54 by 1 (units place of 11): \\(54 \\times 1 = 54\\)\n   - Multiply 54 by 10 (tens place of 11): \\(54 \\times 10 = 540\\)\n   - Add the results: \\(54 + 540 = 594\\)\n\n2. **11-Multiplication Trick**:\n   - For a two-digit number \\(AB\\), multiplying by 11 gives \\(A(A+B)B\\). Here, \\(A = 5\\) and \\(B = 4\\).\n   - Sum of digits: \\(5 + 4 = 9\\)\n   - Result: \\(594\\)\n\n3. **Verification by Division**:\n   - Divide 594 by 11: \\(594 \\div 11 = 54\\), confirming the product is correct.\n\n4. **Breaking Down the Numbers**:\n   - \\(54 \\times 11 = 54 \\times (10 + 1) = 54 \\times 10 + 54 \\times 1 = 540 + 54 = 594\\)\n\nAll methods consistently yield the result 594. \n\nThe product of 11 and 54 is \\(\\boxed{594}\\).",
