messages:  [['Please solve the math problem.\nQuestion: What is the Product of 66 and 73?', "<think>:.\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 66 and 73. Hmm, let me think about the best way to do this. I remember that multiplying two numbers can sometimes be done more easily by breaking them down into smaller parts. Maybe using the distributive property? Let me try that.\n\nFirst, I can write 66 as 60 + 6, and 73 as 70 + 3. So, the problem becomes (60 + 6) multiplied by (70 + 3). To use the distributive property, I have to multiply each part of the first number by each part of the second number and then add them all together. Let me write that out step by step.\n\nStarting with 60 times 70. That should be straightforward. 60 times 70 is.. Well, 6 times 7 is 42, and then I have to add the two zeros from 60 and 70, so that makes 4200. Okay, got that part.\n\nNext, 60 times 3. That's easier. 60 times 3 is 180. Alright, moving on to the next term in the first parentheses. That's 6 times 70. 6 times 70 is 420. And finally, the last part is 6 times 3, which is 18. .\n\nNow I need to add all those results together. Let me list them again to make sure I don't miss any:.\n\n- 60 * 70 = 4200.\n- 60 * 3 = 180.\n- 6 * 70 = 420.\n- 6 * 3 = 18.\n\nAdding them up step by step. Let's start with 4200 + 180. That's 4380. Then, adding 420 to that gives me 4380 + 420. Hmm, 4380 + 400 would be 4780, and then +20 more is 4800. Wait, that seems off. Let me check again. 4380 + 420. 4380 + 400 is 4780, plus 20 more is 4800. Yeah, that's right. Then add the last term, which is 18. Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
