So, if I let a=60, b=6, c=70, d=3, then it's exactly what I did first. So that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe breaking down 73 into 72 +1. Then, 66*(72 +1)=66*72 +66. Let me try that. 66*72. Hmm, 66*70 is 4620, and 66*2 is 132. So 4620 +132 = 4752. Then add 66: 4752 +66 = 4818. Yep, same result.\n\nAnother way: 66 *73. Let me think of 73 as 70 +3, so 66*(70+3) = 66*70 +66*3. That's exactly what I did before. So, 66*70 is 4620, plus 198 is 4818.\n\nHmm, all these different methods are giving me the same answer, so I think it's safe to say that the product is 4818. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations. Let me just go through the steps once more quickly to confirm.\n\nFirst method:\n\n60*70 = 4200\n\n60*3 = 180 \u2192 4200 + 180 = 4380\n\n6*70 = 420 \u2192 4380 + 420 = 4800\n\n6*3 = 18 \u2192 4800 + 18 = 4818 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nSecond method:\n\n66  \n\u00d773  \n------  \n198 (from 3\u00d766)  \n4620 (from 70\u00d766, shifted one position to the left)  \n------  \nTotal: 198 + 4620 = 4818 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nThird method:\n\n66*70 = 4620  \n66*3 = 198  \nTotal: 4620 + 198 = 4818 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nFourth method:\n\n66*(72+1) = 66*72 +66  \n66*72 = (66*(70+2)) = 4620 +132 = 4752  \n4752 +66 = 4818 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nAlright, all methods confirm that the product is indeed 4818. I think that's solid. So the answer should be 4818.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 66 and 73 is \\boxed{4818}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 66 and 73, we can use the distributive property by breaking down the numbers into smaller parts:\n\n1. Break down 66 into 60 + 6 and 73 into 70 + 3:\n   \\[\n   (60 + 6)(70 + 3)\n   \\]\n\n2. Apply the distributive property:\n   \\[\n   60 \\times 70 + 60 \\times 3 + 6 \\times 70 + 6 \\times 3\n   \\]\n\n3. Calculate each term:\n   \\[\n   60 \\times 70 = 4200\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   60 \\times 3 = 180\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   6 \\times 70 = 420\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   6 \\times 3 = 18\n   \\]\n\n4. Sum the results of each term:\n   \\[\n   4200 + 180 + 420 + 18