0. 1144 -1100 =44. 44 divided by 22 is 2. So yes, 50+2=52. That works. \n\nAlternatively, 1144 divided by 52. Let me compute that. 52 \u00d720 =1040. 1144-1040=104. 52\u00d72=104. So total 20 +2=22. Perfect, so that confirms it. \n\nTherefore, after multiple methods and checks, I can be sure that the product of 52 and 22 is indeed 1144.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 52 and 22 is \\boxed{1144}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 52 and 22, we can use multiple methods to ensure accuracy:\n\n1. **Standard Algorithm**:\n   - Multiply 52 by 2 (units place of 22):\n     \\[\n     52 \\times 2 = 104\n     \\]\n   - Multiply 52 by 20 (tens place of 22, shifted one position left):\n     \\[\n     52 \\times 20 = 1040\n     \\]\n   - Add the partial products:\n     \\[\n     104 + 1040 = 1144\n     \\]\n\n2. **Distributive Property**:\n   - Break down 22 into 20 and 2:\n     \\[\n     52 \\times 22 = 52 \\times (20 + 2) = (52 \\times 20) + (52 \\times 2)\n     \\]\n   - Calculate each part:\n     \\[\n     52 \\times 20 = 1040 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 52 \\times 2 = 104\n     \\]\n   - Add the results:\n     \\[\n     1040 + 104 = 1144\n     \\]\n\n3. **Alternative Breakdown**:\n   - Break down both numbers into smaller parts:\n     \\[\n     (50 + 2) \\times (20 + 2) = 50 \\times 20 + 50 \\times 2 + 2 \\times 20 + 2 \\times 2\n     \\]\n   - Calculate each term:\n     \\[\n     50 \\times 20 = 1000, \\quad 50 \\times 2 = 100, \\quad 2 \\times 20 = 40, \\quad 2 \\times 2 = 4\n     \\]\n   - Sum the terms:\n     \\[\n     1000 + 100 + 40 + 4 = 1144\n     \\]\n\n4. **Verification by Division**:\n   - Divide the result by one of the factors to check:\n     \\[\n     1144 \\div 22 = 52 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 1144 \\div 52 = 22\n     \\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 52 and 22 is \\(\\boxed{1144}\\).",
