      "text": "720 plus 36 is 756. \n\nWait, let me double-check to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Alternatively, I can use another method to confirm. Maybe using the standard algorithm for multiplication. Writing it down:\n\n\u2003\u00a063  \n\u00d7\u00a012  \n\u2014\u2014\u2014  \nFirst, multiply 63 by 2, which is 126.  \nThen multiply 63 by 10 (since the 1 in 12 is in the tens place), so that's 630.  \nAdding those two results: 126 + 630. Let's see, 126 plus 600 is 726, then plus 30 more gives 756. Okay, same result. That matches my first method. \n\nJust to be thorough, perhaps I can do it another way. Maybe breaking down 12 into 10 + 2. So, (10 + 2) times 63. Which would be 10\u00d763 + 2\u00d763. \n\n10 times 63 is 630, and 2 times 63 is 126. Adding those together again gives 630 + 126 = 756. Yep, still the same answer. \n\nI think that's solid. All methods are giving me 756. I don't see any errors in my calculations. Let me just confirm once more with a calculator approach. 12 multiplied by 63. \n\n12 \u00d7 60 is 720, 12 \u00d73 is 36. 720 +36 is 756. Yep, that's consistent. Alright, so I'm confident the product is 756.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 12 and 63 is calculated by multiplying the two numbers together:\n\n\\[\n12 \\times 63 = 756\n\\]\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n1. **Break down using distributive property:**\n   \\[\n   12 \\times 63 = 12 \\times (60 + 3) = (12 \\times 60) + (12 \\times 3)\n   \\]\n2. **Calculate each part:**\n   - \\(12 \\times 60 = 720\\)\n   - \\(12 \\times 3 = 36\\)\n3. **Add the results:**\n   \\[\n   720 + 36 = 756\n   \\]\n\n**Final Answer:** \\(\\boxed{756}\\)",
