\nTop right cell: 3*9=27, so 2 on top and 7 on bottom. \n\nBottom left cell: 2*2=4, so 0 and 4? Wait, no. Wait, actually, the second number is 2 (tens place) and 9 (ones). So actually, the right side is 2 (top) and 9 (bottom). Wait, maybe I got the orientation wrong. \n\nWait, let me recall the lattice setup. The first number is across the top, and the second number is down the right side, each digit in its own box. \n\nSo for 32 x 29:\n\nTop: 3 (hundreds) and 2 (ones) ?\n\nWait, no. The first number is 32, so split into tens and ones: 3 (tens) and 2 (ones). The second number is 29: 2 (tens) and 9 (ones). \n\nSo the grid is set up with 3 and 2 on the top, and 2 and 9 on the right. \n\nEach cell is divided by a diagonal. \n\nFirst cell (top left): 3*2=6, so write 6 above the diagonal.\n\nSecond cell (top right): 3*9=27, so 2 above and 7 below the diagonal.\n\nThird cell (bottom left): 2*2=4, so 0 above and 4 below? Wait, no. Wait, actually, each digit of the first number (3 and 2) is multiplied by each digit of the second number (2 and 9). \n\nWait, actually, maybe I should have the first number along the top and the second number on the right side, with digits going from top to bottom and left to right. \n\nLet me try again. \n\nThe grid has two columns for the first number (32: 3 and 2) and two rows for the second number (29: 2 and 9). \n\nSo the cells are:\n\nRow 1 (for the 2 in 29's tens place):\n\nColumn 1: 3*2=6 \u2192 top left cell has 6 on the top right?\n\nWait, maybe it's better to visualize:\n\nEach cell's top-left to bottom-right diagonal splits it. The digits of the product go above and below the diagonal. \n\nSo for the first cell (3 * 2):\n\nMultiply 3 (tens place of first number) and 2 (tens place of second number). The product is 6, which is a single digit. So we put 0 above the diagonal and 6 below? Or 6 above and 0 below? Hmm, maybe I'm confused here. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps each multiplication result is split into tens and units. For example, 3*2 =6. Since 6 is a single digit, the tens part is 0 and units is 6. So the upper triangle is tens, the lower triangle is units. So 0 and 6. \n\nThen next cell: 3 (from first number's tens) * 9 (from second number's ones). 3*9=27. So upper triangle (tens place) 2, lower triangle (units) 7.\n\nNext row, second row (for the 9 in the second number's ones place):\n\nFirst cell: 2 (first number's ones place) * 2 (second number's tens place) =4. So upper 0, lower 4.\n\nSecond cell: 2 (first number's ones) *9 (second number's ones) =18. Upper triangle 1, lower 8.\n\nNow, the grid looks like this:\n\nTop left cell: 0 | 6  \nTop right cell: 2 |7  \nBottom left: 0 |4  \nBottom right:1 |8  \n\nWait, actually, arranging them:\n\nThe grid would be:\n\nFirst row (from the top number's first digit, 3):\n\nFirst cell (3*2): upper 0, lower 6.\n\nSecond cell (3*9): upper 2, lower 7.\n\nSecond row (from the top number's second digit, 2):\n\nFirst cell (2*2): upper 0, lower 4.\n\nSecond cell (2*9): upper 1, lower 8.\n\nNow, to read the result, you add the diagonals from right to left.\n\nThe rightmost diagonal is just the lower right cell's lower part: 8.\n\nNext diagonal: the lower part of the top right (7) and the upper part of the bottom right (1). So 7 +1 =8.\n\nNext diagonal: the lower part of the bottom left (4), the upper part of the bottom right (1?), wait no. Wait, let me see:\n\nActually, the diagonals are formed by the upper and lower parts. Let me think again.\n\nThe diagonals are as follows:\n\nThe first (rightmost) diagonal is the lower triangle of the last column. So the bottom number in the top right cell (7) and the bottom number in the bottom right cell (8)? Wait no. Maybe I need to consider each diagonal from bottom to top.\n\nWait, perhaps I need to visualize the grid with the diagonals. Let me try:\n\nThe grid is arranged such that each cell's division allows the diagonals to be summed vertically. The rightmost diagonal is the lower right corner of the bottom right