can use the distributive property by breaking down the numbers into smaller parts:\n\n\\[\n27 \\times 43 = (20 + 7) \\times (40 + 3)\n\\]\n\nExpanding this using the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n(20 + 7) \\times (40 + 3) = 20 \\times 40 + 20 \\times 3 + 7 \\times 40 + 7 \\times 3\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term separately:\n\n\\[\n20 \\times 40 = 800\n\\]\n\\[\n20 \\times 3 = 60\n\\]\n\\[\n7 \\times 40 = 280\n\\]\n\\[\n7 \\times 3 = 21\n\\]\n\nAdding these results together:\n\n\\[\n800 + 60 + 280 + 21 = 860 + 280 + 21 = 1140 + 21 = 1161\n\\]\n\nWe can also verify this by multiplying vertically:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   27 \\\\\n\\times 43 \\\\\n\\hline\n   81 \\quad (\\text{27} \\times 3) \\\\\n+1080 \\quad (\\text{27} \\times 40) \\\\\n\\hline\n  1161 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nAnother method using the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n27 \\times 43 = 27 \\times (40 + 3) = 27 \\times 40 + 27 \\times 3 = 1080 + 81 = 1161\n\\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 27 and 43 is \\(\\boxed{1161}\\).",
