erent methods lead to 255. I think that's solid. Maybe check with a calculator? But since I can't use a calculator, but I can verify with division. Let's see, if 255 divided by 15 should be 17. Let's do that division. 15\u00d717=255, so 255 \u00f715. 15 goes into 25 once, remainder 10. Bring down 5, making 105. 15 goes into 105 exactly 7 times. So yes, 17. That checks out. Similarly, 255 divided by 17 should be 15. Let's see. 17\u00d715 is 255. 17\u00d710 is 170, 17\u00d75 is 85, add them to get 255. So division checks out. .\n\nTherefore, I'm confident the answer is 255.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 15 and 17 is calculated as follows:\n\n\\[\n15 \\times 17 = (10 + 5) \\times 17 = 10 \\times 17 + 5 \\times 17 = 170 + 85 = 255\n\\]\n\n**Answer:** 255",
