      "text": "2900 + 26 = 2926. Wait, hold on, let me check that addition again. 2310 plus 600 is 2910, and then adding the remaining 16 gives 2910 +16 = 2926. That seems right. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do it another way to cross-verify. Let me try multiplying 77 by 38 using another method. Maybe the standard algorithm?\n\nLet me write it out vertically:\n\n\u200377  \n\u00d738  \n------  \nFirst, multiply 77 by 8. As before, that's 616.  \nThen, multiply 77 by 30. Wait, actually in the standard algorithm, it's 77 multiplied by 3 (tens place), so shifted one position to the left. So 77\u00d73 is 231, but since it's the tens place, it's 2310. Then add 616 + 2310, which is the same as before, giving 2926. Okay, that matches. \n\nAnother way: Maybe using the difference of squares? Hmm, not sure if that applies here. Let me see. Alternatively, perhaps breaking down both numbers into smaller components?\n\nWait, 77 is 70 +7, and 38 is 30 +8. So, expanding (70+7)(30+8). That would be 70\u00d730 + 70\u00d78 +7\u00d730 +7\u00d78. Let me compute each term:\n\n70\u00d730 = 2100,\n\n70\u00d78 = 560,\n\n7\u00d730 = 210,\n\n7\u00d78 = 56.\n\nAdding those up: 2100 + 560 = 2660, plus 210 is 2870, plus 56 is 2926. Yep, same result. Okay, so that's consistent.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using another approach like lattice multiplication? Let me try that quickly.\n\nSetting up a lattice grid for 77\u00d738. Since both numbers are two digits, the grid would be 2x2. The top would be 7 and 7 for 77, and the right side would be 3 and 8 for 38 (but actually, wait, lattice multiplication usually has the digits of the second number on the right side. Wait, let me recall the exact steps.)\n\nWait, perhaps I should write 77 on the top and 38 on the right, each digit in a column or row. Hmm, maybe this is getting too complicated. Since the other methods all gave me 2926, I can be confident.\n\nAlternatively, maybe check using a calculator? Wait, since I don't have a calculator here, but let me do a quick estimation to see if 2926 makes sense. 77 is close to 80, 38 is close to 40. 80\u00d740 is 3200. Since both original numbers are a bit less than those estimates, 2926 is a reasonable answer, as it's lower than 3200. \n\nAlternatively, think of 77\u00d738 as (70+7)(30+8) as before, which I already did. All methods seem to converge on 2926. I think that's the correct answer. Let me just verify once more with step-by-step addition:\n\nCalculating 77\u00d738:\n\nFirst, 77\u00d730:\n\n77\u00d710 = 770,\n\n77\u00d730 = 770\u00d73 = 2310.\n\nThen 77\u00d78:\n\n77\u00d78: 70\u00d78=560, 7\u00d78=56, total 616.\n\nAdding 2310 + 616:\n\n2310 + 600 = 2910,\n\n2910 +16 = 2926. Yep, that's correct. I don't see any errors in my calculations. So, the product of 77 and 38 is 2926.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 77 and 38 is \\boxed{2926}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 77 and 38, we can use the distributive property by breaking down the numbers into smaller components:\n\n1. Split 38 into 30 and 8:\n   \\[\n   77 \\times 38 = 77 \\times (30 + 8)\n   \\]\n\n2. Calculate \\(77 \\times 30\\):\n   \\[\n   77 \\times 30 = 77 \\times 3 \\times 10 = 231 \\times 10 = 2310\n   \\]\n\n3. Calculate \\(77 \\times 8\\):\n   \\[\n   77 \\times 8 = (70 + 7) \\times 8 = 70 \\times 8 + 7 \\times 8 = 560 + 56 = 616\n   \\]\n\n4. Add the results of the two products:\n   \\[\n   2310 + 616 = 2926\n   \\]\n\nVerification using the standard algorithm:\n- Multiply 77 by 8: \\(77 \\times 8 = 616\\)\n- Multiply 77 by 30 (shifted one position to the left): \\(77 \\times 30 = 2310\\)\n- Add the results: \\(2310 + 616 = 2926\\)\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 77 and 38 is \\(\\boxed{2926}\\).",
