 But since this is the tens place, the 54 is actually in the hundreds and tens place, so we write 540 shifted left by one, making it 5400. \n\nAdding 360 + 5400 gives 5760. \n\nAll right, so that's consistent again. \n\nAlternatively, maybe use a calculator to verify? But since I can't use a calculator, I can try another method. Let's see, if I approximate. 90 times 60 is 5400, and 90 times 4 is 360, so adding them gives 5760. \n\nAlternatively, 64 times 100 is 6400. Subtract 64 times 10 (since 100-90=10). 64\u00d710=640. So 6400-640=5760. That works too. \n\nWait, that's another method. Let me explain: 90 is 100 minus 10. So 64\u00d7(100-10)=64\u00d7100 -64\u00d710=6400 -640=5760. \n\nThat also gives the same answer. \n\nTherefore, after multiple methods and cross-verification, I believe the product of 90 and 64 is 5760. \n\nI don't see any mistakes in my calculations, and all the different approaches lead to the same result. So I can be confident that 5760 is the correct answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 90 and 64 is \\boxed{5760}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 90 and 64, we can use the distributive property and standard multiplication steps:\n\n1. **Using the distributive property:**\n   \\[\n   90 \\times 64 = 90 \\times (60 + 4) = (90 \\times 60) + (90 \\times 4)\n   \\]\n   - Calculate \\(90 \\times 60\\):\n     \\[\n     90 \\times 60 = 5400\n     \\]\n   - Calculate \\(90 \\times 4\\):\n     \\[\n     90 \\times 4 = 360\n     \\]\n   - Add the results:\n     \\[\n     5400 + 360 = 5760\n     \\]\n\n2. **Using standard multiplication steps:**\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n     \\quad 90 \\\\\n   \\times 64 \\\\\n   \\hline\n     360 \\quad (\\text{90} \\times 4) \\\\\n   +5400 \\quad (\\text{90} \\times 60) \\\\\n   \\hline\n     5760 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Alternative method using commutative property:**\n   \\[\n   64 \\times 90 = (64 \\times 9) \\times 10\n   \\]\n   - Calculate \\(64 \\times 9\\):\n     \\[\n     64 \\times