      "text": "1680 plus 36? Wait, no, that's not right. Wait, hold on, I think I messed up the addition. Let me check again.\n\nWait, no, actually, after 1200 + 360 is 1560. Then adding the next term, which is 120, so 1560 + 120 is indeed 1680. Then adding the last term, 36, so 1680 + 36 is 1716. Oh, I see, I almost forgot the last part. So the total would be 1716. Wait, so that's the answer?\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a quicker way. Let me try another method to verify. Maybe using the standard multiplication algorithm.\n\nMultiplying 44 by 39. Let me write it down vertically:\n\n\u2003\u00a044\n\u00d739\n------\n\nStarting with the units place: 9 times 44. 9*4 is 36, write down 6 and carry over 3. Then 9*4 is 36 plus the carried 3 is 39. So that gives 396.\n\nThen, moving to the tens place: 3 (which is actually 30) times 44. So 3*4 is 12, write down 2 and carry over 1. Then 3*4 is 12 plus the 1 is 13. So that gives 132, but since it's the tens place, we need to shift one position to the left, so it's 1320.\n\nNow add the two results: 396 + 1320. Let's see, 396 + 1320. 396 + 1300 is 1696, plus 20 more is 1716. Okay, that matches the previous result. So that's good.\n\nWait, so both methods give me 1716. Hmm, but let me try another way just to be sure. Maybe using some other distributive approach.\n\nAlternatively, 44*39 can be thought of as (40 + 4)*(40 - 1). Wait, no, 39 is 40 minus 1? Wait, no, 40 minus 1 is 39, yes. Wait, but that would be (40 +4)*(40 -1). Hmm, maybe expanding that. Let me see:\n\n(40 +4)*(40 -1) = 40*(40 -1) +4*(40 -1) = 40*39 +4*39. Wait, but that might not help. Alternatively, maybe there's a different way to split the numbers.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps use the fact that 44*39 = 44*(40 -1) = 44*40 -44*1. Let's try that. 44 times 40 is 1760, and subtract 44, so 1760 -44 is 1716. Yes, that works too. So that method also gives 1716. Okay, so three different methods all leading to the same answer. That gives me confidence that the product is indeed 1716.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use some calculator? But since I can't do that here, I can check with another approach. Let me do the multiplication step by step again.\n\n44 multiplied by 39.\n\nFirst, 44 times 30 is 1320, and 44 times 9 is 396. Adding them together: 1320 + 396. Let's see:\n\n1320 + 300 is 1620, then plus 96 is 1716. Yep, same answer again.\n\nHmm, so all the methods I try give me 1716, so I think that's the correct product. Therefore, I can conclude that 44 multiplied by 39 equals 1716.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 44 and 39 is \\boxed{1716}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 44 and 39, we can use the distributive property and verify with different methods:\n\n1. **Distributive Property Method:**\n   \\[\n   (40 + 4) \\times (30 + 9) = 40 \\times 30 + 40 \\times 9 + 4 \\times 30 + 4 \\times 9\n   \\]\n   Calculating each term:\n   \\[\n   40 \\times 30 = 1200\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   40 \\times 9 = 360\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   4 \\times 30 = 120\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   4 \\times 9 = 36\n   \\]\n   Adding all terms together:\n   \\[\n   1200 + 360 + 120 + 36 = 1716\n   \\]\n\n2. **Standard Multiplication Algorithm:**\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n     \\quad 44 \\\\\n   \\times 39 \\\\\n   \\hline\n     396 \\quad (\\text{44} \\times 9) \\\\\n   +1320 \\quad (\\text{44} \\times 30 \\text{ shifted one position left}) \\\\\n   \\hline\n     1716 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Alternative Distributive Approach:**\n   \\[\n   44 \\times 39 = 44 \\times (40 - 1) = 44 \\times 40 - 44 \\times 1\n   \\]\n   Calculating each term:\n   \\[\n   44 \\times 40 = 1760\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   1760 - 44 = 1716\n   \\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 44 and 39 is \\(\\boxed{1716}\\).",
