      "text": "6270 + 28. Let me calculate that. 6270 + 20 is 6290, and then +8 is 6298.\n\nWait, hold on. Let me check that again because sometimes when adding step by step, it's easy to make a mistake. Let me verify each step:\n\nFirst term: 90*60=5400, correct.\n\nSecond term: 90*7=630, that's right.\n\nThird term: 4*60=240, yes.\n\nFourth term: 4*7=28, correct.\n\nNow adding them all together:\n\n5400 + 630 is indeed 6030. Then 6030 +240: Let's see, 6000 + 240 is 6240, plus 30 is 6270. Then 6270 +28 is 6298. Hmm, so the total is 6298?\n\nWait, but let me check with another method to be sure. Maybe I can do the multiplication the standard way.\n\nMultiplying 94 by 67:\n\nFirst, write 94\n\nx67\n\n--------\n\nMultiply 94 by 7: 94*7. Let's see, 90*7=630 and 4*7=28, so 630+28=658.\n\nThen multiply 94 by 60. Wait, since the 6 is in the tens place, so it's actually 94*60. So 94*6=564, then times 10 is 5640.\n\nThen add 658 and 5640 together.\n\n658 + 5640. Let's see, 5640 + 600 is 6240, then +58 is 6298. Okay, that matches the previous result. So that seems right.\n\nAlternatively, maybe do 94 times 67 as (90 +4)(60 +7). Wait, that's exactly what I did first.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use another method like the lattice method or something else? Hmm, perhaps overkill. Alternatively, use a calculator? Well, since I can't use a calculator, but let me see.\n\nAlternatively, maybe break it down differently. Let's see, 94 times 67. Maybe 94 times 70 minus 94 times 3. Wait, that might be another way. Let me try that.\n\n94*(70 - 3) = 94*70 - 94*3. Wait, but 67 is 70 minus 3, so that's another approach. Let's see:\n\n94*70: 94*7=658, so times 10 is 6580. Then subtract 94*3. 94*3 is 282. So 6580 -282 = ?\n\n6580 minus 200 is 6380, minus 82 more is 6380 -82 = 6298. Yep, same answer again. Okay, so that's good.\n\nSo, all three methods give me 6298, so I think that's the right answer. Maybe I was just overthinking, but better safe than sorry. Let me just do one more check.\n\nAlternatively, add 94 sixty-seven times, but that's tedious. Alternatively, think in terms of 100 times 67 minus 6 times 67. Because 94 is 100 minus 6.\n\nSo, 100*67 = 6700\n\nMinus 6*67. 6*60 is 360, 6*7 is 42, so 360+42=402. Therefore, 6700 - 402 = ?\n\n6700 - 400 is 6300, minus 2 more is 6298. Yep, same answer again. Alright, so that's four different methods, all giving me 6298. I think that's solid.\n\nSo the product of 94 and 67 is 6298.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 94 and 67 is \\boxed{6298}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 94 and 67, we can use the distributive property by breaking down the numbers into smaller parts:\n\n\\[\n(90 + 4)(60 + 7)\n\\]\n\nExpanding this using the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n90 \\times 60 + 90 \\times 7 + 4 \\times 60 + 4 \\times 7\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term separately:\n\n1. \\(90 \\times 60 = 5400\\)\n2. \\(90 \\times 7 = 630\\)\n3. \\(4 \\times 60 = 240\\)\n4. \\(4 \\times 7 = 28\\)\n\nAdding these results together:\n\n\\[\n5400 + 630 + 240 + 28\n\\]\n\nFirst, add \\(5400 + 630\\):\n\n\\[\n5400 + 630 = 6030\n\\]\n\nNext, add \\(6030 + 240\\):\n\n\\[\n6030 + 240 = 6270\n\\]\n\nFinally, add \\(6270 + 28\\):\n\n\\[\n6270 + 28 = 6298\n\\]\n\nTo verify, we can use another method by breaking down the multiplication:\n\n\\[\n94 \\times 67 = 94 \\times (70 - 3) = 94 \\times 70 - 94 \\times 3\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n1. \\(94 \\times 70 = 6580\\)\n2. \\(94 \\times 3 = 282\\)\n\nSubtracting these results:\n\n\\[\n6580 - 282 = 6298\n\\]\n\nAnother verification method using \\(100 - 6\\):\n\n\\[\n94 \\times 67 = (100 - 6) \\times 67 = 100 \\times 67 - 6 \\times 67\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n1. \\(100 \\times 67 = 6700\\)\n2. \\(6 \\times 67 = 402\\)\n\nSubtracting these results:\n\n\\[\n6700 - 402 = 6298\n\\]\n\nAll methods confirm the product is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6298}\n\\]",
