2: 7. So that gives 70, but shifted left, so 700.\n\nAdding the two lines: 112 + 700 = 812. Yep, same.\n\nAlternatively, let me do it without shifting and see:\n\nWait, actually, in the standard algorithm, when you move to the next digit (the tens place), you write the next partial product shifted one place to the left, so the 5\u00d714 is written as 70 but shifted, hence 700. Then adding them up gives the total.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use logarithms? That might be overkill, but just for verification. Wait, logs won't help here because it's multiplication, but maybe approximate?\n\nWait, 14\u00d758. 14\u00d760 is 840, so subtract 14\u00d72 (28), so 840-28=812. That's another way to see it. So that also gives 812.\n\nAlternatively, maybe do 15\u00d758, which is (10+5)\u00d758=580+290=870, then subtract 1\u00d758, so 870-58=812. Yep, that works too.\n\nAlright, so all these different methods are converging on the same answer. I think that's solid. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations. The answer must be 812.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 14 and 58 is \\boxed{812}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 14 and 58, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n1. Write the numbers vertically aligned by place value:\n   ```\n    14\n  \u00d7 58\n  ------\n  ```\n\n2. Multiply 14 by the units digit of 58 (8):\n   - \\(8 \\times 4 = 32\\), write down 2 and carry over 3.\n   - \\(8 \\times 1 = 8\\), add the carried over 3 to get 11.\n   - The first partial product is 112.\n\n3. Multiply 14 by the tens digit of 58 (5), and shift the result one place to the left (equivalent to multiplying by 10):\n   - \\(5 \\times 4 = 20\\), write down 0 and carry over 2.\n   - \\(5 \\times 1 = 5\\), add the carried over 2 to get 7.\n   - The second partial product is 70 shifted left, resulting in 700.\n\n4. Add the partial products:\n   - \\(112 + 700 = 812\\).\n\nWe can verify this using different methods:\n- **Distributive Property**: \\(14 \\times (50 + 8) = 14 \\times 50 + 14 \\times 8 = 700 + 112 = 812\\).\n- **Breaking Down Numbers**: \\((10 + 4) \\times 58 = 10 \\times 58 + 4 \\times 58 = 580 + 232 = 812\\).\n- **Approximation**: \\(14 \\times 60 - 14 \\times 2 = 840 - 28 = 812\\).\n\nAll methods confirm the result is consistent.\n\n### Final Answer\nThe product of 14 and 58 is \\boxed{812}.",
