      "text": "660 plus 66 is 726. So the product should be 726. \n\nWait, let me check again to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Alternatively, maybe I can do it another way to confirm. Let's see, 22 multiplied by 33. Maybe I can do 20 times 33 and then add 2 times 33. 20 times 33 is 660, and 2 times 33 is 66. Adding those together again gives 660 + 66 = 726. Okay, same result. That's reassuring. \n\nAlternatively, maybe using the standard multiplication algorithm. Let me write it out vertically:\n\n\u2003\u00a022\n\u00d7\u00a033\n-------\nFirst multiply 22 by 3 (the units place of 33), which is 66. Then multiply 22 by 30 (the tens place), which is 660. Adding those two results: 66 + 660 = 726. Yep, same answer. \n\nHmm, seems consistent across different methods. Maybe I can also check using a calculator if I have one, but since I'm doing this mentally, I'll stick with the multiple methods confirming the same result. \n\nAlternatively, another approach: 22 times 33 is the same as (20 + 2)(30 + 3). Expanding that, it would be 20*30 + 20*3 + 2*30 + 2*3. Let's compute each term:\n\n20*30 = 600,\n\n20*3 = 60,\n\n2*30 = 60,\n\n2*3 = 6.\n\nAdding them all together: 600 + 60 + 60 + 6. That's 600 + 120 + 6 = 726. \n\nSame answer again. Alright, so all these different methods lead me to 726. I think that's solid. I don't see any errors in my calculations. \n\nJust to be thorough, maybe I can think of it as 33 times 22. Since multiplication is commutative, it should be the same. Let me try 33 times 22. \n\nBreaking it down: 30*22 + 3*22. 30*22 is 660, and 3*22 is 66. Adding them gives 726. Yep. \n\nAlternatively, if I consider exponents or other properties, but I don't think that's necessary here. Since all methods give the same result, I can be confident that the product of 22 and 33 is indeed 726. \n\nWait a second, just to make sure I didn't miscalculate 22 times 3. Let me confirm 22*3: 20*3 is 60, plus 2*3 is 6, so 66. Correct. And 22*30: 22*3 is 66, so times 10 is 660. Yes. So adding those gives 726. \n\nAnother sanity check: 22 is close to 20, and 33 is close to 30. 20*30 is 600, so 22*33 should be a bit more than that, which 726 is. 726 minus 600 is 126. That seems reasonable. \n\nAlternatively, approximate: 22*33 is approximately 20*35=700, which is close to 726. Since 22 is a bit more than 20 and 33 is a bit less than 35, so the actual value would be a bit higher than 700. 726 is reasonable. \n\nOkay, I think I've verified it enough ways. I'm confident the answer is 726.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 22 and 33 is calculated as follows:\n\n1. **Using the distributive property:**\n   \\[\n   22 \\times 33 = 22 \\times (30 + 3) = (22 \\times 30) + (22 \\times 3)\n   \\]\n   - \\(22 \\times 30 = 660\\)\n   - \\(22 \\times 3 = 66\\)\n   - Adding the results: \\(660 + 66 = 726\\)\n\n2. **Standard multiplication algorithm:**\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n     \\quad 22 \\\\\n   \\times 33 \\\\\n   \\hline\n     \\quad 66 \\quad (\\text{22 \u00d7 3}) \\\\\n   + 660 \\quad (\\text{22 \u00d7 30, shifted left by one digit}) \\\\\n   \\hline\n     726 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Expanding using \\((a+b)(c+d)\\):**\n   \\[\n   (20 + 2)(30 + 3) = 20\u00d730 + 20\u00d73 + 2\u00d730 + 2\u00d73 = 600 + 60 + 60 + 6 = 726\n   \\]\n\nAll methods confirm the result is consistent. \n\n**Final Answer:** \\(\\boxed{726}\\)",
