      "text": "600 + 130 = 730, plus 5 makes 735. Wait, but let me check that again because I might have messed up the addition steps. Alternatively, 490 + 245. Let's add 490 + 200 first, which is 690. Then add 45 more. 690 + 45 is 735. Yeah, that works. So the total is 735. \n\nAlternatively, maybe there's another way to do it. Like using the standard multiplication algorithm. Let me try that to verify. \n\nMultiplying 49 by 15:\n\nFirst, write 49  \n           x15  \n       --------\n           245  (this is 49*5)\n          +490   (this is 49*10, shifted one position to the left)\n       --------\n           735\n\nYep, same result. So that checks out. \n\nWait another thought: 49 is close to 50. Maybe I can use that. 50 times 15 is 750. But since it's 49 instead of 50, that's one less. So 750 - 15 = 735. Oh, that works too! So that's another way to think of it. 50*15 = 750, subtract 15 gives 735. That confirms it again. \n\nHmm, so all the methods are leading me to 735. I think that's the right answer. Let me just do a quick check with a calculator in my mind. 49 * 10 is 490, 49*5 is 245, so 490 +245 is definitely 735. Yep, that's correct. Okay, I feel confident that the product of 49 and 15 is 735.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 49 and 15 is calculated as follows:\n\n1. **Using the distributive property**:  \n   \\( 49 \\times 15 = 49 \\times (10 + 5) \\)  \n   \\( = (49 \\times 10) + (49 \\times 5) \\)  \n   \\( = 490 + 245 \\)  \n   \\( = 735 \\).\n\n2. **Alternative method (breaking down 49)**:  \n   \\( 49 = 50 - 1 \\), so:  \n   \\( (50 - 1) \\times 15 = 50 \\times 15 - 1 \\times 15 \\)  \n   \\( = 750 - 15 \\)  \n   \\( = 735 \\).\n\n3. **Verification via standard multiplication**:  \n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n     \\quad 49 \\\\\n   \\times 15 \\\\\n   \\hline\n     245 \\quad (\\text{49 \u00d7 5}) \\\\\n   +490 \\quad (\\text{49 \u00d7 10, shifted left}) \\\\\n   \\hline\n     735 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\nAll methods confirm the result is **735**.\n\n**Answer:**  \n\\boxed{735}",
