      "text": "1240 minus 62. Let me do that subtraction.\n\n1240 minus 60 would be 1180, and then minus 2 more is 1178. Wait, but let me check again to make sure. Alternatively, maybe I can do it another way to verify.\n\nAlternatively, maybe multiply 62 by 19 directly. Let's see. 62 times 10 is 620, and 62 times 9 is 558. Adding those together: 620 + 558. Let's add 600 + 500 first, which is 1100, then 20 + 58 is 78. So total is 1178. That matches the first method. Okay, that seems right.\n\nBut just to be thorough, maybe do the standard multiplication:\n\n   62  \nx 19  \n-------\nFirst multiply 62 by 9: 62*9. 60*9=540, 2*9=18, so 558.\n\nThen multiply 62 by 10 (since the 1 in 19 is actually 10). 62*10=620.\n\nNow add 558 and 620. Let's see: 558 + 620. 500 + 600 is 1100. 58 + 20 is 78. So 1100 +78 is 1178. Yep, same result.\n\nHmm, seems like all methods lead to 1178. I think that's correct. Wait, but let me check with another approach. Maybe using the difference of squares? Not sure if that applies here, but let me think.\n\nAlternatively, 62 * 19. Let me write them as (60 + 2)*(20 -1). Then expand using FOIL. 60*20 is 1200, 60*(-1) is -60, 2*20 is 40, and 2*(-1) is -2. So adding those together: 1200 -60 +40 -2. Let's compute step by step. 1200 -60 is 1140, plus 40 is 1180, minus 2 is 1178. Same answer again. So that's consistent.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use a calculator? But since I don't have one, but I can trust that all these methods are giving me the same number. So I think I can be confident that 62 multiplied by 19 is 1178. \n\nWait, hold on, let me just do the multiplication vertically again step by step to ensure there's no arithmetic mistake.\n\nMultiply 62 by 9:\n\n9*2 = 18, write down 8 and carry over 1.\n\n9*6 = 54, plus the carried 1 is 55. So that gives 558.\n\nThen multiply 62 by 10 (since the 1 in 19's tens place is actually 10):\n\n62*10 is straightforward: 620.\n\nAdding 558 + 620:\n\n558 + 600 would be 1158, but since it's 620, that's 1158 +20 = 1178. Wait, no: 558 +620: 500 +600 =1100, 58 +20 =78, so 1100 +78 =1178. Correct.\n\nAlternatively, adding 558 + 620:\n\n558 + 620. Let's add the hundreds: 500 +600 =1100. Then the tens and units: 58 +20 =78. Total 1178. Yep.\n\nAlright, seems like all methods confirm that the product is 1178. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations, so I think that's the right answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 62 and 19 is \\boxed{1178}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 62 and 19, we can use the distributive property by breaking down 19 into 20 - 1:\n\n\\[\n62 \\times 19 = 62 \\times (20 - 1) = 62 \\times 20 - 62 \\times 1\n\\]\n\nFirst, calculate \\(62 \\times 20\\):\n\n\\[\n62 \\times 20 = 1240\n\\]\n\nThen subtract \\(62 \\times 1\\):\n\n\\[\n1240 - 62 = 1178\n\\]\n\nTo verify, we can also use the standard multiplication method:\n\n1. Multiply 62 by 9:\n   \\[\n   62 \\times 9 = 558\n   \\]\n\n2. Multiply 62 by 10 (since the 1 in 19 is in the tens place):\n   \\[\n   62 \\times 10 = 620\n   \\]\n\n3. Add the results of the two multiplications:\n   \\[\n   558 + 620 = 1178\n   \\]\n\nAnother verification using the expansion of \\((60 + 2) \\times (20 - 1)\\):\n\n\\[\n(60 + 2)(20 - 1) = 60 \\times 20 + 60 \\times (-1) + 2 \\times 20 + 2 \\times (-1)\n\\]\n\\[\n= 1200 - 60 + 40 - 2 = 1178\n\\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 62 and 19 is \\(\\boxed{1178}\\).",
