thods confirm that the product is 2318. I think that's solid. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations here. So I can confidently say the answer is 2318.\n\nWait, but just to be absolutely sure, maybe I can check using another approach, like the difference of squares or something? Let me see. Hmm, 38 and 61. Not sure if they fit into a difference of squares, but maybe approximate?\n\nAlternatively, maybe use a calculator? But since this is a problem-solving exercise, perhaps I shouldn't. But given that multiple manual methods have given me the same result, I think it's safe.\n\nAlternatively, check if 2318 divided by 38 equals 61. Let me do that division to verify.\n\n2318 \u00f7 38. Let's see how many times 38 goes into 2318.\n\nFirst, 38 \u00d7 60 is 2280. Subtract that from 2318: 2318 - 2280 = 38. Then 38 \u00f738 is 1. So total is 60 +1 =61. Perfect, so that division works out. Therefore, 38\u00d761=2318 is correct.\n\nOkay, so after multiple checks, I feel confident that the answer is 2318.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 38 and 61 is \\boxed{2318}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 38 and 61, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n1. Write the numbers vertically:\n   ```\n    38\n  \u00d7 61\n  ------\n   ```\n\n2. Multiply 38 by the ones digit of 61 (which is 1):\n   \\[\n   38 \\times 1 = 38\n   \\]\n   Write down 38.\n\n3. Multiply 38 by the tens digit of 61 (which is 6, representing 60). First, calculate \\(38 \\times 6\\):\n   \\[\n   38 \\times 6 = 228\n   \\]\n   Since this is the tens place, shift the result one position to the left:\n   \\[\n   228 \\times 10 = 2280\n   \\]\n\n4. Add the two partial products:\n   \\[\n   38 + 2280 = 2318\n   \\]\n\nTo verify, we can use the distributive property:\n\\[\n(30 + 8) \\times (60 + 1) = 30 \\times 60 + 30 \\times 1 + 8 \\times 60 + 8 \\times 1\n\\]\nCalculating each term:\n\\[\n30 \\times 60 = 1800, \\quad 30 \\times 1 = 30, \\quad 8 \\times 60 = 480, \\quad 8 \\times 1 = 8\n\\]\nAdding these together:\n\\[\n1800 + 30 + 480 + 8 = 2318\n\\]\n\nAnother verification using division:\n\\[\n2318 \\div 38 = 61\n\\]\nThis confirms the result is correct.\n\nThe product of 38 and 61 is \\(\\boxed{2318}\\).",
