0 +60 +7. Wait, maybe that complicates. Alternatively, 2670 + 267: \n\n2670  \n+\u00a0267  \n------  \nAdd units: 0+7=7  \nTens:7+6=13, carryover 1  \nHundreds:6 +2 +1=9  \nThousands:2 +0=2  \nSo total is 2937. Yep, that's right. \n\nAlright, I think that's solid. So the product is 2937. \n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 89 and 33 is \\boxed{2937}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 89 and 33, we can use the distributive property and verify using the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n1. **Using the distributive property:**\n   - Break down 33 into 30 and 3.\n   - Calculate \\(89 \\times 30\\):\n     \\[\n     89 \\times 30 = 89 \\times 3 \\times 10 = 267 \\times 10 = 2670\n     \\]\n   - Calculate \\(89 \\times 3\\):\n     \\[\n     89 \\times 3 = (80 \\times 3) + (9 \\times 3) = 240 + 27 = 267\n     \\]\n   - Add the results:\n     \\[\n     2670 + 267 = 2937\n     \\]\n\n2. **Using the standard multiplication algorithm:**\n   - Multiply 89 by 3 (units place):\n     \\[\n     89 \\times 3 = 267\n     \\]\n   - Multiply 89 by 30 (tens place, shifted one position left):\n     \\[\n     89 \\times 30 = 2670\n     \\]\n   - Add the partial products:\n     \\[\n     2670 + 267 = 2937\n     \\]\n\n3. **Alternative method using (90 - 1) * 33:**\n   - Calculate \\(90 \\times 33\\):\n     \\[\n     90 \\times 33 = 2970\n     \\]\n   - Subtract \\(1 \\times 33\\):\n     \\[\n     2970 - 33 = 2937\n     \\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 89 and 33 is \\(\\boxed{2937}\\).",
