xt diagonal (hundreds place):\n\nThe top half of the top right cell (4), the bottom half of the bottom left cell (8), and the top half of the bottom left cell (4). Wait, the bottom left cell is the first cell in the second row, which is 4 | 8.\n\nWait, actually, the hundreds place diagonal would be:\n\nThe top half of the top left cell (5), the bottom half of the bottom left cell (8), and the top half of the top right cell (4). Wait, no, maybe I need to think of the diagonals as follows:\n\nWait, perhaps I need to arrange the numbers in the grid properly. Let me think of it as:\n\nThe grid has four cells arranged like:\n\n[ (5/6) (4/2) ]\n\n[ (4/8) (3/6) ]\n\nWhere each cell has the top half and bottom half separated by a diagonal line.\n\nThe diagonals for addition go from top-right to bottom-left. So the first diagonal (rightmost) is just the bottom of the last cell (the 6 in the bottom right cell's bottom half).\n\nThe next diagonal is the bottom half of the cell to the left (which is the bottom half of the top right cell, which is 2) and the top half of the cell below (the top half of the bottom right cell, which is 3). So 2 +3=5.\n\nThe next diagonal is the top half of the top right cell (4), the bottom half of the bottom left cell (8), and the top half of the top left cell (5). Wait, no:\n\nWait actually, the third diagonal (moving left) would be:\n\nThe top half of the top right cell (4), the bottom half of the bottom left cell (8), and the top half of the bottom left cell (4)? Hmm, getting confused. Maybe it's better to list the diagonals step by step:\n\nFirst diagonal (units): 6.\n\nSecond diagonal (tens): 2 (from top right cell's bottom) + 3 (from bottom right cell's top) =5.\n\nThird diagonal (hundreds): 4 (top right cell's top) + 8 (bottom left cell's bottom) + 4 (bottom left cell's top?) Wait, no, perhaps the third diagonal includes the top half of the top left cell (5), the bottom half of the bottom left cell (8), and the top half of the top right cell (4)?\n\nWait, maybe I should visualize the grid as:\n\nThe four cells arranged in two rows and two columns.\n\nTop row cells:\n\nFirst cell (top-left): split into 5(top) and 6(bottom)\n\nSecond cell (top-right): split into 4(top) and 2(bottom)\n\nSecond row cells:\n\nFirst cell (bottom-left): split into 4(top) and 8(bottom)\n\nSecond cell (bottom-right): split into 3(top) and 6(bottom)\n\nNow, the diagonals for addition:\n\nStarting from the bottom-right corner (the units place):\n\nFirst diagonal is just the bottom part of the bottom-right cell: 6.\n\nSecond diagonal (moving to the left and up):\n\nThis includes the bottom part of the top-right cell (2) and the top part of the bottom-right cell (3). So 2 + 3 = 5.\n\nThird diagonal (next):\n\nThis would include the top part of the top-right cell (4), the bottom part of the bottom-left cell (8), and the top part of the bottom-left cell (4)? Wait, no, perhaps the third diagonal is the top part of the top-left cell (5), the bottom part of the bottom-left cell (8), and the top part of the top-right cell (4). Wait, maybe I need to think in terms of diagonal lines from top-right to bottom-left.\n\nLet me try to draw this mentally with lines:\n\nThe diagonals would be:\n\n1. The rightmost diagonal: just the bottom-right cell's bottom half (6).\n\n2. The next diagonal to the left: includes the bottom half of the top-right cell (2) and the top half of the bottom-right cell (3) \u2192 total 5.\n\n3. The next diagonal: includes the top half of the top-right cell (4), the bottom half of the bottom-left cell (8), and the top half of the top-left cell (5). Wait, no, maybe only two cells here.\n\nWait, actually, the third diagonal would consist of the top half of the top-right cell (4) and the bottom half of the bottom-left cell (8). Plus the top half of the top-left cell (5)? Hmm, this is getting too confusing. Maybe another way.\n\nAlternatively, let's list all the numbers in the grid and see how the diagonals add up:\n\nThe cells (with top/bottom separated):\n\nTop row:\n\nFirst cell: 5 (top), 6 (bottom)\n\nSecond cell: 4 (top), 2 (bottom)\n\nBottom row:\n\nFirst cell:4 (top), 8 (bottom)\n\nSecond cell:3 (top), 6 (bottom)\n\nNow, arrange them in the grid:\n\nRow 1: [5/6] [4/2]\n\nRow 2: [4/8] [3/6]\n\nNow, the diagonals for adding are from bottom-left to top-right:\n\nWait, no, actually in lattice multiplication, the diagonals are read from the bottom-right to top-left, and you add the numbers along each diagonal, starting from the bottom-right.\n\nSo, starting from the bottom-right corner (the 6 in the bottom-right cell's bottom half), that's the first diagonal (units place).\n\nThen moving to the left, the next diagonal includes:\n\nThe bottom half of the top-right cell (2) and the top half of the bottom-right cell (3). So 2 +3 =5 (tens place).\n\nNext diagonal to the left includes:\n\nThe top half of the top-right cell (4), the bottom half of the bottom-left cell (8), and the top half of the bottom-left cell (4). Wait, no, the bottom-left cell's bottom half is 8, and its top half is 4, but which are part of which diagonals?\n\nAlternatively, the third diagonal (hundreds place) would be:\n\nThe top half of the top-right cell (4), the bottom half of the bottom-left cell (8), and the top half of the top-left cell (5). Wait, maybe:\n\nWait, perhaps the third diagonal is the top half of the top-right cell (4) plus the bottom half of the bottom-left cell (8), and the top half of the top-left cell (5). Hmm, but how does that work?\n\nWait, let's see:\n\nThe third diagonal (hundreds place):\n\nLooking at the grid, moving up along the diagonal:\n\nStarting from the second row, first column's top half (4) and the first row, first column's top half (5), and the first row, second column's top half (4). Wait, no, perhaps it's the sum of the top halves and bottom halves in the correct diagonal.\n\nAlternatively, maybe it's the sum of the top half of the top-left cell (5), the bottom half of the bottom-left cell (8), and the top half of the top-right cell (4). Wait, that seems too much. Let me think step by step.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the third diagonal (hundreds place):\n\nLooking at the diagonal that passes through the top half of the top-left cell (5), the bottom half of the top-right cell (2), and the top half of the bottom-right cell (3)? No, not sure.\n\nThis is getting too confusing. Maybe I should just accept that lattice method is taking too long here and trust the previous methods which all gave me 6536. Since all three methods (standard algorithm, distributive property, and another factoring) gave the same result, I think that's solid.\n\nAlternatively, let me do a quick check using estimation. 86 is approximately 90, and 76 is approximately 80. 90*80 is 7200. But 86 is 4 less than 90, and 76 is 4 less than 80. So the actual product should be less than 7200. 6536 is 7200 minus around 664, which makes sense because reducing both numbers by 4 each would reduce the product significantly. Alternatively, maybe compute the exact difference:\n\nThe difference between (90-4)(80-4) = 90*80 -90*4 -80*4 +4*4 = 7200 -360 -320 +16 = 7200 -680 +16 = 6536. Oh, that's exactly the answer I got. So that's another way to confirm. Since (a - b)(a - c) where a is 90 and 80? Wait, actually, in this case, it's (90 -4)(80 -4), so expanding that gives the same as earlier. So that's a fourth method confirming 6536. \n\nTherefore, I'm pretty confident that the product of 86 and 76 is 6536. I think I've checked it thoroughly. Initially, I was worried because sometimes when doing mental math, it's easy to make a small error like miscounting a carry-over or misadding partial products, but all methods align here. So I think that's the correct answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 86 and 76 is \\boxed{6536}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 86 and 76, we can use multiple methods to ensure accuracy.\n\n### Method 1: Standard Algorithm\n1. Multiply 86 by 6 (ones place):\n   \\[\n   86 \\times 6 = 516\n   \\]\n2. Multiply 86 by 70 (tens place, represented by 7 in the tens place):\n   \\[\n   86 \\times 70 = 6020\n   \\]\n3. Add the partial products:\n   \\[\n   516 + 6020 = 6536\n   \\]\n\n### Method 2: Distributive Property\n1. Expand the product using \\((80 + 6)(70 + 6)\\):\n   \\[\n   (80 + 6)(70 + 6) = 80 \\times 70 + 80 \\times 6 + 6 \\times 70 + 6 \\times 6\n   \\]\n2. Calculate each term:\n   \\[\n   80 \\times 70 = 5600, \\quad 80 \\times 6 = 480, \\quad 6 \\times 70 = 420, \\quad 6 \\times 6 = 36\n   \\]\n3. Sum the terms:\n   \\[\n   5600 + 480 + 420 + 36 = 6536\n   \\]\n\n### Method 3: Factoring and Expansion\n1. Express 86 and 76 as \\((80 + 6)\\) and \\((80 - 4)\\) respectively:\n   \\[\n   (80 + 6)(80 - 4) = 80 \\times 80 + 80 \\times (-4) + 6 \\times 80 + 6 \\times (-4)\n   \\]\n2. Calculate each term:\n   \\[\n   80 \\times 80 = 6400, \\quad 80 \\times (-4) = -320, \\quad 6 \\times 80 = 480, \\quad 6 \\times (-4) = -24\n   \\]\n3. Sum the terms:\n   \\[\n   6400 - 320 + 480 - 24 = 6536\n   \\]\n\n### Method 4: Estimation and Verification\n1. Estimate using nearby numbers:\n   \\[\n   (90 - 4)(80 - 4) = 90 \\times 80 - 90 \\times 4 - 80 \\times 4 + 4 \\times 4\n   \\]\n2. Calculate each term:\n   \\[\n   90 \\times 80 = 7200, \\quad 90 \\times 4 = 360, \\quad 80 \\times 4 = 320, \\quad 4 \\times 4 = 16\n   \\]\n3. Sum the terms:\n   \\[\n   7200 - 360 - 320 + 16 = 6536\n   \\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 86 and 76 is \\(\\boxed{6536}\\).",
