gorithm, and breaking into factors, all lead me to 4050. I think that's correct. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations. Let me check once more with a calculator approach mentally.\n\nAlternatively, 54 \u00d775. Let's reverse the numbers. 54\u00d770 is 3780, 54\u00d75 is 270. Adding 3780 +270 = 4050. Yep, same thing. Okay, definitely confident now. The product is 4050.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 75 and 54 is calculated as follows:\n\n1. **Break down using distributive property**:\n   \\[\n   75 \\times 54 = 75 \\times (50 + 4) = (75 \\times 50) + (75 \\times 4)\n   \\]\n   \n2. **Compute each part**:\n   - \\( 75 \\times 50 = 3750 \\)\n   - \\( 75 \\times 4 = 300 \\)\n\n3. **Sum the results**:\n   \\[\n   3750 + 300 = 4050\n   \\]\n\n**Verification via alternative methods** (standard multiplication, factoring, and reversing the multiplication) all confirm the result is consistent.\n\n**Answer**: \\(\\boxed{4050}\\)",
