      "text": "800 +10 = 810. Wait, that makes sense. Alternatively, adding a zero to 81 gives 810 directly. Yeah, that checks out. I think that's correct.\n\nBut let me do it step by step to be sure. 81 multiplied by 10. Let's write it out vertically:\n\n\u2003\u00a081\n\u00d7\u00a010\n------\n\u2003\u00a000 (since 81 \u00d7 0 is 0)\n+810 (since 81 \u00d7 10 is shifting one position to the left)\n------\n\u00a0810\n\nYep, that adds up to 810. So the product should be 810. I don't see any mistakes here. Maybe another way to look at it is using the distributive property. 10 is 10^1, so when you multiply any number by 10, you're essentially moving the decimal point one place to the right. So 81.0 becomes 810.0. That also works. \n\nAlternatively, thinking in terms of place value, 81 is 8 tens and 1 unit. Multiplying by 10 would shift each digit one place higher, so 8 tens become 8 hundreds, and 1 unit becomes 1 ten. So 800 +10 = 810. All these methods lead to the same answer. \n\nI guess there's no reason to doubt it. The answer must be 810. I can't think of any other way to verify, but all the different approaches confirm the same result. So I'm confident that's the right answer.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 81 and 10 is calculated by multiplying the two numbers together:\n\n\\[\n81 \\times 10 = 810\n\\]\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n1. **Understand multiplication by 10:** Multiplying any number by 10 appends a zero to the end of the number.\n2. **Apply the rule:** Add a zero to 81, resulting in **810**.\n3. **Verification (optional):**\n   - Break down: \\(81 \\times 10 = (80 + 1) \\times 10 = 800 + 10 = 810\\).\n   - Vertical multiplication:\n     ```\n     \u200381\n     \u00d710\n     ----\n     \u200300  \n     +810  \n     ----\n     \u00a0810\n     ```\n\n**Final Answer:** \\(\\boxed{810}\\)",
