es into 0 zero times. So it's 100. Yep, that checks out. \n\nAlternatively, 3100 divided by 100 should be 31. 3100 divided by 100 is 31, which is correct. \n\nOkay, so all my methods confirm that the product is 3100. I think that's the right answer. I don't see any mistakes in my reasoning here. All the different approaches\u2014using place value, distributive property, checking with division\u2014everything lines up. So I feel confident that the answer is 3100. \n\nJust to recap once more: Multiplying by 100 adds two zeros to the original number. So 31 with two zeros is 3100. Yep, that's straightforward. \n\nI guess there's no trick or anything here. It's a straightforward multiplication. Maybe if the numbers were larger or had more complex digits it would be harder, but with 31 and 100, it's pretty simple. \n\nAnother way to think about it: 31 times 100 is 31 hundreds. Since one hundred is 100, 31 hundreds would be 31 followed by two zeros, which is 3100. Exactly. \n\nAlright, I think I've thought this through every possible way. I can't find any errors, so I'll go with 3100 as the answer.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 31 and 100 is calculated by multiplying the two numbers together. \n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n1. **Understanding Multiplication by 100:** Multiplying any number by 100 appends two zeros to the end of that number.\n2. **Apply to 31:** Adding two zeros to 31 gives **3100**.\n3. **Verification via Distributive Property:**  \n   \\( 31 \\times 100 = (30 + 1) \\times 100 = 30 \\times 100 + 1 \\times 100 = 3000 + 100 = 3100 \\).\n4. **Check via Division:**  \n   \\( 3100 \\div 31 = 100 \\), confirming the result is correct.\n\n**Answer:**  \nThe product of 31 and 100 is \\boxed{3100}.",
