      "text": "870. Wait, let me check again. 87 times 10. If I break it down, 80 times 10 is 800, and 7 times 10 is 70. Adding those together, 800 +70 is 870. Yeah, that makes sense. So, I think that's correct. But maybe I should write it out the long way to be sure. \n\nMultiplying 87 by 10:\n\n   87\nx 10\n-----\n   00   (87 x 0)\n+870    (87 x 10, shifted one position to the left)\n-----\n  870\n\nYep, that adds up. So the product is definitely 870. I don't think I made any mistakes here. It's pretty basic, but it's always good to double-check. Another way to look at it is using place value. 87 is 8 tens and 7 ones. Multiplying by 10 increases each place value by one, so 8 tens become 8 hundreds, and 7 ones become 7 tens. So 800 +70 = 870. That checks out too. Alright, I'm confident the answer is 870.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 87 and 10 is calculated by multiplying the two numbers together:\n\n\\[\n87 \\times 10 = 870\n\\]\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**  \n1. **Multiplication by 10 Rule**: When multiplying any number by 10, append a zero to the end of the number.  \n   - Example: \\(87 \\times 10 = 870\\) (add a zero to 87).  \n2. **Verification**:  \n   - Break down \\(87 \\times 10\\) into \\((80 + 7) \\times 10\\).  \n   - \\(80 \\times 10 = 800\\)  \n   - \\(7 \\times 10 = 70\\)  \n   - Sum the results: \\(800 + 70 = 870\\).  \n\n**Final Answer:**  \n\\boxed{870}",
