t cell's bottom-right triangle and the 4 from the bottom-right cell's bottom-right. Wait, maybe I need to visualize better.\n\nActually, in lattice, the diagonals separate the digits. Let me try to lay it out:\n\nThe grid after products:\n\nTop row cells:\n\nFirst cell (2\u00d78): 1 | 6\n\nSecond cell (2\u00d79): 1 | 8\n\nBottom row cells:\n\nThird cell (6\u00d78): 4 | 8\n\nFourth cell (6\u00d79): 5 | 4\n\nWait, perhaps arranging the diagonals:\n\nThe grid's diagonals split the cells into upper left and lower right. The addition is done along the diagonals from bottom to top.\n\nSo the final number is read from the top left to bottom right after adding.\n\nWait, maybe this is getting too complicated. Let me see:\n\nAlternatively, here's a step-by-step for lattice:\n\n1. Draw a 2x2 grid.\n\n2. Label the top edges with 2 and 6 (the digits of the first number).\n\n3. Label the right edges with 8 and 9 (the digits of the second number).\n\n4. Split each cell with a diagonal line.\n\n5. Multiply the digits for each cell and write the tens and units in the respective triangles.\n\nSo, top-left cell: 2\u00d78=16 \u2192 1 (tens) and 6 (units).\n\nTop-right cell: 2\u00d79=18 \u2192 1 and 8.\n\nBottom-left cell: 6\u00d78=48 \u21924 and8.\n\nBottom-right cell:6\u00d79=54 \u21925 and4.\n\nNow, add along the diagonals starting from the rightmost diagonal.\n\nThe rightmost diagonal is just the lower right corner of the bottom-right cell, which is 4. So first digit is 4.\n\nNext diagonal is the middle vertical line: the lower right of the bottom-left cell (8) plus the upper left of the bottom-right cell (5). So 8 +5=13. Write down 3 and carry over 1.\n\nNext diagonal is the leftmost vertical and the upper part: the lower right of top-right cell (8), upper left of bottom-left cell (4), and the carried over 1. Wait, maybe I need to think in terms of the grid's diagonals.\n\nActually, the diagonals are from bottom-right to top-left. Let me try again:\n\nThe diagonals are as follows:\