ch digit of the result is obtained by summing the numbers along the diagonals from top-right to bottom-left.\n\nStarting from the top-right corner:\n\nFirst diagonal (units place): the lower-right of the top-right cell (8).\n\nSecond diagonal (tens place): upper-left of top-right cell (1) and lower-right of bottom-right cell (4). 1 +4=5.\n\nThird diagonal (hundreds place): upper-left of bottom-right cell (5), lower-right of top-left cell (6), and lower-right of bottom-left cell (8). 5 +6 +8=19 \u21929, carryover1.\n\nFourth diagonal (thousands place): upper-left of top-left cell (1), upper-left of bottom-left cell (4), plus carryover1. 1 +4 +1=6.\n\nFifth diagonal (ten thousands place): ?\n\nWait this is getting too confusing. Maybe I need to look up the exact steps. Since I'm getting conflicting results, perhaps lattice isn't the best method here, or I'm making a mistake in its application. Given that all other methods confirm 2314, I should trust those.\n\nTherefore, the correct product is 2314. The lattice method must have been applied incorrectly, likely due to a miscalculation in how the diagonals are summed. Since multiple standard methods give me 2314, I'll go with that confidence.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 26 and 89 is \\boxed{2314}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 26 and 89, we can use multiple methods to ensure accuracy:\n\n1. **Standard Multiplication Method**:\n   - Multiply 26 by 9: \n     \\[\n     26 \\times 9 = 234\n     \\]\n   - Multiply 26 by 80 (since 8 is in the tens place):\n     \\[\n     26 \\times 80 = 2080\n     \\]\n   - Add the results:\n     \\[\n     234 + 2080 = 2314\n     \\]\n\n2. **Distributive Property**:\n   - Break down 89 into 90 - 1:\n     \\[\n     26 \\times 89 = 26 \\times (90 - 1)\n     \\]\n   - Calculate \\(26 \\times 90\\) and subtract \\(26 \\times 1\\):\n     \\[\n     26 \\times 90 = 2340 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 2340 - 26 = 2314\n     \\]\n\n3. **Breaking Down Both Numbers**:\n   - Break down 26 into 2