60\u00d75=300, 9\u00d75=45. Adding those: 1500+300=1800, +45=1845. Yep, same answer again.\n\nAlright, so after checking multiple methods: standard multiplication, distributive property, difference of squares, breaking into factors, and incremental multiplication, all give me 1845. I think that's solid. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations, so I'm confident that's the correct product.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 45 and 41 is \\boxed{1845}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 45 and 41, we can use multiple methods to verify the result:\n\n1. **Standard Algorithm**:\n   - Multiply 45 by 1 (the ones place of 41):\n     \\[\n     45 \\times 1 = 45\n     \\]\n   - Multiply 45 by 40 (the tens place of 41, which is 4 \u00d7 10):\n     \\[\n     45 \\times 40 = 45 \\times 4 \\times 10 = 180 \\times 10 = 1800\n     \\]\n   - Add the results:\n     \\[\n     45 + 1800 = 1845\n     \\]\n\n2. **Distributive Property**:\n   - Expand \\((40 + 5)(40 + 1)\\):\n     \\[\n     40 \\times 40 + 40 \\times 1 + 5 \\times 40 + 5 \\times 1\n     \\]\n   - Calculate each term:\n     \\[\n     1600 + 40 + 200 + 5 = 1600 + 40 = 1640; \\quad 1640 + 200 = 1840; \\quad 1840 + 5 = 1845\n     \\]\n\n3. **Difference of Squares**:\n   - Recognize that \\(45 = 43 + 2\\) and \\(41 = 43 - 2\\):\n     \\[\n     (43 + 2)(43 - 2) = 43^2 - 2^2\n     \\]\n   - Calculate:\n     \\[\n     43^2 = 1849; \\quad 1849 - 4 = 1845\n     \\]\n\n4. **Alternative Breakdown**:\n   - Multiply 45 by 40 and then add 45:\n     \\[\n     45 \\times 40 = 1800; \\quad 1800 + 45 = 1845\n     \\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 45 and 41 is \\(\\boxed{1845}\\).",
