cell (2,2) is in the second row first column. Hmm, maybe I need to visualize the grid as:\n\nColumns are for the digits of the first number (2 and 7), rows for the second number (8 and 2). So the grid cells are arranged so that each cell (row, column) is row's digit times column's digit.\n\nSo the grid is:\n\n          Columns: 2      7\n\nRows:\n\n8         1/6      5/6\n\n2         0/4      1/4\n\nNow, the diagonals are drawn from top-right to bottom-left. The first diagonal (rightmost) is just the bottom half of the last column's bottom row (the 4 in the 1/4 of the 2-7 cell). Then moving left:\n\nSecond diagonal includes:\n\n- The bottom half of the cell above (cell (8,7) has bottom 6) and the bottom half of the cell to the left in the same row (cell (2,2) has bottom 4). Wait, actually, each diagonal is a diagonal line through the grid. Let me think of the positions:\n\nThe first diagonal (units place):\n\n- The bottom-right cell's bottom half: 4 (from cell (2,7))\n\nSecond diagonal (tens place):\n\n- The bottom half of cell (8,7) (6) and the bottom half of cell (2,2) (4). Wait, but those are in different rows and columns. Hmm. Maybe I'm getting confused here.\n\nAlternatively, let's list all the bottom halves and top halves in order:\n\nStarting from the top-right corner, moving down each diagonal:\n\nThe first diagonal (units place):\n\n- The bottom of the last column's bottom row (cell (2,7) bottom: 4). So units digit is 4.\n\nSecond diagonal (tens place):\n\n- The bottom of the cell above it (cell (8,7) bottom: 6) and the bottom of the cell to the left in the same row (cell (2,2) bottom: 4). Wait, but cell (2,2) is in row 2, column 1. The cell above it would be cell (8,2) which is in row1, column1.\n\nWait, maybe the diagonals are constructed as follows:\n\nEach diagonal is a diagonal line going from top-right to bottom-left through the grid. The numbers along each diagonal are summed.\n\nFirst diagonal (rightmost): starts at cell (8,7) bottom (6) and cell (2