      "text": "12 times 100, which is 1200.\n\nLet me check if that's right. Maybe using another method. If I write it out the standard way:\n\n   40\nx 30\n-----\n?\n\nMultiplying 40 by 0 (the units place of 30) gives 0. Then multiplying 40 by 3 (the tens place) but since it's actually 30, so it's 3 times 10, so the result is 120 shifted one position to the left, making it 1200. Yeah, that matches. \n\nAlternatively, I can use the distributive property: 40*30 = (40)*(30) = (4*10)*(3*10) = 4*3*10*10 = 12*100 = 1200. Yep, same result.\n\nI guess that's correct. Let me just do a quick sanity check. If I multiply 40 by 10, I get 400. So 40*30 is three times that, which is 1200. Yep, that works too. All the different methods give me the same answer, so I think the product of 40 and 30 is definitely 1200. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations. \n\nWait, another way: using area. If I imagine a rectangle that's 40 units long and 30 units wide, the area would be length times width, which is 40*30. The area is 1200 square units. That makes sense. \n\nAlternatively, maybe using exponents. 40 is 4*10^1 and 30 is 3*10^1. Multiplying those gives 4*3 * 10^(1+1) = 12*10^2 = 12*100 = 1200. Yep, same answer again. \n\nAlright, I think I'm confident that 40 multiplied by 30 is 1200. I can't think of any other way to verify it, but all methods point to the same number. So I'll go with that. \n\nJust to be absolutely sure, maybe do the multiplication step by step. 40 times 30. Since both numbers end with a zero, I can multiply 4*3=12 and then add the two zeros from the original numbers, making it 1200. Yep, that's straightforward. \n\nSo, all the different approaches confirm that the product is 1200. I think that's solid. No mistakes here. The answer has to be 1200.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 40 and 30 is calculated by multiplying the two numbers together. \n\n\\[\n40 \\times 30 = (4 \\times 10) \\times (3 \\times 10) = (4 \\times 3) \\times (10 \\times 10) = 12 \\times 100 = 1200\n\\]\n\n**Answer:** 1200",
