25\u00d770 is 1750, and 25\u00d73 is 75, so adding them gives 1825. Yep, that's the same as before. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe verify through division. If 25 \u00d773 =1825, then 1825 divided by 25 should be 73. Let me try dividing 1825 by 25. 25 \u00d770 is 1750. Subtract that from 1825: 1825 -1750=75. Then 75 divided by 25 is 3. So total is 70 +3 =73. Perfect, that works. Similarly, 1825 divided by 73 should be 25. Let me check that. 73\u00d720=1460. 1825-1460=365. 73\u00d75=365. So 20+5=25. That works too. So division confirms it. .\n\nAlright, I think that's solid. All different methods of multiplication and division confirm that the product is 1825. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations. So the answer should be 1825.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 25 and 73 is calculated as follows:\n\n1. **Breakdown using distributive property**:\n   \\[\n   25 \\times 73 = 25 \\times (70 + 3) = (25 \\times 70) + (25 \\times 3)\n   \\]\n   - \\(25 \\times 70 = 1750\\)\n   - \\(25 \\times 3 = 75\\)\n   - Adding these results: \\(1750 + 75 = 1825\\)\n\n2. **Verification through division**:\n   - \\(1825 \\div 25 = 73\\) (since \\(25 \\times 73 = 1825\\))\n   - \\(1825 \\div 73 = 25\\) (since \\(73 \\times 25 = 1825\\))\n\nBoth methods confirm the result is consistent.\n\n**Answer**: \\(\\boxed{1825}\\)",
