her form. 77 is 7 times 11, so 95\u00d77\u00d711. Maybe compute 95\u00d77 first? 95\u00d77 is 665, then 665\u00d711. Hmm, multiplying by 11 can be done by the trick where you add the digits. 665 \u00d711: 6 (6+6)=12, (6+5)=11, 5. Wait, that's not the standard 11 trick. Wait, actually, the 11 trick for three-digit numbers: for a three-digit number abc multiplied by 11, it's a (a+b) (b+c) c, but if any of the sums exceed 9, you have to carry over. Let me try:\n\n665 \u00d711:\n\nStart from the right: 5 \u00d711 is 55, so write down 5 and carry over 5? Wait, perhaps better to do it step by step.\n\nAlternatively, 665 \u00d710 is 6650, plus 665 is 7315. That's the same as before.\n\nAlternatively, 665 \u00d711: 600\u00d711=6600, 60\u00d711=660, 5\u00d711=55. Adding those: 6600 + 660 is 7260, plus 55 is 7315. Yep, same result.\n\nOkay, so all methods are converging on 7315. Let me just check with a calculator-style approach, multiplying step by step:\n\n95 \u00d777:\n\nFirst, 5 \u00d77 =35, write down 5 carryover 3.\n\nThen 9 \u00d77 +3 =63 +3=66, so that gives 665.\n\nNext, moving to the tens place:\n\n5 \u00d77 (the 7 in the tens place is actually 70, so 5\u00d770=350, but in the standard algorithm, you do 5\u00d77=35, shifted one place so it's 350).\n\nWait, perhaps breaking down the second multiplication step:\n\nWhen you write it out, multiplying by the tens digit (7 in the tens place is 70), so:\n\n95 \u00d770:\n\n90\u00d770 = 6300,\n\n5\u00d770=350,\n\nso 6300+350=6650. Then adding to the previous 665 gives 7315. All right.\n\nAlternatively, if I do 95 \u00d777, I can also think of 95 \u00d7 (80 - 3). Wait, that might complicate, but let's see:\n\n95 \u00d780 = 7600,\n\n95 \u00d73 = 285,\n\nso 7600 - 285 = 7315. Wait, yes, that also works. 7600 minus 285: 7600 - 200 is 7400, then minus 85 is 7315. Perfect.\n\nHmm, that's another method. So that also gives the same result. I think that's enough verification. I don't see any errors in my calculations, and all methods cross-check to 7315. I think that's the correct answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 95 and 77 is \\boxed{7315}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 95 and 77, we can use multiple methods to ensure accuracy:\n\n1. **Distributive Property**:\n   \\[\n   (90 + 5)(70 + 7) = 90 \\times 70 + 90 \\times 7 + 5 \\times 70 + 5 \\times 7\n   \\]\n   Calculating each term:\n   \\[\n   90 \\times 70 = 6300, \\quad 90 \\times 7 = 630, \\quad 5 \\times 70 = 350, \\quad 5 \\times 7 = 35\n   \\]\n   Adding all terms together:\n   \\[\n   6300 + 630 + 350 + 35 = 7315\n   \\]\n\n2. **Standard Multiplication**:\n   - Multiply 95 by 7:\n     \\[\n     95 \\times 7 = 665\n     \\]\n   - Multiply 95 by 70 (shifted one position left):\n     \\[\n     95 \\times 70 = 6650\n     \\]\n   - Add the results:\n     \\[\n     665 + 6650 = 7315\n     \\]\n\n3. **Using 95 as (100 - 5)**:\n   \\[\n   95 \\times 77 = (100 - 5) \\times 77 = 100 \\times 77 - 5 \\times 77\n   \\]\n   Calculating each term:\n   \\[\n   100 \\times 77 = 7700, \\quad 5 \\times 77 = 385\n   \\]\n   Subtracting the terms:\n   \\[\n   7700 - 385 = 7315\n   \\]\n\n4. **Breaking Down 77 as 7 \u00d7 11**:\n   - First, multiply 95 by 7:\n     \\[\n     95 \\times 7 = 665\n     \\]\n   - Then multiply by 11:\n     \\[\n     665 \\times 11 = 7315\n     \\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 95 and 77 is \\(\\boxed{7315}\\).",
