just brings me back to the original problem. So maybe that's not helpful here, but confirms the prime factors. \n\nAlternatively, maybe approximate? 36*50 is 1800, so 36*49 is 36 less, so 1764. So that's consistent. \n\nAlright, I think that's enough verification. The answer must be 1764.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 36 and 49 can be calculated using the distributive property by breaking down 49 into 50 - 1:\n\n\\[\n36 \\times 49 = 36 \\times (50 - 1) = (36 \\times 50) - (36 \\times 1)\n\\]\n\nFirst, compute \\( 36 \\times 50 \\):\n\\[\n36 \\times 50 = 1800\n\\]\n\nThen subtract \\( 36 \\times 1 \\):\n\\[\n1800 - 36 = 1764\n\\]\n\nTo verify, use the standard multiplication method:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   \\quad 36 \\\\\n\\times 49 \\\\\n\\hline\n   324 \\quad (\\text{36 \u00d7 9}) \\\\\n+1440 \\quad (\\text{36 \u00d7 40, shifted left by one digit}) \\\\\n\\hline\n   1764 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm the result. Thus, the product of 36 and 49 is:\n\n**Answer:** \\boxed{1764}",
