      "index": 0,
      "text": "900 + 36 = 936. Wait, let me check that addition again. 720 plus 200 would be 920, then plus 16 more is 936? Yes, that's right. Alternatively, adding 720 + 200 is 920, then adding the remaining 16 gives 936. Okay, that seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do the multiplication vertically to confirm. Let's see:\n\n\u2003\u200372\n\u00d713\n------\nFirst, multiply 72 by 3 (the units place of 13). 3\u00d72 is 6, 3\u00d77 is 21. So that gives 216.\n\nThen, multiply 72 by 10 (the tens place of 13, which is 1, but shifted one position to the left). So that's 72\u00d710 = 720.\n\nAdding those two: 216 + 720. Let me add the hundreds: 700 + 200 = 900, the tens: 20 + 10 = 30, and the units: 6. Wait, actually, 216 is 200+16, so adding 720 (700+20) +216 (200+16):\n\n700 + 200 = 900; 20 + 16 = 36; so total is 900 + 36 = 936. Yep.\n\nAlternatively, adding vertically:\n\n\u2003216\n\n+720\n\n------\n\nAdding the units digits: 6 + 0 =6\n\nTens digits: 1 + 2 = 3\n\nHundreds digits: 2 +7 =9\n\nSo that gives 936. Perfect.\n\nHmm, seems like both methods give the same result. Maybe I can check using another method to be sure. Let me try the lattice method or another approach.\n\nAlternatively, think of 72 \u00d713 as (70 +2)(10 +3). Expanding this using FOIL:\n\nFirst: 70\u00d710=700\n\nOuter:70\u00d73=210\n\nInner:2\u00d710=20\n\nLast:2\u00d73=6\n\nAdding them all up:700 +210 is 910, plus 20 is 930, plus 6 is 936. Yep, same answer.\n\nAlternatively, maybe breaking down 13 into 12 +1? Let's see:\n\n72 \u00d713 =72\u00d7(12+1)=72\u00d712 +72\u00d71.\n\n72\u00d712: 72\u00d710=720, 72\u00d72=144, so 720+144=864. Then add 72\u00d71=72. So 864 +72 = 936. Same result again.\n\nOkay, so all different methods lead to 936, so I think that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, using calculator? Well, since I can't use a calculator here, but I can verify step by step.\n\nAlternatively, let me check using division. If 72\u00d713=936, then dividing 936 by 13 should give 72. Let me try that.\n\n936 \u00f713. Let's see how many times 13 goes into 93. 13\u00d77=91. So 7 times with a remainder of 2. Bring down the 6, making 26. 13\u00d72=26, so that's 2. So total is 72. Perfect, that checks out.\n\nAlternatively, 13\u00d770=910. Then 13\u00d72=26. 910+26=936. Yep.\n\nAlright, so I think the answer is 936. All methods confirm it, so I'm confident that's correct.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 72 and 13 is \\boxed{936}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 72 and 13, we can use the distributive property by breaking down 13 into 10 and 3:\n\n1. Calculate \\(72 \\times 10\\):\n   \\[\n   72 \\times 10 = 720\n   \\]\n\n2. Calculate \\(72 \\times 3\\):\n   \\[\n   72 \\times 3 = 216\n   \\]\n\n3. Add the results of the two calculations:\n   \\[\n   720 + 216 = 936\n   \\]\n\nTo verify, we can use another method such as expanding \\((70 + 2)(10 + 3)\\) using the distributive property (FOIL):\n- First: \\(70 \\times 10 = 700\\)\n- Outer: \\(70 \\times 3 = 210\\)\n- Inner: \\(2 \\times 10 = 20\\)\n- Last: \\(2 \\times 3 = 6\\)\n\nAdding these results together:\n\\[\n700 + 210 + 20 + 6 = 936\n\\]\n\nAdditionally, verifying by division:\n\\[\n936 \\div 13 = 72\n\\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 72 and 13 is \\(\\boxed{936}\\).",
