      "text": "18 + 540 = 558. So the product is 558. Wait, that seems right? Let me check.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another method to verify. Like the distributive property. 18 \u00d7 31 can be written as 18 \u00d7 (30 + 1) = 18\u00d730 + 18\u00d71. Which is exactly what I did before. 18\u00d730 is 540, plus 18\u00d71 is 18, so 540+18=558. Yep, that matches. So that gives me confidence.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do 31 \u00d7 18 instead. Let me try that way. 31 \u00d7 18. Let's see if that's easier or not.\n\nSo, 31 on top and 18 below.\n\n\u2003\u00a031.\n\u00d7\u00a018.\n-------.\n\nAgain, starting with the ones place: 8 \u00d7 31. 8\u00d71 is 8, 8\u00d73 is 24. So that would be 248. Then, moving to the tens place of the bottom number, which is 1 (tens place), so 1\u00d731 is 31, but shifted one position to the left (since it's the tens place), so that's 310. Then adding 248 + 310 = 558. Same result. Okay, so that checks out too.\n\nAnother way: maybe breaking down the numbers into smaller parts. Let's see, 18 can be 20 - 2, and 31 is 30 +1. Hmm, maybe that complicates it, but let me try.\n\n(20 - 2)(30 +1) = 20\u00d730 + 20\u00d71 - 2\u00d730 - 2\u00d71. That would be 600 +20 -60 -2. So 600+20 is 620, minus 60 is 560, minus 2 is 558. Wow, that also works. So that gives me the same answer. So that's a third method confirming it's 558.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using the area model. If I imagine a rectangle with sides 18 and 31, the area would be the product. Breaking down the sides into tens and ones. 18 is 10 +8, and 31 is 30+1. So the area would be (10+8)(30+1) = 10\u00d730 + 10\u00d71 +8\u00d730 +8\u00d71. Calculating each part: 300 +10 +240 +8. Adding those up: 300+240 is 540, plus 10 is 550, plus 8 is 558. Yep, same answer again.\n\nHmm, seems like all methods are pointing to 558. Maybe I can check using a calculator, but since I don't have one here, let me try another approach. Let's see, 18 times 30 is 540, so adding another 18 gives 540+18=558. That's straightforward.\n\nAlternatively, 31 times 20 would be 620, but since we have 18 instead of 20, subtract 2 times 31 which is 62, so 620-62=558. That works too. So that's another way: 31\u00d7(20-2)=31\u00d718=558.\n\nWait, let me verify that subtraction: 620 minus 62. 620 - 60 is 560, minus 2 is 558. Correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the fact that 18 is 9\u00d72, so 9\u00d72\u00d731. Let me compute 9\u00d731 first. 9\u00d730=270, 9\u00d71=9, so total 279. Then multiply by 2: 279\u00d72=558. That also works.\n\nHmm, all these different methods are giving me the same result, so I think 558 is the correct answer. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations. Let me just recap once again using the standard algorithm step by step to ensure I didn't make a mistake there.\n\nFirst step: Multiply 18 by 1 (the ones digit of 31):\n\n1 (from 31) \u00d7 8 (from 18) = 8.\n\n1 \u00d7 1 (tens place) = 1. So first partial product is 18.\n\nSecond step: Multiply 18 by 3 (the tens digit of 31, which represents 30). So:\n\n3 \u00d78 =24. Write down 4 and carry over 2.\n\n3\u00d71=3 plus the carried over 2 =5. So the second partial product is 54, but since it's the tens place, we shift one position to the left, making it 540.\n\nThen add 18 +540. 540 +10 is 550, plus 8 is 558. Exactly.\n\nAlright, that's solid. Another way, maybe using the commutative property, 31\u00d718, which I did before, also gives the same. I think that's thorough. I can't think of any other way to check. All the different methods cross-verify to 558. So I'm confident that's the correct product.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 18 and 31 is \\boxed{558}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 18 and 31, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n1. **Multiply 18 by the ones digit of 31 (which is 1):**\n   \\[\n   18 \\times 1 = 18\n   \\]\n\n2. **Multiply 18 by the tens digit of 31 (which is 3, representing 30):**\n   \\[\n   18 \\times 3 = 54\n   \\]\n   Since this is the tens place, we shift the result one position to the left, resulting in 540.\n\n3. **Add the partial products together:**\n   \\[\n   18 + 540 = 558\n   \\]\n\nWe can verify this using different methods:\n- **Distributive Property:**\n  \\[\n  18 \\times 31 = 18 \\times (30 + 1) = (18 \\times 30) + (18 \\times 1) = 540 + 18 = 558\n  \\]\n\n- **Breaking down numbers:**\n  \\[\n  (20 - 2) \\times (30 + 1) = 20 \\times 30 + 20 \\times 1 - 2 \\times 30 - 2 \\times 1 = 600 + 20 - 60 - 2 = 558\n  \\]\n\n- **Alternative multiplication order:**\n  \\[\n  31 \\times 18 = 31 \\times (20 - 2) = (31 \\times 20) - (31 \\times 2) = 620 - 62 = 558\n  \\]\n\nAll methods confirm the result is consistent. Therefore, the product of 18 and 31 is \\(\\boxed{558}\\).",
