2, so 4*10). 21 times 40 is 840. Then add those two results: 42 + 840. 42 plus 800 is 842, plus 40 more is 882. Yep, that's correct. \n\nAlternatively, using the standard algorithm:\n\n   21  \nx 42\n------\n   42   (21 x 2)\n+840   (21 x 40)\n------\n  882\n\nYes, adding those gives 882. \n\nI think that's consistent across all methods. I can't see any mistakes here, so I'm confident that the product of 21 and 42 is 882. \n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 21 and 42 is \\boxed{882}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 21 and 42, we can use the distributive property and verify through multiple methods:\n\n1. **Distributive Property**:\n   \\[\n   (20 + 1) \\times (40 + 2) = 20 \\times 40 + 20 \\times 2 + 1 \\times 40 + 1 \\times 2\n   \\]\n   Calculating each term:\n   \\[\n   20 \\times 40 = 800, \\quad 20 \\times 2 = 40, \\quad 1 \\times 40 = 40, \\quad 1 \\times 2 = 2\n   \\]\n   Adding these together:\n   \\[\n   800 + 40 + 40 + 2 = 882\n   \\]\n\n2. **Vertical Multiplication**:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n     21 \\\\\n   \\times 42 \\\\\n   \\hline\n     42 \\quad (\\text{21} \\times 2) \\\\\n   +840 \\quad (\\text{21} \\times 40) \\\\\n   \\hline\n     882 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Breaking Down Multiplication**:\n   \\[\n   21 \\times 42 = 21 \\times (40 + 2) = 21 \\times 40 + 21 \\times 2 = 840 + 42 = 882\n   \\]\n\n4. **Using Squared Terms**:\n   \\[\n   21 \\times 42 = 21 \\times (21 \\times 2) = (21^2) \\times 2 = 441 \\times 2 = 882\n   \\]\n\nAll methods consistently yield the result 882. Therefore, the product of 21 and 42 is \\(\\boxed{882}\\).",
