 me check one more time with another method. Maybe using the fact that 59*28 = (60 -1)*28 = 60*28 -28. \n\n60*28 is 1680 (since 6*28=168, so times 10 is 1680). Then subtract 28: 1680 -28 = 1652. That works too. \n\nAlternatively, 28*60 is 1680, so 28 less is 1652. \n\nAnother way: 28*59. Let me reverse the multiplication. 28 times 59. \n\n28*50 is 1400, 28*9 is 252. So 1400 +252 = 1652. Yep, same result. \n\nAlright, so all methods confirm that the product is 1652. I think that's solid. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations. So the answer should be 1652.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 59 and 28 is calculated as follows:\n\n1. **Using the standard multiplication algorithm:**\n   - Multiply 59 by 8 (ones place of 28):  \n     \\( 59 \\times 8 = 472 \\).  \n   - Multiply 59 by 20 (tens place of 28, shifted left by one digit):  \n     \\( 59 \\times 20 = 1180 \\).  \n   - Add the partial products:  \n     \\( 472 + 1180 = 1652 \\).\n\n2. **Verification with the distributive property:**  \n   Break down \\( 59 \\times 28 \\) into:  \n   \\( (60 - 1) \\times 28 = 60 \\times 28 - 1 \\times 28 \\).  \n   \\( 60 \\times 28 = 1680 \\),  \n   \\( 1680 - 28 = 1652 \\).\n\n3. **Alternative method (breaking into smaller parts):**  \n   \\( 59 \\times 28 = (50 + 9) \\times (20 + 8) \\).  \n   Expand and sum:  \n   \\( 50 \\times 20 = 1000 \\),  \n   \\( 50 \\times 8 = 400 \\),  \n   \\( 9 \\times 20 = 180 \\),  \n   \\( 9 \\times 8 = 72 \\).  \n   Total: \\( 1000 + 400 + 180 + 72 = 1652 \\).\n\nAll methods confirm the result is **1652**.\n\n**Answer:** The product of 59 and 28 is \\boxed{1652}.",
