tmost diagonal is just the 9 from the bottom right. Next diagonal: 8 (from 3x1) + 9 (from 9x1's tens place? Wait, maybe I'm mixing up. Let me think again.\n\nWait, perhaps I need to draw it out step by step. Since I might be getting confused. Let me try again.\n\nThe lattice grid for 39 \u00d7 61 would be 2x2 grid. The digits of 39 are on the top: 3 and 9. The digits of 61 are on the right side: 6 and 1.\n\nEach cell is divided by a diagonal line. So:\n\nTop row cells: first cell is 3 \u00d7 6, second is 3 \u00d7 1.\n\nSecond row cells: first is 9 \u00d7 6, second is 9 \u00d7 1.\n\nSo:\n\nFirst cell (top-left): 3\u00d76=18. So write 1 on the top half and 8 on the bottom half of the cell.\n\nSecond cell (top-right): 3\u00d71=3. Write 0 on top and 3 on bottom? Wait, no, if it's a single digit, maybe 0 top and 3 bottom? Or maybe leading zero. Hmm, perhaps better to write 0 and 3.\n\nThird cell (bottom-left): 9\u00d76=54. So top half 5, bottom half 4.\n\nFourth cell (bottom-right): 9\u00d71=9. So top half 0, bottom half 9.\n\nNow, the diagonals. The rightmost diagonal is just the bottom half of the top-right cell (3) and the bottom half of the bottom-right cell (9). Wait, actually, in lattice multiplication, the diagonals go from top-right to bottom-left. Let me recall the exact steps.\n\nThe diagonals split the cells, and you sum the numbers along each diagonal, carrying over if needed.\n\nStarting from the bottom right corner:\n\nThe first diagonal (the units place) is the bottom half of the bottom-right cell: 9.\n\nNext diagonal (tens place) is the bottom half of the top-right cell (3) plus the top half of the bottom-right cell (0). Wait, no. Maybe I need to visualize better.\n\nActually, each cell's bottom half is part of the same diagonal as the cell above to the right's top half.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps it's better to think of the diagonals as follows:\n\nThe rightmost diagonal is the bottom-right cell's bottom half (9). Then moving left, the next diagonal includes the top-right cell's bottom half (3) and the bottom-left cell's bottom half (4). Then the next diagonal includes the top-left cell's bottom half (8), the top-right cell's top half (0), and the bottom-left cell's top half (5). Then the leftmost diagonal is the top-left cell's top half (1).\n\nWait, that might not be accurate. Let me look up the steps mentally.\n\nActually, in lattice multiplication, each cell's bottom triangle is part of the same diagonal as the next cell's top triangle to the right. Hmm, perhaps this is getting too convoluted. Maybe I should just proceed step by step.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I should accept that since all other methods give 2379, and the lattice method is error-prone for me without drawing, so maybe it's better to trust the previous methods. Since three different methods have confirmed the answer, I can be confident.\n\nAlternatively, maybe just use another approach. Let me think of 39\u00d760 as 39\u00d76\u00d710. 39\u00d76 is 234, so 234\u00d710 is 2340. Then add 39\u00d71 which is 39. So 2340+39=2379. Yep.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps use the fact that 40\u00d760 = 2400, and then adjust. Since 39 is one less than 40 and 61 is one more than 60. So (40 -1)(60 +1) = 40\u00d760 +40\u00d71 -1\u00d760 -1\u00d71=2400 +40-60-1=2400 -20 -1=2379. Which again gives the same result. \n\nSo all these different methods are converging to the same answer, so I can be confident that 2379 is correct.\n\nI think that's enough verification. The key insight was recognizing that 39 and 61 are equidistant from 50, so using the difference of squares simplifies the calculation. But even without that, straightforward multiplication confirms the result. So I can confidently say the product is 2379.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 39 and 61 is \\boxed{2379}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 39 and 61, we can use the difference of squares formula. Recognizing that 39 and 61 are equidistant from 50, we rewrite them as \\(50 - 11\\) and \\(50 + 11\\)