ng them gives 1100. \n\nAlternatively, if I convert them into numbers in terms of hundreds or something else, but I think that's complicating. Let me try fractions. 50 is 5*10, 22 is 22. So 5*22 is 110, then times 10 gives 1100. \n\nAlternatively, 22 multiplied by 50 is the same as (20 + 2) \u00d750 = 20\u00d750 + 2\u00d750 = 1000 + 100 = 1100. Yeah, that's the same as before. \n\nI think I've checked it multiple ways. All the methods lead to 1100, so I'm pretty sure that's the correct answer. Maybe if I write it vertically again:\n\n\u2003\u200322  \n\u2003\u00d750  \n\u2003------  \n\u2003\u200300 (22\u00d70)  \n+1100 (22\u00d75 shifted one position to the left)  \n=1100. \n\nYep, that's correct. I don't see any errors in my calculations. So I think I can confidently say the product of 50 and 22 is 1100.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 50 and 22 is \\boxed{1100}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 50 and 22, we can use multiple methods to verify the result:\n\n1. **Breaking down 50 into 5 and 10:**\n   \\[\n   50 \\times 22 = (5 \\times 10) \\times 22 = 5 \\times (10 \\times 22) = 5 \\times 220 = 1100\n   \\]\n\n2. **Using the distributive property:**\n   \\[\n   50 \\times 22 = 50 \\times (20 + 2) = (50 \\times 20) + (50 \\times 2) = 1000 + 100 = 1100\n   \\]\n\n3. **Standard multiplication method:**\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n     \\quad 22 \\\\\n   \\times 50 \\\\\n   \\hline\n     \\quad 0 \\quad \\text{(22 \u00d7 0)} \\\\\n   + 1100 \\quad \\text{(22 \u00d7 5 shifted one position left)} \\\\\n   \\hline\n     1100 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Alternative approach using 100:**\n   \\[\n   22 \\times 100 = 2200 \\quad \\text{and then halving it} \\quad \\frac{2200}{2} = 1100\n   \\]\n\nAll methods consistently yield the result 1100. \n\nThe product of 50 and 22 is \\boxed{1100}.",
