's applicable here. Alternatively, think of 45 as half of 90, so 45 \u00d792 = (90/2) \u00d792 = 90\u00d792 /2. \n\nCalculating 90\u00d792: 90\u00d790 is 8100, plus 90\u00d72 is 180, so total 8100+180=8280. Divide by 2 is 4140. So that's another way. \n\nSo all methods are giving me 4140, so I think that's the correct product. \n\nWait, just to make sure, maybe I can use another approach. Let me do the multiplication in another order. 45 multiplied by 92. Let's think of 92 as 100 - 8. So 45\u00d7(100 -8) = 45\u00d7100 -45\u00d78 = 4500 - 360 = 4140. Yep, same result. \n\nAlright, so after multiple methods and cross-verifying, I'm confident that the product of 45 and 92 is indeed 4140. I don't think I made a mistake here. \n\nTherefore, the answer should be 4140.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 45 and 92 is \\boxed{4140}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 45 and 92, we can use the standard multiplication method:\n\n1. Set up the multiplication vertically:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n    92 \\\\\n   \\times 45 \\\\\n   \\hline\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n2. Multiply 92 by the units digit of 45 (which is 5):\n   \\[\n   92 \\times 5 = 460\n   \\]\n\n3. Multiply 92 by the tens digit of 45 (which is 4, representing 40), and shift the result one place to the left:\n   \\[\n   92 \\times 4 = 368 \\quad \\text{(then shift left by one digit to get 3680)}\n   \\]\n\n4. Add the two partial products:\n   \\[\n   460 + 3680 = 4140\n   \\]\n\nVerification using different methods:\n- **Distributive Property**: \\(45 \\times 92 = 45 \\times (90 + 2) = 45 \\times 90 + 45 \\times 2 = 4050 + 90 = 4140\\)\n- **Another Breakdown**: \\(45 \\times 92 = (40 + 5) \\times 92 = 40 \\times 92 + 5 \\times 92 = 3680 + 460 = 4140\\)\n- **Difference of Squares Trick**: \\(45 \\times 92 = \\frac{90 \\times 92}{2} = \\frac{8280}{2} = 4140\\)\n- **Alternative Representation**: \\(45 \\times 92 = 45 \\times (100 - 8) = 4500 - 360 = 4140\\)\n\nAll methods confirm the result is consistent. \n\nThe product of 45 and 92 is \\(\\boxed{4140}\\).",
