      "index": 0,
      "text": "2607? Wait, let me check that addition again.\n\nWait, 2310 plus 297. Let's see, 2310 + 200 is 2510, then +97 is 2607. Yeah, that seems right.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can cross-verify using another method to make sure I didn't make a mistake. Maybe the distributive property. Let me think.\n\n33 times 79 can be thought of as (30 + 3)(70 + 9). Then expanding that:\n\n30*70 + 30*9 + 3*70 + 3*9.\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n30*70 is 2100,\n\n30*9 is 270,\n\n3*70 is 210,\n\n3*9 is 27.\n\nAdding all those together: 2100 + 270 = 2370; 210 +27 = 237. Then 2370 + 237 = 2607. Same result. Okay, that matches.\n\nAlternatively, maybe another way. Let me try using the difference of squares or some other trick. Hmm, 33 and 79. Not sure if that applies here, but perhaps.\n\nWait, 33 times 79. Let me see, maybe express 79 as 80 -1. Then 33*(80 -1) = 33*80 - 33*1. 33*80 is 2640, minus 33 is 2640 -33 = 2607. Oh, that works too. That was easier!\n\nHmm, so that method is quicker. Let me confirm that again:\n\n33 * 80 = (30*80) + (3*80) = 2400 +240 = 2640. Then subtract 33 gives 2640 -33 = 2607. Yep, same answer.\n\nSo all methods lead to 2607. Therefore, I think that's correct. But just to be thorough, let me do the standard multiplication again to check.\n\nMultiplying 33 by 79:\n\nFirst, 33 \u00d7 9:\n\n9 \u00d7 3 is 27, write down 7 and carry over 2.\n\nThen 9 \u00d7 3 is 27 plus the carried 2 is 29. So that gives 297.\n\nThen 33 \u00d770:\n\nSince it's the tens place, we can think of it as 33 \u00d77 \u00d710.\n\n33 \u00d77: 7\u00d73 is 21, write down 1 and carry 2. 7\u00d73 is 21 plus 2 is 23. So 231, then multiply by 10 gives 2310.\n\nAdding 297 +2310:\n\n2310 + 200 is 2510, plus 97 is 2607. Yep, same result.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use a calculator? Wait, but since I can't use a calculator, but I can do another verification.\n\nLet me divide 2607 by 33 and see if I get 79.\n\n2607 \u00f733. Let's see, 33 \u00d770 is 2310. 2607 -2310 = 297. Then 297 \u00f733 is 9. So total is 70 +9 =79. Perfect, that checks out.\n\nAlternatively, 2607 divided by 79. Let me see, 79 \u00d730 is 2370. Subtract that from 2607: 2607 -2370 = 237. Then 79 \u00d73 is 237. So 30 +3=33. Yep, that works too.\n\nTherefore, I can be confident that the product is 2607.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 33 and 79 is \\boxed{2607}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 33 and 79, we can use multiple methods to ensure accuracy.\n\n### Method 1: Standard Multiplication\n1. Multiply 33 by 9:\n   \\[\n   33 \\times 9 = 297\n   \\]\n2. Multiply 33 by 70 (since 7 is in the tens place):\n   \\[\n   33 \\times 70 = 2310\n   \\]\n3. Add the results of the two partial products:\n   \\[\n   297 + 2310 = 2607\n   \\]\n\n### Method 2: Distributive Property\nExpand the product using the distributive property:\n\\[\n(30 + 3)(70 + 9) = 30 \\times 70 + 30 \\times 9 + 3 \\times 70 + 3 \\times 9\n\\]\nCalculate each term:\n\\[\n30 \\times 70 = 2100, \\quad 30 \\times 9 = 270, \\quad 3 \\times 70 = 210, \\quad 3 \\times 9 = 27\n\\]\nSum the terms:\n\\[\n2100 + 270 + 210 + 27 = 2607\n\\]\n\n### Method 3: Difference of Squares Trick\nRewrite 79 as \\(80 - 1\\):\n\\[\n33 \\times (80 - 1) = 33 \\times 80 - 33 \\times 1\n\\]\nCalculate each term:\n\\[\n33 \\times 80 = 2640, \\quad 33 \\times 1 = 33\n\\]\nSubtract the results:\n\\[\n2640 - 33 = 2607\n\\]\n\n### Verification\nDivide the result by one of the factors to check:\n\\[\n2607 \\div 33 = 79 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 2607 \\div 79 = 33\n\\]\nBoth divisions confirm the result is correct.\n\n### Final Answer\nThe product of 33 and 79 is \\boxed{2607}.",
