      "index": 0,
      "text": "2790?\n\nWait, let me check that again. Maybe another way to verify. Maybe using the standard multiplication algorithm. Let me write it out in my mind. \n\n   90\nx 31\n-------\nMultiply 90 by 1 first. That's 90. Then multiply 90 by 30. Wait, but in the standard way, you do 90 x 1 (units place) and then 90 x 3 (tens place), but shifted one position. Let me see:\n\nSo:\n\n     90\nx   31\n-------\n     90  (this is 90 x 1)\n+2700   (this is 90 x 30, but written as 90 x 3 shifted one to the left)\n-------\n    2790\n\nYes, that matches. So that seems right. \n\nAlternatively, maybe using the distributive property: 90*(30 +1) = 90*30 +90*1 = 2700 +90=2790. Yep, same result.\n\nIs there another way to check? Maybe dividing the answer by one of the numbers to see if we get the other. Let's say 2790 divided by 90. Let's see: 2790 \u00f790. Well, 90 goes into 2790 how many times? 90*30 is 2700, so 2790 -2700 is 90, so that's 30 +1 =31. So that checks out. Alternatively, 2790 divided by 31. Let me do that division. 31 times 90 is 2790. Let me see: 31*90. 30*90=2700, and 1*90=90, so again 2790. So that works. \n\nHmm, so all methods lead to 2790. I think that's correct. Maybe I can also use a calculator, but since I don't have one, but the manual calculations check out. So I think the product is 2790.\n\nWait, but just to make sure I didn't make any addition errors. 2700 +90. 2700 plus 100 would be 2800, so minus 10 is 2790. Yep, that's right. Okay, I'm confident now. The answer should be 2790.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 90 and 31 is calculated as follows:\n\n\\[\n90 \\times 31 = 90 \\times (30 + 1) = (90 \\times 30) + (90 \\times 1) = 2700 + 90 = 2790\n\\]\n\n**Answer:** 2790",
