uld be 64. Let me check that. \n\nDividing 832 by 13. Let's see. 13*60=780. 832-780=52. 52 divided by13 is 4. So total is 60+4=64. That works. \n\nSimilarly, 832 divided by 64 should be 13. Let's see. 64*10=640, subtract that from 832: 832-640=192. 64*3=192, so total is 10+3=13. Perfect. \n\nTherefore, I'm confident the product is 832.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 64 and 13 is \\boxed{832}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 64 and 13, we can use the distributive property by breaking down the numbers into smaller parts:\n\n1. Calculate \\(64 \\times 10\\):\n   \\[\n   64 \\times 10 = 640\n   \\]\n\n2. Calculate \\(64 \\times 3\\):\n   \\[\n   64 \\times 3 = 192\n   \\]\n\n3. Add the results of the two calculations:\n   \\[\n   640 + 192 = 832\n   \\]\n\nWe can verify this result using different methods:\n- Using the standard multiplication algorithm:\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{r}\n    \\quad 64 \\\\\n  \\times 13 \\\\\n  \\hline\n    \\quad 192 \\quad (\\text{which is } 64 \\times 3) \\\\\n  + 640 \\quad \\ (\\text{which is } 64 \\times 10 \\text{ shifted one position to the left}) \\\\\n  \\hline\n    832 \\\\\n  \\end{array}\n  \\]\n\n- Checking with division:\n  \\[\n  832 \\div 13 = 64 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 832 \\div 64 = 13\n  \\]\n\nBoth verification methods confirm the result is correct.\n\n### Final Answer\nThe product of 64 and 13 is \\boxed{832}.",
