ives 1+2+0=3. Then add the carried-over 1 from the tens place, making it 4. So hundreds digit is 4, no carryover.\n\n4. Thousands place: Cell1's top-left (2). No other contributions here. So thousands digit is 2.\n\nPutting it all together: 2 (thousands), 4 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 9 (units) \u2192 2449. There we go! I must have made a mistake in my previous breakdown by not considering that the tens place includes both Cell2 and Cell3's bottom-right parts. \n\nSo the lattice method, when done correctly, also gives 2449. That confirms the answer again. \n\nTherefore, all methods lead to 2449, so I can be confident that the product of 31 and 79 is indeed 2449. I think that's solid. Just to recap, the difference of squares was a neat shortcut here because the numbers were symmetrically placed around 55, but even without that, standard multiplication works. It's good to verify with multiple methods to ensure there's no arithmetic error. I might have made a mistake in my initial lattice approach because I miscounted the cells contributing to each diagonal, but upon correcting that, it aligns with the other methods. So, final answer is 2449.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 31 and 79 is \\boxed{2449}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 31 and 79, we can use the difference of squares formula. \n\nFirst, we note that 31 and 79 are equidistant from 55:\n\\[\n31 = 55 - 24 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 79 = 55 + 24\n\\]\nUsing the difference of squares formula \\((a - b)(a + b) = a^2 - b^2\\), we have:\n\\[\n(55 - 24)(55 + 24) = 55^2 - 24^2\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term separately:\n\\[\n55^2 = (50 + 5)^2 = 50^2 + 2 \\cdot 50 \\cdot 5 + 5^2 = 2500 + 500 + 25 = 3025\n\\]\n\\[\n24^2 = (20 + 4)^2 = 20^2 + 2 \\cdot 20 \\cdot 4 + 4^2 = 400 + 160 + 16 = 576\n\\]\n\nSubtracting these results:\n\\[\n3025 - 576 = 2449\n\\]\n\nTo verify, we use standard multiplication:\n\\[\n31 \\times 79 = 31 \\times (70 + 9) = 31 \\times 70 + 31 \\times 9 = 2170 + 279 = 2449\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm