170 plus 26, which is 1196. That seems right.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can think of 13\u00d792 as (10 +3)(90 +2). Expanding that:\n\n10\u00d790 = 900,\n\n10\u00d72 =20,\n\n3\u00d790=270,\n\n3\u00d72=6.\n\nAdding all those: 900 +20 +270 +6 = 900+20 is 920, plus 270 is 1190, plus 6 is 1196. Yep, same answer again.\n\nHmm, so all methods are giving me 1196. That gives me more confidence that the answer is correct. I think I can be sure that 13 multiplied by 92 is 1196.\n\nWait, but just to be thorough, let me check division as the inverse operation. If I divide 1196 by 13, do I get 92?\n\nLet me do that division. 13 into 1196. How many times does 13 go into 119?\n\n13\u00d79 is 117. So 9 times, with a remainder of 2. Bring down the 6, making 26. 13 goes into 26 exactly 2 times. So total is 92. So yes, 13\u00d792=1196. That checks out.\n\nAlternatively, 1196 divided by 92 should be 13. Let me try that division as well.\n\n92 into 1196. 92\u00d713 is 1196, so that's straightforward. Let me see: 92\u00d710=920, subtract that from 1196: 1196-920=276. 92\u00d73=276. So 10+3=13. Perfect.\n\nAlright, so after multiple methods and checks, I'm confident the product is 1196. I think that's the correct answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 13 and 92 is \\boxed{1196}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 13 and 92, we can use the distributive property by breaking down the numbers into smaller parts:\n\n1. Split 92 into 90 and 2:\n   \\[\n   13 \\times 92 = 13 \\times (90 + 2)\n   \\]\n2. Calculate each part separately:\n   \\[\n   13 \\times 90 = 1170\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   13 \\times 2 = 26\n   \\]\n3. Add the results of the two parts:\n   \\[\n   1170 + 26 = 1196\n   \\]\n\nWe can verify this result using another method by expanding the numbers differently:\n1. Split 13 into 10 and 3:\n   \\[\n   92 \\times 13 = 92 \\times (10 + 3)\n   \\]\n2. Calculate each part separately:\n   \\[\n   92 \\times 10 = 920\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   92 \\times 3 = 276\n   \\]\n3. Add the results of the two parts:\n   \\[\n   920 + 276 = 1196\n   \\]\n\nAdditionally, using the standard multiplication algorithm:\n1. Multiply 13 by 2:\n   \\[\n   13 \\times 2 = 26\n   \\]\n2. Multiply 13 by 90 (shifted one position to the left):\n   \\[\n   13 \\times 90 = 1170\n   \\]\n3. Add the results:\n   \\[\n   26 + 1170 = 1196\n   \\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 13 and 92 is \\boxed{1196}.",
