      "text": "4500 minus 75 equals 4425. Wait, let me check that subtraction again. 4500 minus 75 is like subtracting 100 and then adding back 25, right? So 4500 - 100 = 4400, plus 25 gives 4425. Yeah, that's correct. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can verify this another way to be sure I didn't make a mistake. Let's try multiplying 59 by 75 the standard way. Let me write it out vertically:\n\n\u2003\u200359  \n\u00d7\u00a075  \n\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014  \nFirst, multiply 59 by 5. 5\u00d79 is 45, write down 5 and carry over 4. Then 5\u00d75 is 25 plus 4 is 29. So that gives 295. \n\nNext, multiply 59 by 70 (since the 7 is in the tens place). So that's 59\u00d77\u00d710. Let me compute 59\u00d77 first. 7\u00d79 is 63, write down 3 and carry over 6. 7\u00d75 is 35 plus 6 is 41. So that's 413, then multiply by 10 gives 4130. \n\nNow add the two results: 295 + 4130. Let's see: 295 + 4130. 295 + 4000 is 4295, then +130 is 4425. Yep, same result. So that checks out. \n\nHmm, so both methods give me 4425, so that must be the correct answer. I think that's solid. \n\nAnother way to cross-verify could be using another decomposition. Maybe breaking down 75 into 25\u00d73? Let's see. 59 \u00d7 75 = 59 \u00d7 (25 \u00d7 3) = (59 \u00d7 25) \u00d73. Hmm, 59\u00d725 is... Well, 25\u00d74 is 100, so 59\u00d725 can be thought of as (60 -1)\u00d725 = 1500 -25 = 1475. Then multiply that by 3: 1475 \u00d73. Let's do 1400\u00d73 =4200 and 75\u00d73=225, so adding those gives 4200 + 225 = 4425. Again, the same answer. \n\nSo, three different methods all leading to 4425. That gives me confidence that the answer is correct. \n\nAlternatively, maybe using the difference of squares or some other algebraic identity? Let me think. 59 and 75 aren't obviously part of a difference of squares, but let's see. \n\nWait, 59 and 75 average to... (59 +75)/2 = 134/2 =67. So 59 is 67 minus 8, and 75 is 67 plus 8. Wait, no, 67 +8 is 75, but 67-8 is 59. Wait, that's exactly a difference of squares scenario. Because (a - b)(a + b) = a\u00b2 - b\u00b2. \n\nWait a second! If I let a = 67 and b =8, then (67 -8)(67 +8) =67\u00b2 -8\u00b2. So that would be 59\u00d775. Let me compute that:\n\n67 squared is... 60\u00b2 + 2\u00d760\u00d77 +7\u00b2 = 3600 + 840 +49 = 4489. Then 8 squared is 64. So 4489 -64 = 4425. Wow, that's another method and still the same answer. That's pretty cool. \n\nSo, using the difference of squares identity also gives 4425. Therefore, this must be the correct product. \n\nI think I've verified it enough ways. All methods converge to 4425, so I can confidently say that the product of 59 and 75 is 4425.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 59 and 75 is \\boxed{4425}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 59 and 75, we can use multiple methods to ensure accuracy:\n\n1. **Using the distributive property:**\n   \\[\n   59 \\times 75 = (60 - 1) \\times 75 = 60 \\times 75 - 1 \\times 75\n   \\]\n   - Calculate \\(60 \\times 75\\):\n     \\[\n     60 \\times 75 = 4500\n     \\]\n   - Subtract \\(1 \\times 75\\):\n     \\[\n     4500 - 75 = 4425\n     \\]\n\n2. **Standard multiplication algorithm:**\n   - Multiply 59 by 5:\n     \\[\n     59 \\times 5 = 295\n     \\]\n   - Multiply 59 by 70 (since 7 is in the tens place):\n     \\[\n     59 \\times 70 = 4130\n     \\]\n   - Add the results:\n     \\[\n     295 + 4130 = 4425\n     \\]\n\n3. **Using decomposition of 75:**\n   \\[\n   59 \\times 75 = 59 \\times (25 \\times 3) = (59 \\times 25) \\times 3\n   \\]\n   - Calculate \\(59 \\times 25\\):\n     \\[\n     (60 - 1) \\times 25 = 1500 - 25 = 1475\n     \\]\n   - Multiply by 3:\n     \\[\n     1475 \\times 3 = 4425\n     \\]\n\n4. **Using the difference of squares:**\n   \\[\n   59 \\times 75 = (67 - 8)(67 + 8) = 67^2 - 8^2\n   \\]\n   - Calculate \\(67^2\\) and \\(8^2\\):\n     \\[\n     67^2 = 4489 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 8^2 = 64\n     \\]\n   - Subtract the squares:\n     \\[\n     4489 - 64 = 4425\n     \\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 59 and 75 is \\(\\boxed{4425}\\).",
