a calculator method in my mind. 44 multiplied by 96.\n\nAlternatively, 44*96. Let me think of 44*100 = 4400, subtract 44*4 (which is 176), so 4400 - 176 = 4224. Yep, that works.\n\nAlternatively, maybe 44*96 is the same as (40 + 4)*(90 +6) as I did before. Either way, all the methods are leading me to 4224. I don't think I made a mistake here, since all methods cross-check. So I think that's the correct product.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 44 and 96 is \\boxed{4224}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 44 and 96, we can use multiple methods to ensure accuracy:\n\n1. **Distributive Property**:\n   \\[\n   (40 + 4)(90 + 6) = 40 \\times 90 + 40 \\times 6 + 4 \\times 90 + 4 \\times 6\n   \\]\n   Calculating each term:\n   \\[\n   40 \\times 90 = 3600, \\quad 40 \\times 6 = 240, \\quad 4 \\times 90 = 360, \\quad 4 \\times 6 = 24\n   \\]\n   Adding these together:\n   \\[\n   3600 + 240 + 360 + 24 = 3600 + 240 = 3840 \\\\\n   3840 + 360 = 4200 \\\\\n   4200 + 24 = 4224\n   \\]\n\n2. **Using Near 100 Trick**:\n   \\[\n   44 \\times 96 = 44 \\times (100 - 4) = 44 \\times 100 - 44 \\times 4\n   \\]\n   Calculating each term:\n   \\[\n   44 \\times 100 = 4400, \\quad 44 \\times 4 = 176\n   \\]\n   Subtracting these:\n   \\[\n   4400 - 176 = 4224\n   \\]\n\n3. **Standard Algorithm**:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n     44 \\\\\n   \\times 96 \\\\\n   \\hline\n     264 \\quad (\\text{44} \\times 6) \\\\\n   + 3960 \\quad (\\text{44} \\times 90 \\text{ shifted one position left}) \\\\\n   \\hline\n     4224 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 44 and 96 is \\(\\boxed{4224}\\).",
