 the multiplication is correct.\n\nTherefore, I think the answer is 4472.\n\nWait, but just to make sure I didn't make a mistake in my initial calculations. Let me recompute each step once again.\n\nFirst method:\n\n(50 + 2)*(80 +6) = 50*80 +50*6 +2*80 +2*6\n\n50*80: 50*80=4000\n\n50*6=300\n\n2*80=160\n\n2*6=12\n\nAdding them: 4000 + 300 =4300; 4300 +160=4460; 4460 +12=4472.\n\nSecond method:\n\n52\n\nx86\n\nMultiply 52 by 6: 52*6=312\n\nMultiply 52 by 80: 52*80=4160\n\nTotal: 312+4160=4472.\n\nThird method: 50*86 +2*86=4300 +172=4472.\n\nAll methods lead to the same result, so I think that's correct. Therefore, the product of 52 and 86 is 4472. I think that's the answer. I can't see any mistakes in the steps, so I'll go with that.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 52 and 86 is \\boxed{4472}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 52 and 86, we can use the distributive property by breaking down the numbers into smaller parts:\n\n\\[\n(50 + 2)(80 + 6)\n\\]\n\nExpanding this expression, we get:\n\n\\[\n50 \\times 80 + 50 \\times 6 + 2 \\times 80 + 2 \\times 6\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term individually:\n\n1. \\(50 \\times 80 = 4000\\)\n2. \\(50 \\times 6 = 300\\)\n3. \\(2 \\times 80 = 160\\)\n4. \\(2 \\times 6 = 12\\)\n\nAdding these results together:\n\n\\[\n4000 + 300 + 160 + 12\n\\]\n\nFirst, add \\(4000 + 300\\):\n\n\\[\n4000 + 300 = 4300\n\\]\n\nNext, add \\(4300 + 160\\):\n\n\\[\n4300 + 160 = 4460\n\\]\n\nFinally, add \\(4460 + 12\\):\n\n\\[\n4460 + 12 = 4472\n\\]\n\nTo verify, we can use another method by multiplying directly:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   52 \\\\\n\\times 86 \\\\\n\\hline\n   312 \\quad (\\text{52} \\times 6) \\\\\n+4160 \\quad (\\text{52} \\times 80) \\\\\n\\hline\n  4472 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 52 and 86 is \\(\\boxed{4472}\\).",
