e second partial product is 63*(30) = 63*3*10. So first compute 63*3 = 189, then multiply by 10 to get 1890. Then adding 252 + 1890 = 2142. So that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, when writing it out:\n\n   63\nx 34\n-----\n  252  (63 x 4)\n+1890 (63 x 30)\n-----\n 2142\n\nYes, that's exactly it. So all the steps check out. Therefore, the product of 63 and 34 is indeed 2142. I don't see any errors in my calculations here, and multiple methods confirm the same result. I think that's solid.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 63 and 34 is \\boxed{2142}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 63 and 34, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n1. Multiply 63 by the units digit of 34 (which is 4):\n   \\[\n   63 \\times 4 = 252\n   \\]\n2. Multiply 63 by the tens digit of 34 (which is 3, representing 30) and shift the result one position to the left:\n   \\[\n   63 \\times 30 = 1890\n   \\]\n3. Add the two partial products together:\n   \\[\n   252 + 1890 = 2142\n   \\]\n\nVerification using the distributive property:\n\\[\n(60 + 3)(30 + 4) = 60 \\times 30 + 60 \\times 4 + 3 \\times 30 + 3 \\times 4\n\\]\n\\[\n= 1800 + 240 + 90 + 12 = 2142\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 63 and 34 is \\(\\boxed{2142}\\).",
