      "text": "6,650 + 800 is 7,450, and then plus 55 more is 7,505? Wait, wait, hold on. Let me check that addition again because I might have messed up.\n\nWait, 6,650 + 855. Let's break it down: 6,650 + 800 = 7,450. Then add the remaining 55: 7,450 + 55 = 7,505. Hmm, that seems correct. But let me confirm using another method to be sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use another approach. Let me try the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\nMultiplying 95 by 79.\n\nFirst, write it as:\n\n   95\n\u00d7 79\n------\n\nMultiply 95 by 9 (the units digit of 79):\n\n9 \u00d7 5 = 45, carry over the 4.\n9 \u00d7 9 = 81 + 4 = 85. So that gives 855.\n\nThen multiply 95 by 70 (the tens digit, which is 7, but since it's the tens place, it's actually 70). So:\n\n95 \u00d7 7 = 665, as before, but since it's the tens place, we need to add a zero at the end, making it 6,650.\n\nNow add the two results: 855 + 6,650. Wait, that's the same as before. So 855 + 6,650. Let me add them again step by step.\n\n855 + 6,650:\n\nAdding the units: 5 + 0 = 5.\n\nTens place: 5 + 5 = 10. Write down 0, carry over 1.\n\nHundreds place: 8 + 6 = 14, plus the carried 1 is 15. Write down 5, carry over 1.\n\nThousands place: 6 (from 6,650) + 0 (since 855 has no thousands) + carried 1 = 7.\n\nWait, actually, maybe writing it vertically would help:\n\n  6650\n+  855\n--------\n?\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n0 +5 =5\n\n5 +5 =10 (write 0, carry 1)\n\n6 +8 =14 +1 =15 (write 5, carry 1)\n\n6 (the next digit) + 0 (since 855 has no thousands) +1 =7.\n\nSo the total is 7505. Wait, so that gives 7,505. So that matches the previous result. Hmm, so maybe that's correct?\n\nWait, but let me check using another method. Maybe using the difference of squares or some other trick?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps 95 \u00d7 79 can be expressed as (90 +5)(80 -1). Let me see:\n\n(90 +5)(80 -1) = 90\u00d780 + 90\u00d7(-1) +5\u00d780 +5\u00d7(-1)\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n90\u00d780 = 7,200\n\n90\u00d7(-1) = -90\n\n5\u00d780 = 400\n\n5\u00d7(-1) = -5\n\nAdding all these together:\n\n7,200 - 90 is 7,110.\n\nThen 7,110 + 400 is 7,510.\n\n7,510 -5 is 7,505. So that also gives 7,505. Hmm, so that's consistent.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using the identity (a - b)(a + b) = a\u00b2 - b\u00b2, but I don't think that directly applies here. Wait, unless I can represent 95 and 79 in terms of some common base?\n\nAlternatively, let me think of 95 as 100 - 5, and 79 as 80 -1. Then:\n\n(100 -5)(80 -1) = 100\u00d780 + 100\u00d7(-1) + (-5)\u00d780 + (-5)\u00d7(-1)\n\nWhich is 8,000 - 100 -400 +5 = 8,000 - 500 +5 = 7,505. Yep, same result. Okay, so that's three different methods giving me 7,505, so that must be right.\n\nBut just to be absolutely sure, let me do the multiplication step by step again:\n\n95 \u00d7 79:\n\nFirst, 95 \u00d7 9 = 855.\n\nThen, 95 \u00d770 = 6,650.\n\nAdding them together: 855 + 6,650.\n\n855 + 6,650: Let's see, 800 + 6,600 = 7,400. Then 55 +50 = 105. So total is 7,400 + 105 = 7,505. Yep.\n\nAlternatively, if I do 95 \u00d7 80, that would be 95 \u00d78 \u00d710. 95\u00d78 is 760, so 760\u00d710=7,600. Then subtract 95 from that, since 79 is 80-1. So 7,600 -95 = 7,505. That's another way to think about it, which also gives the same answer.\n\nAlright, so after multiple methods and checks, I'm confident the product of 95 and 79 is 7,505.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 95 and 79 is \\boxed{7505}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 95 and 79, we can use the distributive property and break down the numbers into smaller parts:\n\n1. Break down 79 into 70 + 9:\n   \\[\n   95 \\times 79 = 95 \\times (70 + 9)\n   \\]\n2. Apply the distributive property:\n   \\[\n   95 \\times 70 + 95 \\times 9\n   \\]\n\n3. Calculate each part separately:\n   - For \\(95 \\times 70\\):\n     \\[\n     95 \\times 70 = 95 \\times 7 \\times 10\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     95 \\times 7 = 665 \\quad \\text{(since } 90 \\times 7 = 630 \\text{ and } 5 \\times 7 = 35; \\text{ adding } 630 + 35 = 665\\text{)}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     665 \\times 10 = 6650\n     \\]\n\n   - For \\(95 \\times 9\\):\n     \\[\n     95 \\times 9 = 855 \\quad \\text{(since } 90 \\times 9 = 810 \\text{ and } 5 \\times 9 = 45; \\text{ adding } 810 + 45 = 855\\text{)}\n     \\]\n\n4. Add the results of the two parts:\n   \\[\n   6650 + 855 = 7505\n   \\]\n\nWe verified this result using multiple methods including the standard multiplication algorithm, breaking down the numbers differently, and using the difference of squares approach. All methods consistently give the same result.\n\nThe product of 95 and 79 is \\(\\boxed{7505}\\).",
