      "text": "3230 plus 3 equals 3233?\n\nWait, hold on a second. Let me check that again because adding 3000 + 180 gives 3180. Then 3180 + 50 is indeed 3230, and 3230 + 3 is 3233. Hmm, but I have a feeling that might not be right. Let me verify with another method to be sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should do the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's line them up:\n\n\u2003\u200361  \n\u00d7\u00a053  \n\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014  \nFirst multiply 61 by 3: 61\u00d73. 1\u00d73 is 3, 6\u00d73 is 18, so that gives 183.\n\nThen multiply 61 by 50 (since the 5 is in the tens place, so it's 50). 61\u00d750. Well, 61\u00d75 is 305, so adding a zero gives 3050.\n\nAdding those two results together: 183 + 3050. Let's see: 3050 + 100 is 3150, then plus 83 is 3233. Hmm, same result as before. So maybe it's correct?\n\nWait, but I feel like 60 times 50 is 3000, so 61 times 53 should be a bit more than that. 3000 plus 60*3 (180) plus 53*1 (53) so total 3000+180+53 = 3233? Wait, that's actually another way to think of it. Yeah, that seems consistent. So maybe I was right the first time. But why do I have a doubt?\n\nAlternatively, let me try using another method, perhaps the difference of squares? Since 61 and 53 are both around 57? Let me see. Wait, 61 and 53: their average is (61+53)/2 = 57, and the difference from the average is 4 and -4. So, (57 +4)(57-4) = 57\u00b2 - 4\u00b2. That's 57 squared minus 16. Let me compute 57 squared first. 50 squared is 2500, 7 squared is 49, and the cross term is 2*50*7=700. So 2500 + 700 +49 = 3249. Then subtract 16: 3249 -16 = 3233. Wait, so that also gives 3233. Okay, so three different methods all leading to 3233. Hmm, so maybe I was correct after all. But initially, I thought maybe I made a mistake, but now I'm more confident.\n\nAlternatively, let me just do straight multiplication step by step again. 61 times 53. Let me write it vertically:\n\n\u2003\u2003\u00a061  \n\u2003\u00d7\u00a053  \n\u2003\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500  \nFirst step: Multiply 61 by 3. 1\u00d73=3, 6\u00d73=18. So 183.\n\nSecond step: Multiply 61 by 5 (since the 5 is in the tens place, it's actually 50). But here, when we write it down, we shift one position to the left:\n\n\u2003\u200361  \n\u2003\u00d7\u00a053  \n\u2003\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500  \n\u2003\u00a0183  \n\u20033050 (but written as 305 shifted one left, so 3050)  \n\u2003\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500  \nAdding 183 + 3050. 3050 + 183: 3050 + 100 is 3150, plus 83 is 3233. Yep, same result.\n\nHmm, so all methods confirm 3233. Maybe my initial doubt was unnecessary. So the product of 61 and 53 is 3233.\n\nWait, but hold on, let me check with a calculator. Since I can't use an actual calculator, maybe I can compute 60*53 and then add 1*53. 60*53: 60*50 is 3000, 60*3 is 180, so 3180. Then add 53, so 3180 +53 is 3233. Yep, that works too. So yeah, I think that's right. So the answer is 3233.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 61 and 53 is \\boxed{3233}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 61 and 53, we can use the distributive property and break down the numbers:\n\n\\[\n61 \\times 53 = (60 + 1) \\times (50 + 3)\n\\]\n\nExpanding this, we get:\n\n\\[\n60 \\times 50 + 60 \\times 3 + 1 \\times 50 + 1 \\times 3\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term separately:\n\n\\[\n60 \\times 50 = 3000\n\\]\n\\[\n60 \\times 3 = 180\n\\]\n\\[\n1 \\times 50 = 50\n\\]\n\\[\n1 \\times 3 = 3\n\\]\n\nAdding all these terms together:\n\n\\[\n3000 + 180 + 50 + 3 = 3233\n\\]\n\nWe can verify this using the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   \\quad 61 \\\\\n\\times 53 \\\\\n\\hline\n   183 \\quad (\\text{61} \\times 3) \\\\\n+ 3050 \\quad (\\text{61} \\times 50) \\\\\n\\hline\n   3233 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nAnother verification using the difference of squares method:\n\n\\[\n(57 + 4)(57 - 4) = 57^2 - 4^2\n\\]\n\\[\n57^2 = 3249 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 4^2 = 16\n\\]\n\\[\n3249 - 16 = 3233\n\\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 61 and 53 is \\(\\boxed{3233}\\).",
