46. So first partial product is 462.\n\nThen multiply 77 by 60 (the tens digit; so shift one position to the left):\n\n7*6=42, write 2, carryover 4.\n\n7*6=42 plus 4 is 46. So the second partial product is 4620 (since it's shifted left by one).\n\nAdd 462 and 4620: 462 + 4620. Let's add 462 + 4620:\n\n4620\n\n+\u00a0 462\n\n= ?\n\nAdding units: 0+2=2\n\nTens: 2+6=8\n\nHundreds: 6 +4=10, write 0 carryover 1\n\nThousands: 4 + 0 (from 462's thousands) plus the carryover 1 = 5.\n\nWait, wait, hold on. Wait, 4620 is 4 thousands, 6 hundreds, 2 tens, 0 ones.\n\n462 is 4 hundreds, 6 tens, 2 ones.\n\nSo adding:\n\nThousands place: 4 (from 4620) + 0 (since 462 has no thousands) =4.\n\nHundreds place: 6 (from 4620) +4 (from 462) =10. So write 0 and carryover 1.\n\nTens place: 2 (from 4620) +6 (from 462) =8 plus the carried over 1 makes 9.\n\nOnes place: 0 +2=2.\n\nWait, that gives 5082? Wait no, wait:\n\nWait the thousands place after adding the hundreds carryover would be 4 +1 (carryover from hundreds)? Wait, let me write it vertically:\n\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 4 6 2 0\n\n+\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 4 6 2\n\n------------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\nOnes place: 0 +2 =2.\n\nTens place: 2 +6 =8.\n\nHundreds place:6 +4 =10. So write 0 and carryover 1.\n\nThousands place:4 (from 4620) + 0 (since 462 doesn't have a thousands digit) plus the carried over 1 =5.\n\nSo the total is 5 0 8 2. So 5082. Yes, correct.\n\nOkay, that's consistent. So I think that's the right answer. So the product of 77 and 66 is 5082.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can check using a calculator? But since I don't have one here, I'll have to rely on these methods. Since multiple methods give the same result, I can be confident it's correct. So yeah, the answer should be 5082.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 77 and 66 is \\boxed{5082}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 77 and 66, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm and verify using the distributive property.\n\nFirst, using the standard algorithm:\n\n1. Multiply 77 by 6 (the units digit of 66):\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n     77 \\\\\n   \\times 6 \\\\\n   \\hline\n     462 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n   Here, \\(7 \\times 6 = 42\\), write down 2 and carry over 4. Then \\(7 \\times 6 = 42\\) plus the carried over 4 gives 46.\n\n2. Multiply 77 by 60 (the tens digit of 66, shifted one position to the left):\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n     77 \\\\\n   \\times 60 \\\\\n   \\hline\n     4620 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n   Here, \\(77 \\times 6 = 462\\), then multiply by 10 to get 4620.\n\n3. Add the two results:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{r}\n     4620 \\\\\n   + \\quad 462 \\\\\n   \\hline\n     5082 \\\\\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\nUsing the distributive property:\n\\[\n77 \\times 66 = (70 + 7)(60 + 6) = 70 \\times 60 + 70 \\times 6 + 7 \\times 60 + 7 \\times 6\n\\]\nCalculating each term:\n- \\(70 \\times 60 = 4200\\)\n- \\(70 \\times 6 = 420\\)\n- \\(7 \\times 60 = 420\\)\n- \\(7 \\times 6 = 42\\)\n\nAdding these together:\n\\[\n4200 + 420 + 420 + 42 = 4200 + 840 + 42 = 5082\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 77 and 66 is \\(\\boxed{5082}\\).",
