using the standard algorithm once again but written out step by step.\n\nFirst, write 28  \n\u00d7 79  \n________\n\nMultiply 28 by 9:  \n8\u00d79=72, write 2, carryover 7.  \n2\u00d79=18 +7=25. So 252.\n\nThen multiply 28 by 7 (the tens place, so it's actually 70). So:\n\n28 \u00d77:  \n8\u00d77=56, write 6, carryover 5.  \n2\u00d77=14 +5=19. So that gives 196. But since it's the tens place, shift one position to the left, so 1960.\n\nNow add the two results:\n\n\u2003\u00a0252  \n+1960  \n______  \n2212.\n\nYes, exactly. So that's consistent. Therefore, I think 2212 is the correct product. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations here. All methods cross-verify. So I think that's the answer.\n\nBut just to be extra sure, maybe I can use another approach, like adding 28 seventy-nine times. But that's tedious. Alternatively, use calculator-like step-by-step.\n\nAlternatively, maybe approximate to check if 2212 makes sense. 28 is near 30, and 79 is near 80. 30\u00d780=2400. So 2212 is a bit less, which makes sense because 28 is 2 less than 30, and 79 is 1 less than 80. So the approximate decrease would be 2\u00d780 + 1\u00d730 - but overlapping, so maybe total decrease is around (30\u00d780) - (2\u00d780 +1\u00d730) + (2\u00d71) = 2400 -160 -30 +2= 2400 -190 +2= 2212. Wait, that's exactly the answer. Wait, that's interesting.\n\nLet me explain that again. If we approximate 28\u00d779 as (30 -2)\u00d7(80 -1). Then expanding, it's 30\u00d780 -30\u00d71 -2\u00d780 +2\u00d71. Which is 2400 -30 -160 +2 = 2400 -190 +2 = 2212. So that gives the exact answer because the approximation was actually an exact expansion. So that also confirms it. Therefore, all methods point to 2212. I think that's correct. I don't see any errors in my calculations. So I'll go with 2212 as the product of 28 and 79.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 28 and 79 is \\boxed{2212}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 28 and 79, we can use the distributive property and break down the numbers into smaller components:\n\n1. Break down 79 into 70 and 9:\n   \\[\n   28 \\times 79 = 28 \\times (70 + 9)\n   \\]\n2. Calculate \\(28 \\times 70\\):\n   \\[\n   28 \\times 70 = 28 \\times 7 \\times 10 = 196 \\times 10 = 1960\n   \\]\n3. Calculate \\(28 \\times 9\\):\n   \\[\n   28 \\times 9 = 252\n   \\]\n4. Add the results of the two products:\n   \\[\n   1960 + 252 = 2212\n   \\]\n\nTo verify, we can use another method by expanding both numbers:\n1. Break down both numbers into smaller components:\n   \\[\n   (20 + 8) \\times (70 + 9)\n   \\]\n2. Use the distributive property:\n   \\[\n   20 \\times 70 + 20 \\times 9 + 8 \\times 70 + 8 \\times 9\n   \\]\n3. Calculate each term:\n   \\[\n   20 \\times 70 = 1400\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   20 \\times 9 = 180\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   8 \\times 70 = 560\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   8 \\times 9 = 72\n   \\]\n4. Sum all the terms:\n   \\[\n   1400 + 180 + 560 + 72 = 2212\n   \\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 28 and 79 is \\(\\boxed{2212}\\).",
