mistake in any step? Let me check the 13*6 again. 13*6: 10+3, so 10*6=60, 3*6=18. 60+18=78. That's correct. Then 78*10 is moving the decimal one place, so 780. \n\nAlternatively, maybe breaking down 13 into 10 + 3, so (10 + 3)*60 = 10*60 + 3*60 = 600 + 180 = 780. \n\nAll methods lead to the same answer. I think that's solid. Maybe another way: 60 times 10 is 600, 60 times 3 is 180, so 600 plus 180 is 780. \n\nAlternatively, if I think of 13 as 12 +1, then 12*60 +1*60. 12*60 is 720, plus 60 is 780. Hmm, that also works. \n\nWait, let me verify that. 12*60: 10*60=600, 2*60=120, so 600+120=720. Then adding 1*60=60 gives 720+60=780. Yep, that works too. \n\nSo regardless of how I break it down, the result is the same. Therefore, I can be confident that the product of 13 and 60 is 780. \n\nI don't see any errors in my calculations, so I think that's the correct answer. Probably overkill with all the methods, but better safe than sorry!\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 13 and 60 is \\boxed{780}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 13 and 60, we can use multiple methods to ensure accuracy:\n\n1. **Breaking down the numbers**:\n   - Recognize that \\(60 = 6 \\times 10\\).\n   - Calculate \\(13 \\times 6\\):\n     \\[\n     13 \\times 6 = (10 \\times 6) + (3 \\times 6) = 60 + 18 = 78\n     \\]\n   - Multiply the result by 10:\n     \\[\n     78 \\times 10 = 780\n     \\]\n\n2. **Standard multiplication algorithm**:\n   - Multiply 13 by 0 (units place of 60) to get 0.\n   - Multiply 13 by 6 (tens place of 60, which is actually 60) and shift one position to the left:\n     \\[\n     13 \\times 6 = 78 \\quad \\text{(then shift left by one digit)} \\quad 780\n     \\]\n   - Add the results: \\(0 + 780 = 780\\).\n\n3. **Distributive property**:\n   - Break down 13 into 10 and 3:\n     \\[\n     13 \\times 60 = (10 \\times 60) + (3 \\times 60) = 600 + 180 = 780\n     \\]\n\n4. **Alternative breakdown**:\n   - Break down 13 into 12 and 1:\n     \\[\n     13 \\times 60 = (12 \\times 60) + (1 \\times 60) = 720 + 60 = 780\n     \\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 13 and 60 is \\(\\boxed{780}\\).",
