      "text": "2460 + 41 is 2501?\n\nWait, hold on, that seems low? Let me check again. 41 times 60: 40*60 is 2400, plus 1*60 is 60, so 2400 +60 is 2460. Then adding 41 gives 2460 +41=2501. Hmm, maybe that's correct. Alternatively, maybe I can do it another way to confirm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using the standard multiplication:\n\n   41\nx 61\n-------\nFirst multiply 41 by 1 (units place): 41*1=41.\n\nThen multiply 41 by 60 (since the 6 is in the tens place, so 6*10). So 41*60=2460.\n\nThen add them together: 41 + 2460 = 2501. Same result. Okay, so that seems consistent.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use another method like the lattice method or the difference of squares? Let me try the difference of squares to see if that works here. Wait, difference of squares is for (a + b)(a - b) = a\u00b2 - b\u00b2, but here we have 41 and 61. Not sure if that applies here. Alternatively, maybe express them as (50 - 9) and (50 +11), but that might complicate things. Let me see:\n\nAlternatively, (40 +1)(60 +1). Let's expand that:\n\n40*60 + 40*1 + 1*60 + 1*1.\n\n40*60 is 2400, 40*1 is 40, 1*60 is 60, and 1*1 is 1. Adding those together: 2400 +40 is 2440, plus 60 is 2500, plus 1 is 2501. Yep, same answer again. So that's three different methods all giving me 2501, so I think that must be correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use a calculator to confirm, but since I don't have one here, but let me try to do another check using estimation. 40 times 60 is 2400, and 41 times 61 should be a bit more than that. Since both numbers are 1 more than 40 and 60 respectively, the extra would be 40*1 + 60*1 +1*1 = 40 +60 +1=101. So adding that to 2400 gives 2501. Exactly! That's another way to see it. So all methods point to 2501. Therefore, I think the product of 41 and 61 is 2501. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations, so I'm confident that's the right answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 41 and 61 is \\boxed{2501}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 41 and 61, we can use the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n41 \\times 61 = 41 \\times (60 + 1) = (41 \\times 60) + (41 \\times 1)\n\\]\n\nFirst, calculate \\(41 \\times 60\\):\n\\[\n41 \\times 60 = 41 \\times 6 \\times 10 = 246 \\times 10 = 2460\n\\]\n\nNext, calculate \\(41 \\times 1\\):\n\\[\n41 \\times 1 = 41\n\\]\n\nAdd the results together:\n\\[\n2460 + 41 = 2501\n\\]\n\nWe can verify this using another method by expanding \\((40 + 1)(60 + 1)\\):\n\\[\n(40 + 1)(60 + 1) = 40 \\times 60 + 40 \\times 1 + 1 \\times 60 + 1 \\times 1\n\\]\n\\[\n= 2400 + 40 + 60 + 1 = 2400 + 101 = 2501\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 41 and 61 is \\(\\boxed{2501}\\).",
