e result again.\n\nWow, okay, so all methods are pointing to 7968. So I think that's the correct answer. Let me just confirm once with calculator steps.\n\nAlternatively, if I multiply 96 by 80, that's 7680, and 96 by 3 is 288, adding gives 7968. Yep.\n\nAlternatively, maybe do 83 times 100 which is 8300, subtract 4 times 83 (332) gives 7968. Yep.\n\nSo all the different methods I can think of are giving me the same answer, so I think that's solid. Therefore, the product of 96 and 83 is 7968.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 96 and 83 is \\boxed{7968}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 96 and 83, we can use the distributive property by breaking down the numbers into smaller parts:\n\n\\[\n(90 + 6)(80 + 3)\n\\]\n\nExpanding this using the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n90 \\times 80 + 90 \\times 3 + 6 \\times 80 + 6 \\times 3\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term step by step:\n\n1. \\(90 \\times 80 = 7200\\)\n2. \\(90 \\times 3 = 270\\)\n3. \\(6 \\times 80 = 480\\)\n4. \\(6 \\times 3 = 18\\)\n\nAdding all these results together:\n\n\\[\n7200 + 270 + 480 + 18\n\\]\n\nFirst, add \\(7200 + 270\\):\n\n\\[\n7200 + 270 = 7470\n\\]\n\nNext, add \\(7470 + 480\\):\n\n\\[\n7470 + 480 = 7950\n\\]\n\nFinally, add \\(7950 + 18\\):\n\n\\[\n7950 + 18 = 7968\n\\]\n\nTo verify, we can use another method by directly multiplying 96 by 83:\n\n- Multiply 96 by 3: \\(96 \\times 3 = 288\\)\n- Multiply 96 by 80 (shifted one position to the left): \\(96 \\times 80 = 7680\\)\n\nAdding these results:\n\n\\[\n288 + 7680 = 7968\n\\]\n\nAnother verification using the distributive property with 96 as \\(100 - 4\\):\n\n\\[\n(100 - 4) \\times 83 = 100 \\times 83 - 4 \\times 83\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term:\n\n1. \\(100 \\times 83 = 8300\\)\n2. \\(4 \\times 83 = 332\\)\n\nSubtracting these results:\n\n\\[\n8300 - 332 = 7968\n\\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 96 and 83 is \\(\\boxed{7968}\\).",
