      "text": "536 + 2010 = 2546? Wait, is that correct?\n\nHmm, let me check. Alternatively, maybe I made a mistake in the partial products. Let me verify each step again.\n\nFirst partial product (units place):\n\n8 \u00d7 67:\n\nCompute 8\u00d77 =56, write 6, carryover 5.\n\n8\u00d76 =48 +5=53. So that gives 536. That seems right.\n\nSecond partial product (tens place):\n\n3 (from 38) is in the tens place, so effectively 30. So 30 \u00d767.\n\nTo compute 3\u00d767 first, then add a zero. 3\u00d760=180, 3\u00d77=21. 180+21=201. So 201\u00d710=2010. That's correct.\n\nAdding 536 +2010. Let's add them:\n\n536 + 2000 = 2536, then +10 more is 2546. Hmm, okay, that's 2546.\n\nWait a second, but let me do it another way to confirm. Maybe using the distributive property: (60 +7)(30 +8).\n\nMultiply them out:\n\n60\u00d730 +60\u00d78 +7\u00d730 +7\u00d78.\n\nCompute each term:\n\n60\u00d730 = 1800,\n\n60\u00d78 = 480,\n\n7\u00d730=210,\n\n7\u00d78=56.\n\nAdding all together: 1800 +480 = 2280; 2280 +210 = 2490; 2490 +56 = 2546.\n\nOkay, same result. So that's good. Then perhaps 2546 is correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do 67\u00d740 which would be 2680, then subtract 67\u00d72=134, so 2680-134=2546. Let's see:\n\n67\u00d740 = 67\u00d74\u00d710 = 268\u00d710=2680. Correct.\n\nThen 67\u00d72=134. So subtract 134 from 2680: 2680 - 100 =2580, then subtract 34 more: 2580 -34=2546. Yep, same answer.\n\nHmm, so that seems correct. So I think the answer is 2546. Wait but let me check with another method just to be thorough.\n\nAlternatively, using the standard algorithm again, step by step:\n\n\u2003\u00a067  .\n\u00d7\u00a038  .\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500.\n\u2003536  (this is 67\u00d78) .\n+2010 (this is 67\u00d730) .\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500.\n\u00a02546.\n\nYes, that's the same as before. So I think that's correct. Therefore, the product of 67 and 38 is 2546.\n\nWait a second, but let me confirm with a calculator. Since I can't use a calculator, but I can do another check via addition. 67 added 38 times. But that's tedious. Alternatively, use another multiplication method.\n\nWait, perhaps I can use the difference of squares or something? Let me see. Hmm, 67 and 38 aren't close to the same number, so maybe not helpful. Alternatively, break down 38 into 40 - 2, which is what I did before, which worked.\n\nAlternatively, 67\u00d738 = (70 - 3)\u00d738 = 70\u00d738 -3\u00d738. Let's compute:\n\n70\u00d738: 70\u00d730=2100, 70\u00d78=560, so total 2100 +560=2660.\n\nThen subtract 3\u00d738=114. So 2660 -114=2546. Yep, same result. So that's consistent.\n\nAlright, so all different methods are giving me 2546, so I think that's the correct answer. Therefore, the product of 67 and 38 is 2546.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 67 and 38 is \\boxed{2546}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 67 and 38, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm:\n\n1. Write the numbers vertically aligned by place value:\n   ```\n    67\n  \u00d7 38\n  ----\n   ```\n\n2. Multiply 67 by the units digit (8) of 38:\n   - 8 \u00d7 7 = 56. Write down 6 and carry over 5.\n   - 8 \u00d7 6 = 48. Add the carried over 5 to get 53.\n   - The first partial product is 536.\n\n3. Multiply 67 by the tens digit (3) of 38 (which represents 30), and shift the result one place to the left:\n   - 3 \u00d7 7 = 21. Write down 1 and carry over 2.\n   - 3 \u00d7 6 = 18. Add the carried over 2 to get 20.\n   - The second partial product is 2010 (since it is shifted one place left).\n\n4. Add the partial products:\n   ```\n    536\n  +2010\n  ----\n   2546\n   ```\n\nWe can verify this result using different methods:\n- **Distributive Property**:\n  \\[\n  (60 + 7)(30 + 8) = 60\u00d730 + 60\u00d78 + 7\u00d730 + 7\u00d78 = 1800 + 480 + 210 + 56 = 2546\n  \\]\n\n- **Breaking Down**:\n  \\[\n  67 \u00d7 38 = 67 \u00d7 (40 - 2) = 67\u00d740 - 67\u00d72 = 2680 - 134 = 2546\n  \\]\n\n- **Another Breakdown**:\n  \\[\n  67 \u00d7 38 = (70 - 3) \u00d7 38 = 70\u00d738 - 3\u00d738 = 2660 - 114 = 2546\n  \\]\n\nAll methods confirm the result is consistent. Therefore, the product of 67 and 38 is \\boxed{2546}.",
