I can be confident. So the product of 67 and 58 is 3886.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 67 and 58 is \\boxed{3886}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 67 and 58, we can use the distributive property by breaking down the numbers into smaller parts:\n\n1. Break down 67 into 60 and 7, and 58 into 50 and 8:\n   \\[\n   (60 + 7) \\times (50 + 8)\n   \\]\n\n2. Apply the distributive property:\n   \\[\n   60 \\times 50 + 60 \\times 8 + 7 \\times 50 + 7 \\times 8\n   \\]\n\n3. Calculate each term:\n   \\[\n   60 \\times 50 = 3000\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   60 \\times 8 = 480\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   7 \\times 50 = 350\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   7 \\times 8 = 56\n   \\]\n\n4. Sum the results of each term:\n   \\[\n   3000 + 480 + 350 + 56\n   \\]\n\n5. Perform the addition step-by-step:\n   \\[\n   3000 + 480 = 3480\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   3480 + 350 = 3830\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   3830 + 56 = 3886\n   \\]\n\nTo verify, we can use the standard algorithm:\n- Multiply 67 by 8:\n  \\[\n  67 \\times 8 = 536\n  \\]\n- Multiply 67 by 50:\n  \\[\n  67 \\times 50 = 3350\n  \\]\n- Add the results:\n  \\[\n  536 + 3350 = 3886\n  \\]\n\nAnother verification using the difference of squares approach:\n- Rewrite 67 as \\(70 - 3\\) and 58 as \\(60 - 2\\):\n  \\[\n  (70 - 3)(60 - 2) = 70 \\times 60 - 70 \\times 2 - 3 \\times 60 + 3 \\times 2\n  \\]\n- Calculate each term:\n  \\[\n  70 \\times 60 = 4200\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  70 \\times 2 = 140\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  3 \\times 60 = 180\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  3 \\times 2 = 6\n  \\]\n- Sum the results:\n  \\[\n  4200 - 140 - 180 + 6 = 3886\n  \\]\n\nAll methods confirm the product is \\(\\boxed{3886}\\).",
