      "text": "2010 + 67 equals... 2010 plus 60 is 2070, and then plus 7 more is 2077. Wait, is that right?\n\nWait a second, let me check my math again because sometimes when I do mental math I might make a mistake. Let me verify the first part again. 67*30. If I break it down as (60 + 7)*30, that would be 60*30 + 7*30. 60*30 is 1800, and 7*30 is 210. Adding those together gives 1800 + 210 = 2010. Okay, that part checks out. Then adding 67 gives 2010 + 67 = 2077. Hmm, so that seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do the multiplication the standard way, writing it out vertically. Let me try that method to cross-verify.\n\nSo, writing 67  \ntimes 31  \n________\n\nStarting with the units place: 1 times 67 is 67. Then moving to the tens place: 3 (which is actually 30) times 67. So 3 times 7 is 21, write down 1 and carry over 2. Then 3 times 6 is 18, plus the carried 2 is 20. So that gives 201, but since it's the tens place, it's shifted one position to the left, making it 2010. Then adding the two results: 67 + 2010 = 2077. Same result as before. Okay, so that confirms it.\n\nWait, but just to make sure, maybe another method? Like using the difference of squares or something? Let me see. Hmm, 67 and 31... Not sure if that applies here. Alternatively, maybe breaking both numbers into tens and units?\n\nLet me try that. 67 is 60 +7, and 31 is 30 +1. Then, using the distributive property in full: (60+7)*(30+1). Multiply each term:\n\n60*30 + 60*1 +7*30 +7*1. Let's compute each term:\n\n60*30 = 1800,\n\n60*1 = 60,\n\n7*30 = 210,\n\n7*1 =7.\n\nNow add them all up: 1800 + 60 is 1860, plus 210 is 2070, plus 7 is 2077. Yep, same answer again. Okay, so all three methods give me 2077, so that must be the correct product.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use a calculator? But since I don't have one here, but since all my manual calculations are consistent, I think 2077 is the right answer. I don't see any errors in my steps. So the product of 67 and 31 is 2077. \n\nWait, hold on a second thought. Wait, 67 times 30 is 2010, and adding another 67 gives 2077. Let me try adding them again: 2010 + 60 is 2070, plus 7 is 2077. Yes. Alternatively, 2010 +67: 2010 + 60 is 2070, plus 7 is 2077. Correct.\n\nHmm, maybe I can also check by estimating. 67 is close to 70, and 31 is close to 30. 70*30 is 2100. The actual answer should be a bit less since we rounded 67 up to 70 and 31 down to 30. 2077 is indeed a bit less than 2100, so that seems reasonable. So the estimation also supports that 2077 is correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe do the multiplication in reverse: 31 times 67. Since multiplication is commutative, it should be the same. Let me try that. 31*67. Let's do 30*67 +1*67. 30*67: 3*67 is 201, so times 10 is 2010. Plus 67 is again 2077. Yep, same result.\n\nAlright, I think that's thorough enough. All methods converge to 2077, so I'm confident that's the right answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 67 and 31 is \\boxed{2077}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 67 and 31, we can use the distributive property by breaking down the numbers into smaller parts:\n\n1. Break down 31 into 30 and 1:\n   \\[\n   67 \\times 31 = 67 \\times (30 + 1)\n   \\]\n2. Apply the distributive property:\n   \\[\n   67 \\times 30 + 67 \\times 1\n   \\]\n3. Calculate each part separately:\n   - For \\(67 \\times 30\\):\n     \\[\n     67 \\times 30 = 67 \\times 3 \\times 10 = 201 \\times 10 = 2010\n     \\]\n   - For \\(67 \\times 1\\):\n     \\[\n     67 \\times 1 = 67\n     \\]\n4. Add the results of the two parts:\n   \\[\n   2010 + 67 = 2077\n   \\]\n\nWe can verify this by using another method, such as the standard vertical multiplication:\n- Multiply 67 by 1 (units place): \\(67 \\times 1 = 67\\)\n- Multiply 67 by 30 (tens place): \\(67 \\times 30 = 2010\\)\n- Add the results: \\(67 + 2010 = 2077\\)\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 67 and 31 is \\(\\boxed{2077}\\).",
