o 13. Yep, 12*13 is 156. \n\nAlternatively, 156 divided by 13. Let me see how many times 13 goes into 156. 13*10 is 130, subtract that from 156, you get 26. Then 13*2 is 26. So total is 10+2=12. So that checks out too. \n\nTherefore, I think the correct answer is 156. I can't see any mistakes in my reasoning here. All the different methods cross-verify. So I'm confident the product of 12 and 13 is 156.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 12 and 13 is \\boxed{156}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 12 and 13, we can use the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n12 \\times 13 = 12 \\times (10 + 3) = (12 \\times 10) + (12 \\times 3)\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term separately:\n- \\(12 \\times 10 = 120\\)\n- \\(12 \\times 3 = 36\\)\n\nAdding the results together:\n\\[\n120 + 36 = 156\n\\]\n\nWe can verify this using another method by expanding the product as a binomial:\n\\[\n(10 + 2)(10 + 3) = 10 \\times 10 + 10 \\times 3 + 2 \\times 10 + 2 \\times 3\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term separately:\n- \\(10 \\times 10 = 100\\)\n- \\(10 \\times 3 = 30\\)\n- \\(2 \\times 10 = 20\\)\n- \\(2 \\times 3 = 6\\)\n\nAdding the results together:\n\\[\n100 + 30 + 20 + 6 = 156\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 12 and 13 is \\(\\boxed{156}\\).",
