 and estimation. All lead to 1155. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations. So I think the product of 15 and 77 is 1155.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 15 and 77 is calculated as follows:\n\n1. **Using the distributive property**:\n   \\[\n   15 \\times 77 = 15 \\times (70 + 7) = (15 \\times 70) + (15 \\times 7)\n   \\]\n   - Calculate \\(15 \\times 70\\):\n     \\[\n     15 \\times 70 = 15 \\times 7 \\times 10 = 105 \\times 10 = 1050\n     \\]\n   - Calculate \\(15 \\times 7\\):\n     \\[\n     15 \\times 7 = 105\n     \\]\n   - Add the results:\n     \\[\n     1050 + 105 = 1155\n     \\]\n\n2. **Verification via alternative methods**:\n   - **Commutative property**:\n     \\[\n     77 \\times 15 = (70 + 7) \\times 15 = 70 \\times 15 + 7 \\times 15 = 1050 + 105 = 1155\n     \\]\n   - **Prime factorization**:\n     \\[\n     15 = 3 \\times 5, \\quad 77 = 7 \\times 11\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     15 \\times 77 = (3 \\times 5) \\times (7 \\times 11) = 3 \\times 5 \\times 7 \\times 11 = 105 \\times 11 = 1155\n     \\]\n   - **Estimation check**:\n     \\[\n     15 \\times 80 = 1200 \\quad \\text{(subtract excess for 3 less units)} \\quad 1200 - (15 \\times 3) = 1200 - 45 = 1155\n     \\]\n\n**Final Answer**:  \nThe product of 15 and 77 is \\(\\boxed{1155}\\).",
