acing the sum of the digits between them. However, if the sum is 10 or more, carry over the extra to the next higher place.\n   - Here, the number is 91 (where \\( A = 9 \\) and \\( B = 1 \\)).\n   - Adding the digits: \\( 9 + 1 = 10 \\).\n   - Since the sum is 10, we carry over 1 to the first digit (9 becomes \\( 9 + 1 = 10 \\)).\n   - Placing the remaining digit (0) in the middle and keeping the last digit (1) gives us \\( 10 \\) followed by \\( 0 \\) and \\( 1 \\), resulting in 1001.\n\n2. **Standard multiplication**:\n   - Multiply 91 by 1 (units place of 11): \\( 91 \\times 1 = 91 \\).\n   - Multiply 91 by 10 (tens place of 11): \\( 91 \\times 10 = 910 \\).\n   - Add the results: \\( 91 + 910 = 1001 \\).\n\n3. **Distributive property**:\n   - \\( 91 \\times 11 = 91 \\times (10 + 1) = 91 \\times 10 + 91 \\times 1 = 910 + 91 = 1001 \\).\n\n4. **Verification**:\n   - Breaking down 91 into 90 and 1: \\( (90 \\times 11) + (1 \\times 11) = 990 + 11 = 1001 \\).\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 91 and 11 is \\(\\boxed{1001}\\).",
