left, 4 on bottom right.\n\nBottom-left cell:5*6=30, so 3 on top left, 0 on bottom right.\n\nBottom-right cell:8*6=48, so 4 on top left, 8 on bottom right.\n\nNow, the diagonals are from top-right to bottom-left. The rightmost diagonal is just the 8 in the bottom-right cell's bottom right. That's the units place.\n\nNext diagonal to the left: 4 (from top-right cell's bottom right) +0 (from bottom-left cell's bottom right) +4 (from bottom-right cell's top left?) Wait, maybe I need to sum along the diagonals properly.\n\nActually, the diagonals are vertical lines from top-right to bottom-left. Let me structure it:\n\nThe grid with cells divided by diagonals:\n\nFirst cell (top-left): 1 / 5\n\nSecond cell (top-right): 2 /4\n\nThird cell (bottom-left):3 /0\n\nFourth cell (bottom-right):4 /8\n\nSo the diagonals:\n\nThe rightmost diagonal is the bottom right part of the bottom-right cell: 8.\n\nThe next diagonal is the bottom right of the top-right cell (4) and the bottom right of the bottom-left cell (0) plus the top left of the bottom-right cell (4). Wait, perhaps I need to visualize this better.\n\nAlternatively, the diagonals are as follows:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nFirst diagonal (units place): the bottom-right part of the bottom-right cell: 8.\n\nSecond diagonal (tens place): the bottom-right part of the top-right cell (4) plus the bottom-right part of the bottom-left cell (0) plus the top-left part of the bottom-right cell (4). Wait, no, perhaps it's the diagonal that goes through the bottom-right part of the top-right cell and the top-left part of the bottom-left cell?\n\nThis is getting confusing. Maybe I should look up lattice method steps again, but since I might not have time, let me try another way.\n\nAlternatively, maybe it's better to stick with the previous two methods that gave me 2088. Since both the distributive property and the standard algorithm give the same answer, I can be more confident. \n\nAlternatively, I can do 58*36 as (60 - 2)*36. Let's see:\n\n60*36 = 2160\n\n2*36 =72\n\nSo subtracting: 2160 -72 = 2088. Yep, that also works. \n\nSo that confirms it again. \n\nAnother way: 36*58 = (30 +6)(50 +8). Using FOIL:\n\nFirst: 30*50=1500\n\nOuter: 30*8=240\n\nInner:6*50=300\n\nLast:6*8=48\n\nAdding all those: 1500 +240 is 1740, plus 300 is 2040, plus 48 is 2088. Yep, same result.\n\nOkay, so all methods lead to 2088. Therefore, the product of 58 and 36 is 2088. I think that's solid. I can't see any mistakes in my calculations, and multiple methods confirm it. So I'm confident that's the right answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 58 and 36 is \\boxed{2088}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 58 and 36, we can use the distributive property and the standard algorithm for multiplication.\n\nFirst, breaking down the numbers using the distributive property:\n\\[\n58 \\times 36 = 58 \\times (30 + 6)\n\\]\nCalculating each part separately:\n\\[\n58 \\times 30 = 58 \\times 3 \\times 10 = 174 \\times 10 = 1740\n\\]\n\\[\n58 \\times 6 = (50 \\times 6) + (8 \\times 6) = 300 + 48 = 348\n\\]\nAdding the results together:\n\\[\n1740 + 348 = 2088\n\\]\n\nUsing the standard algorithm:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   \\quad 58 \\\\\n\\times 36 \\\\\n\\hline\n   348 \\quad (\\text{58} \\times 6) \\\\\n+1740 \\quad (\\text{58} \\times 30) \\\\\n\\hline\n  2088 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nAnother verification using the difference of squares approach:\n\\[\n58 \\times 36 = (60 - 2) \\times 36 = 60 \\times 36 - 2 \\times 36 = 2160 - 72 = 2088\n\\]\n\nAll methods confirm the result. Therefore, the product of 58 and 36 is \\(\\boxed{2088}\\).",
