    {
      "index": 0,
      "text": "1800 plus 60 is 1860. Wait, is that right?\n\nLet me check another way just to be sure. Maybe I can do the standard multiplication algorithm. Let's write it down vertically:\n\n   60\nx 31\n------\n   \nStarting with the 1 in the ones place: 1 times 60 is 60. Then, moving to the tens place, which is 3, but since it's the tens place, it's actually 30. So 30 times 60. Hmm, 30 times 60. Well, 3 times 6 is 18, and then add two zeros (one from 30 and one from 60), so that's 1800. Then add the two results: 60 + 1800. Yep, that's 1860 again. Okay, so that checks out.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can think of 31 as 30 + 1 and multiply by 60. Wait, that's what I did first. Either way, same result. Hmm, seems consistent.\n\nAnother way: Maybe using the commutative property. 31 times 60. Well, 31 times 6 is 186, and then add a zero at the end, making it 1860. Oh yeah, that works too. Let me verify that. 31*6: 30*6 is 180, plus 1*6 is 6, so 186. Then adding a zero gives 1860. Yep, that's another method. So all methods are pointing towards 1860. \n\nI guess that's the answer then. Let me just make sure I didn't make any arithmetic errors. 60*30=1800, correct. 60*1=60. 1800+60 is 1860. Yep. The other way, 31*60, same thing. 31*6=186, plus a zero is 1860. So all the different methods give me the same result, so I think that's right. I don't see any mistakes here. So the product of 60 and 31 is 1860.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 60 and 31 is \\boxed{1860}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 60 and 31, we can use the distributive property by breaking down 31 into 30 and 1:\n\n\\[\n60 \\times 31 = 60 \\times (30 + 1)\n\\]\n\nFirst, calculate \\(60 \\times 30\\):\n\n\\[\n60 \\times 30 = 1800\n\\]\n\nNext, calculate \\(60 \\times 1\\):\n\n\\[\n60 \\times 1 = 60\n\\]\n\nAdd the two results together:\n\n\\[\n1800 + 60 = 1860\n\\]\n\nWe can verify this using another method by considering the commutative property:\n\n\\[\n31 \\times 60 = (31 \\times 6) \\times 10\n\\]\n\nCalculate \\(31 \\times 6\\):\n\n\\[\n31 \\times 6 = 186\n\\]\n\nThen multiply by 10:\n\n\\[\n186 \\times 10 = 1860\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 60 and 31 is \\(\\boxed{1860}\\).",
