g those two gives 143. \n\nI think that's solid. So the product of 13 and 11 is 143. I can't see any mistake in my reasoning here. All different methods lead to the same answer. The multiplication trick for 11 works perfectly because it's a two-digit number. So I'm confident the answer is 143.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 13 and 11 is calculated as follows:\n\n**Method 1: Standard Multiplication**  \n```\n   13  \n\u00d7  11  \n-------\n    13   (13 \u00d7 1)  \n  130    (13 \u00d7 10, shifted left)  \n-------\n  143  \n```\n\n**Method 2: 11's Multiplication Trick**  \nFor a two-digit number \\( ab \\), multiplying by 11 gives \\( a(a+b)b \\). If \\( a + b \\geq 10 \\), carry over.  \nHere, \\( 13 \\times 11 = 1 (1+3) 3 = 143 \\).\n\n**Method 3: Distributive Property**  \n\\( 13 \\times 11 = 13 \\times (10 + 1) = 130 + 13 = 143 \\).\n\n**Final Answer:**  \nThe product of 13 and 11 is \\(\\boxed{143}\\).",
