act 1*55 =55. So 4400 -55 = 4345. Same result again. Okay, so that's a third method giving the same answer. So that seems solid.\n\nAlternatively, just do straight multiplication:\n\n   79\n\nx55\n\n--------\n\nFirst step: 79 x5 = 395\n\nSecond step: 79 x5 (but shifted one position to the left because it's the tens place, so actually 79 x50)\n\nSo 79 x50 = 3950\n\nAdding 395 + 3950 gives 4345. So that's consistent.\n\nHmm, seems like all methods are pointing to 4345. So maybe that's the correct answer. Let me just confirm with a calculator, but since I don't have one, I'll check with another approach.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use the difference of squares? Wait, 79 and 55. Not sure if that applies here. Alternatively, think of 79*55 as (70+9)*(50+5). Let's expand that.\n\nMultiply (70 +9)(50 +5) using FOIL:\n\nFirst: 70*50 = 3500\n\nOuter:70*5= 350\n\nInner:9*50=450\n\nLast:9*5=45\n\nNow add all those together: 3500 + 350 is 3850, plus 450 is 4300, plus 45 is 4345. Yep, that's the same as before. So that's four different methods all giving me 4345. I think that must be the correct answer then.\n\nWait, but just to be thorough, maybe I can do the multiplication step by step again.\n\n79 multiplied by 55:\n\nStart with 79 times 5:\n\n9 times 5 is 45, write down 5 carryover 4.\n\n7 times 5 is 35, plus the 4 is 39. So that gives 395.\n\nThen, 79 times 50:\n\nSame as 79 times 5 times 10. So 395 times 10 is 3950.\n\nAdding 395 and 3950:\n\n3950 + 395:\n\n3950 + 300 is 4250, plus 95 is 4345. Yep, same result.\n\nAlternatively, adding 3950 + 395: 3950 + 400 would be 4350, but since it's only 395, subtract 5: 4350 -5 =4345. Correct.\n\nAlright, so all the methods confirm that. So I think the product of 79 and 55 is indeed 4345. I don't see any mistakes in my calculations, so I'm confident this is right.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 79 and 55 is \\boxed{4345}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 79 and 55, we can use the distributive property and break down the multiplication into smaller parts:\n\n1. **Break down 55 into 50 and 5:**\n   \\[\n   79 \\times 55 = 79 \\times (50 + 5)\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate \\(79 \\times 50\\):**\n   \\[\n   79 \\times 50 = 79 \\times 5 \\times 10 = 395 \\times 10 = 3950\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate \\(79 \\times 5\\):**\n   \\[\n   79 \\times 5 = 395\n   \\]\n\n4. **Add the results of the two parts:**\n   \\[\n   3950 + 395 = 4345\n   \\]\n\nTo verify, we can use another method by expressing 79 as \\(80 - 1\\):\n   \\[\n   (80 - 1) \\times 55 = 80 \\times 55 - 1 \\times 55\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   80 \\times 55 = 4400 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 1 \\times 55 = 55\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   4400 - 55 = 4345\n   \\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 79 and 55 is \\(\\boxed{4345}\\).",
