      "text": "474 + 4740 equals 5214. Wait, let me check that addition again. 474 + 4740: 400 + 4700 is 5100, then 74 + 40 is 114, so 5100 + 114 is 5214. Okay, that seems right.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the distributive property. Let me see. 79 times 66 can be written as (70 + 9)(60 + 6). Expanding this, it would be 70*60 + 70*6 + 9*60 + 9*6. Let me compute each term:\n\n70*60 is 4200.\n\n70*6 is 420.\n\n9*60 is 540.\n\n9*6 is 54.\n\nNow add them all up: 4200 + 420 is 4620. Then 4620 +540 is 5160. Then 5160 +54 is 5214. Okay, that matches the previous result. So that's good.\n\nAnother way: Maybe using the difference of squares or some other trick? Hmm, not sure if that applies here. Let me see. Wait, 79 and 66 don't seem to be numbers around a common base that's easy to work with. Alternatively, maybe 79 is 80 -1 and 66 is 60 +6. Let me try that.\n\nSo, (80 -1)(60 +6). Maybe expanding that would work as well. Let's see:\n\nFirst, 80*(60+6) = 80*60 +80*6 = 4800 +480 = 5280.\n\nThen subtract 1*(60+6) which is 66. So 5280 -66 = 5214. Oh, that works too! That's a quicker method. So that confirms the same answer.\n\nHmm, so all methods are giving me 5214. I think that's the correct product. Let me just do a quick check with estimation to see if it makes sense. 80 times 60 is 4800, and 80 times 66 would be 5280, so 79 is one less than 80, so subtract 66 from 5280 gives 5214. Yeah, that makes sense. Alternatively, 79*66 is roughly 80*65=5200, so 5214 is close to that. So that seems reasonable.\n\nI think I can be confident that the answer is 5214. All different methods lead to the same result, so that's good. Maybe I made a mistake in one of the steps, but since they all agree, I think it's correct. Let me just do the standard multiplication again step by step to be thorough.\n\nMultiplying 79 by 66:\n\nFirst line: 79*6 = 474. Second line: 79*60 = 4740. Adding them gives 474 + 4740 = 5214. Yep, same as before.\n\nAlright, so I think that's the answer. 5214.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 79 and 66 is \\boxed{5214}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 79 and 66, we can use multiple methods to ensure accuracy.\n\n**Method 1: Standard Algorithm**\n1. Multiply 79 by 6 (the ones place of 66):\n   \\[\n   79 \\times 6 = 474\n   \\]\n2. Multiply 79 by 60 (the tens place of 66, represented as 60):\n   \\[\n   79 \\times 60 = 4740\n   \\]\n3. Add the partial products:\n   \\[\n   474 + 4740 = 5214\n   \\]\n\n**Method 2: Distributive Property**\n1. Expand the numbers:\n   \\[\n   (70 + 9)(60 + 6)\n   \\]\n2. Calculate each term:\n   \\[\n   70 \\times 60 = 4200\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   70 \\times 6 = 420\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   9 \\times 60 = 540\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   9 \\times 6 = 54\n   \\]\n3. Sum the results:\n   \\[\n   4200 + 420 + 540 + 54 = 5214\n   \\]\n\n**Method 3: Simplifying with Adjustments**\n1. Rewrite 79 as \\(80 - 1\\) and expand:\n   \\[\n   (80 - 1)(60 + 6)\n   \\]\n2. Calculate each part:\n   \\[\n   80 \\times (60 + 6) = 80 \\times 66 = 5280\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   1 \\times (60 + 6) = 66\n   \\]\n3. Subtract the second result from the first:\n   \\[\n   5280 - 66 = 5214\n   \\]\n\nAll methods confirm that the product of 79 and 66 is \\(\\boxed{5214}\\).",
