\times (10 \\times 44) = 8 \\times 440 = 3520\n\\]\n\nNext, compute \\(1 \\times 44\\):\n\n\\[\n1 \\times 44 = 44\n\\]\n\nAdding the results together:\n\n\\[\n3520 + 44 = 3564\n\\]\n\nTo verify, we can use another method by breaking down the multiplication vertically:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   81 \\\\\n\\times 44 \\\\\n\\hline\n   324 \\quad (\\text{4 times 81}) \\\\\n+3240 \\quad (\\text{40 times 81, shifted one position left}) \\\\\n\\hline\n  3564 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nAnother verification using the fact that \\(44 = 4 \\times 11\\):\n\n\\[\n81 \\times 44 = 81 \\times (4 \\times 11) = (81 \\times 4) \\times 11 = 324 \\times 11\n\\]\n\nCalculating \\(324 \\times 11\\):\n\n\\[\n324 \\times 11 = 324 \\times 10 + 324 \\times 1 = 3240 + 324 = 3564\n\\]\n\nAll methods confirm the result is consistent. Therefore, the product of 81 and 44 is \\(\\boxed{3564}\\).",
